/* Validated question bank imported from the HESI Exit simulator project file claude/hesi_sim_bank.json (177 items, 5 cases; each item carries its course-file source and page). */
window.BANK={"items":[{"id":"T01","ver":2,"subj":"Fundamentals","topic":"Assigning tasks to the UAP","cn":"Coordinated Care","cn2":"","fmt":"mc","diff":"I","ask":"Scope","comp":"scope","stem":"The LVN is working with a UAP on a medical unit. Which task is most appropriate for the LVN to assign to the UAP?","opts":["Feed breakfast to a client admitted yesterday with a stroke who coughs when drinking water","Walk a client in the hall who had surgery 2 days ago and walked twice yesterday","Take vital signs on a client admitted 30 minutes ago who is reporting chest pressure","Check capillary refill and toe sensation every hour on a client with a new leg cast"],"key":1,"rat":"The instructor's scope slide gives the UAP ambulation, positioning, ADLs, and routine, stable vital signs, and allows feeding only when there is no aspiration risk. Neurovascular checks are listed in the PN column. A client with a predictable activity pattern is the safest assignment.","why":{"0":"Coughing with thin liquids signals aspiration risk, and UAP feeding excludes clients at risk.","2":"Only routine, stable vital signs go to the UAP; a new admission with chest pressure needs nursing judgment.","3":"Neurovascular checks are a PN-level assessment on the slide."},"traps":{"0":"scope","2":"scope","3":"scope"},"cues":"Stable, predictable activity versus new, changing, or assessment-based tasks.","aid":"UAP gets the routine and the predictable; the nurse keeps anything new, unstable, or needing judgment.","principle":"Assign by stability and predictability, not by how simple the task looks.","takeaway":"Vital signs and feeding look like UAP tasks, but the client's condition decides. Read the client, then the task.","safer":{"s":"Choose the one task that fits UAP scope.","a":"Aspiration signs, a new admission with chest pressure, a new cast, and a stable client with a known activity pattern.","f":"Delegation and scope: routine, stable, predictable tasks that need no nursing judgment.","e":"Feeding with aspiration risk, vital signs on an unstable new admission, and neurovascular checks all need the nurse.","r":"Ambulation of a stable client wins over vital signs because the chest-pressure client is not routine or stable."},"compare":{"a":1,"b":2,"aWhy":"Ambulation is a listed UAP task, and this client has already walked without problems.","bWhy":"Taking vital signs is also a listed UAP task, so it looks equally safe.","decider":"The slide limits the UAP to routine, stable vital signs. A client admitted 30 minutes ago with chest pressure is neither.","flip":"If the chest-pressure client were stable on day 3 with routine vital-sign checks, assigning the vitals would fit UAP scope."},"src":"Fundamentals HESI review 3.pdf","pg":"p59","sameFormExclude":["XE17"],"cog":"analysis"},{"id":"T02","subj":"Maternal-Newborn","topic":"Magnesium sulfate toxicity","cn":"Pharmacological Therapies","cn2":"Reduction of Risk Potential","fmt":"mc","diff":"A","sameFormExclude":["CASE2"],"stem":"A 26-year-old primigravida at 35 weeks with preeclampsia is receiving a continuous IV infusion of magnesium sulfate. At the hourly check: patellar reflex absent, respirations 14/min, BP 148/96, urine output 90 mL over the past 4 hours. She says she feels \"hot all over.\" What is the nurse's first action?","opts":["Document the findings and recheck the patellar reflex and respirations in 1 hour.","Withhold further magnesium sulfate and notify the provider immediately.","Give calcium gluconate IV push from the supply kept at the bedside.","Increase the maintenance IV fluid rate to bring the urine output back up."],"key":1,"rat":"If the patellar reflex is absent, further doses are withheld until it returns. Urine output under 100 mL in 4 hours is reported, and \"feeling hot all over\" is an early toxicity sign. Calcium gluconate is the antidote kept available but requires an order, and IV push medication is outside the PN column.","why":{"0":"Delays action on two toxicity cues.","2":"Needs an order, and IV push is outside PN scope on the instructor's slide.","3":"No source supports raising fluids; it treats the number, not the cause."},"cues":"Absent reflex + low 4-hour output + \"hot all over.\"","aid":"The 3 R's before every magnesium dose: Reflexes, Respirations, Renal output.","src":"Maternal_Health_Linear_Chapter_Sources_Updated.pdf","pg":"pp. 331–381 (Ch. 39); Table 27.3 pp. 91–131; Fundamentals p59","ask":"Intervene","comp":"med","traps":{"0":"delay","2":"scope","3":"otherdx"},"safer":{"s":"Choose the first action during a magnesium infusion.","a":"Absent patellar reflex, urine output 90 mL in 4 hours, feeling hot all over; RR 14.","f":"Medication safety: magnesium toxicity checks before continuing.","e":"Documenting delays action, calcium IV push needs an order and is outside PN scope, extra fluids treat a number.","r":"Withholding and notifying beats giving the antidote because the nurse's first safe step is stopping the drug and escalating."},"principle":"When toxicity cues appear, stop the drug before anything else.","takeaway":"The antidote at the bedside is not the first move; stopping the infusion and calling is.","compare":{"a":1,"b":2,"aWhy":"It stops further magnesium and brings in the provider.","bWhy":"Calcium gluconate is the antidote and is kept at the bedside for this reason.","decider":"The antidote requires an order, and IV push is outside the PN column; withholding is within the nurse's role now.","flip":"With a provider order and an RN giving it, calcium gluconate would follow promptly."},"med":{"why":"Seizure prevention in preeclampsia.","check":"Patellar reflexes, respirations, urine output before continuing.","give":"Continuous IV infusion by pump as prescribed.","watch":"Absent reflexes, falling respirations, output under 100 mL in 4 hours, feeling hot.","teach":"Report flushing, heaviness, or breathing changes."},"xv":2},{"id":"T03","subj":"Hematology","topic":"Thrombocytopenia precautions","cn":"Reduction of Risk Potential","cn2":"Safety and Infection Prevention and Control","fmt":"sata","diff":"I","stem":"A 58-year-old receiving chemotherapy has a platelet count of 16,000/mm³. Which instructions should the nurse reinforce? Select all that apply.","opts":["Use a soft toothbrush.","Blow your nose gently.","Take ibuprofen for muscle aches.","Use a stool softener and high-fiber foods to prevent constipation.","Check your urine, stool, and any vomit for blood.","Resume contact sports to keep your muscles strong."],"key":[0,1,3,4],"rat":"Teaching includes a soft toothbrush, blowing the nose gently, stool softeners with a high-fiber diet, checking for blood, and avoiding trauma. Significant bleeding occurs below 20,000/mm³.","why":{"2":"Box 47.4 lists ibuprofen among drugs with thrombocytopenic effects.","5":"Teaching stresses avoiding trauma."},"cues":"Platelets 16,000.","aid":"Platelets are the road-patch crew: when the crew is short, drive gently and watch for leaks.","src":"Blood_and_Lymph_Complete_Linear_Source_Packet.pdf","pg":"pp47–49","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Teach","comp":"teach","traps":{"2":"unsafe","5":"unsafe"},"safer":{"s":"Select every bleeding-precaution instruction.","a":"Platelets 16,000/mm³, chemotherapy.","f":"Teaching: bleeding precautions in thrombocytopenia.","e":"Ibuprofen has thrombocytopenic effects; contact sports cause trauma.","r":"Each remaining instruction prevents trauma or detects bleeding."},"principle":"In select-all items, judge each option as its own true-or-false decision.","takeaway":"Low platelets: prevent trauma, avoid platelet-affecting drugs, and watch for blood.","xv":2},{"id":"T04","subj":"Pain & Opioids","topic":"Naloxone IM calculation","cn":"Pharmacological Therapies","cn2":"","fmt":"num","diff":"I","stem":"A 62-year-old who took extra doses of his home oxycodone is difficult to arouse, with respirations of 6/min. The provider orders naloxone 0.8 mg IM now. Available: naloxone 0.4 mg/mL. How many mL will the nurse give? Round to the nearest tenth.","key":2.0,"unit":"mL","tol":0,"rat":"0.8 mg ÷ 0.4 mg/mL = 2 mL. The order is within the adult IM overdose range of 0.4–2 mg. 2 mL fits the IM limit (3 mL per site; 2 mL for the deltoid). Afterward, vital signs are taken frequently because naloxone's action is short.","why":{},"cues":"","aid":"Want ÷ Have × Volume.","src":"Pain_Management_and_Urinary_System_Combined.pdf","pg":"pp15–16; Fundamentals p48","sameFormExclude":["XE20"],"ask":"Calculate","comp":"med","traps":{},"safer":{"s":"Calculate the volume to give.","a":"Order 0.8 mg IM; supply 0.4 mg/mL.","f":"Dosage calculation: want ÷ have × volume.","e":"Check the answer is a reasonable IM volume.","r":"0.8 ÷ 0.4 = 2.0 mL."},"principle":"Estimate the expected size before calculating, then confirm units.","takeaway":"Twice the concentration's dose means twice the volume: 0.8 mg of a 0.4 mg/mL supply is 2 mL.","med":{"why":"Opioid overdose with respiratory depression.","check":"Respirations, LOC, history of opioid dependence (withdrawal risk).","give":"0.8 mg IM = 2 mL of 0.4 mg/mL.","watch":"Return of respiratory depression because naloxone is short-acting.","teach":"Explain why frequent checks continue after it works."},"xv":2},{"id":"T05","subj":"Maternal-Newborn","topic":"Newborn bulb syringe teaching","cn":"Health Promotion and Maintenance","cn2":"","fmt":"mc","diff":"F","stem":"The nurse is reinforcing bulb-syringe teaching with the parent of a 1-day-old newborn. Which statement shows the parent needs further teaching?","opts":["\"I'll squeeze the bulb before I put the tip in.\"","\"I'll suction his nose first, then his mouth.\"","\"I'll place the tip along the side of his mouth, not in the middle.\"","\"I can stop when his cry no longer sounds wet or bubbly.\""],"key":1,"rat":"Suction the mouth first: the newborn is an obligatory nose breather, so touching the nares can make him gasp and inhale pharyngeal secretions. Compress the bulb first, use the side of the mouth to avoid the gag reflex, and stop when the cry is clear.","why":{"0":"Correct technique.","2":"Correct technique.","3":"Correct technique."},"cues":"","aid":"M before N, like the alphabet: Mouth, then Nose.","src":"Maternal_Health_Linear_Chapter_Sources_Updated.pdf","pg":"pp. 91–131 (Box 27.4)","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Evaluate","comp":"teach","traps":{"0":"irrelevant","2":"irrelevant","3":"irrelevant"},"safer":{"s":"Find the statement that shows a need for more teaching.","a":"Bulb syringe use in a 1-day-old.","f":"Teaching evaluation: negative stem.","e":"Compressing first, side of the mouth, and stopping when the cry is clear are all correct.","r":"Nose before mouth is the error because the newborn is an obligatory nose breather."},"principle":"In 'further teaching' items, look for the one wrong statement.","takeaway":"Mouth before nose, or the baby gasps secretions into the airway.","xv":2},{"id":"NG1","subj":"Neuro & Sensory","topic":"Increased ICP after head injury","cn":"Physiological Adaptation","cn2":"Reduction of Risk Potential","fmt":"bowtie","diff":"A","ngn":true,"exhibit":"<table><tr><th>Time</th><th>LOC</th><th>BP</th><th>HR</th><th>Resp</th><th>Other</th></tr><tr><td>0800</td><td>Alert, oriented, GCS 15</td><td>132/80</td><td>88</td><td>16, regular</td><td>Mild headache</td></tr><tr><td>1000</td><td>Drowsy, opens eyes to voice</td><td>168/84</td><td>54</td><td>Irregular</td><td>Headache worse; vomited once without nausea</td></tr></table>","stem":"A 34-year-old was admitted for observation after a motorcycle crash with a closed head injury. Complete the bow-tie: choose the condition the client is most likely experiencing, 2 actions to take, and 2 parameters to monitor.","cond":{"opts":["Increased intracranial pressure","Hypovolemic shock","Cerebrospinal fluid leak","Absence seizure"],"key":0},"act":{"opts":["Elevate head of bed 30° with head midline","Lower the head of bed flat","Suction the nares to clear secretions","Reduce stimuli: dim lights, quiet room","Encourage bearing down for a bowel movement"],"key":[0,3]},"par":{"opts":["Level of consciousness (GCS)","Bowel sounds","Pupillary response","Capillary refill in the toes","Skin turgor"],"key":[0,2]},"rat":"Decreased LOC, worse headache, vomiting without nausea, and Cushing's triad (hypertension with widening pulse pressure 52→84, bradycardia 88→54, irregular respirations) indicate increased ICP. Interventions: HOB 30° with head midline, reduce stimuli, avoid straining. Monitor LOC/GCS and pupils.","why":{"Hypovolemic shock":"Shock raises pulse and lowers BP, the opposite trend.","Cerebrospinal fluid leak":"No clear nasal or ear drainage is described.","Absence seizure":"A brief staring spell.","Lower the head of bed flat":"Undoes the ordered 30° elevation.","Suction the nares to clear secretions":"Nasal suctioning is avoided with head injury and possible CSF leak.","Encourage bearing down for a bowel movement":"Straining and Valsalva are avoided.","Bowel sounds":"Does not track ICP.","Capillary refill in the toes":"Does not track ICP.","Skin turgor":"Does not track ICP."},"aid":"The skull is a sealed lunchbox of brain, blood, and CSF: add to one and pressure rises. Cushing's triad: BP up, heart rate down, breathing erratic.","src":"Neuro_Nursing_Complete_Combined_Study_Sources.pdf","pg":"pp. 29–199 (IICP, TBI, CSF slides); Neuro A&P p18","cj":"Prioritize hypotheses","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Interpret","comp":"analysis","safer":{"s":"Complete the bow-tie: condition, two actions, two parameters.","a":"Falling LOC, worse headache, vomiting, BP up with wider pulse pressure, HR down, irregular respirations.","f":"Bow-tie: condition first, then actions that stabilize it, then monitoring that proves response.","e":"Shock trends the opposite way; flat positioning, nasal suction, and straining raise ICP; bowel sounds and skin turgor don't track ICP.","r":"Increased ICP; HOB 30° midline and reduced stimuli; LOC/GCS and pupils."},"principle":"Bow-tie order: name the condition, choose actions that treat it, choose monitoring that shows whether it's working.","takeaway":"Cushing's triad (BP up, HR down, breathing erratic) plus falling LOC means rising ICP.","xv":2},{"id":"NG2","subj":"Respiratory","topic":"Chest tube problem-solving","cn":"Reduction of Risk Potential","cn2":"Safety and Infection Prevention and Control","fmt":"matrix","diff":"A","ngn":true,"stem":"For each chest-tube situation, select the correct nursing action.","rows":["The chest tube is pulled out of the client's chest.","The drainage system cracks and breaks.","There is continuous bubbling in the water-seal chamber.","A transporter places the drainage unit on the bed at chest level."],"cols":["Apply an occlusive dressing with pressure and notify the provider","Place the distal end of the tubing in sterile water","Check the system for an air leak and report it","Lower the unit below the level of the chest"],"key":[0,1,2,3],"rat":"If the tube comes out, immediately apply pressure with an occlusive dressing and notify the provider. If the drainage system breaks, place the distal end of the tubing in sterile water. Continuous bubbling in the water seal means an air leak. The unit is never raised to chest level because fluid drains back into the pleural space. Tubes are not stripped or milked.","why":{},"aid":"Out of chest → seal it. System broken → sink it (sterile water). Nonstop bubbles → leak. Unit high → drop it.","src":"Respiratory System Disorders pt 2.pdf","pg":"pp17, 19, 22","cj":"Take action","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Intervene","comp":"intervention","safer":{"s":"Match each chest-tube problem to its action.","a":"Tube out, system broken, continuous bubbling, unit at chest level.","f":"Matrix: one row at a time, same client.","e":"Each row has one correct action.","r":"Seal it, sink it, check for a leak, lower it."},"principle":"Work a matrix one row at a time.","takeaway":"Out → seal. Broken → sterile water. Nonstop bubbles → leak. High → lower.","xv":2},{"id":"NG3","subj":"Maternal-Newborn","topic":"Postpartum assessment","cn":"Health Promotion and Maintenance","cn2":"Reduction of Risk Potential","fmt":"matrix","diff":"I","ngn":true,"stem":"On postpartum day 2 after a vaginal birth, the nurse reviews these findings. For each, indicate whether it is expected or requires follow-up.","rows":["Bright red lochia with a few small clots","Fundus firm, 2 fingerbreadths below the umbilicus","Fundus boggy and 2 cm above the umbilicus","Heavy sweating at night","Cramping while breastfeeding","Episiotomy red with purulent drainage"],"cols":["Expected","Requires follow-up"],"key":[0,0,1,0,0,1],"rat":"Lochia rubra may contain small clots in the first day or two. The fundus descends about one fingerbreadth per day after rising to the umbilicus in the first 12 hours. Profuse diaphoresis, especially at night, is common in the first week, and afterbirth pains are more common in breastfeeding clients. A boggy, high fundus risks hemorrhage, and an episiotomy should be free of erythema and discharge.","why":{},"aid":"Day-by-day uterus: down a finger a day, firm as a grapefruit; red flags are boggy, high, or a red, draining incision.","src":"Maternal_Health_Linear_Chapter_Sources_Updated.pdf","pg":"pp. 145–168 (Ch. 28)","cj":"Recognize cues","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Interpret","comp":"cue","safer":{"s":"Classify each postpartum day 2 finding.","a":"Lochia, fundal height and tone, sweating, cramping, episiotomy.","f":"Expected versus abnormal postpartum findings.","e":"Small clots, descending firm fundus, night sweats, and afterpains are expected.","r":"Boggy high fundus and a draining red episiotomy need follow-up."},"principle":"Know the expected day-by-day course to spot deviations.","takeaway":"Down a finger a day and firm; boggy, high, or draining means follow-up.","xv":2},{"id":"NG4","subj":"Maternal-Newborn","topic":"Postpartum hypovolemic shock trend","cn":"Physiological Adaptation","cn2":"Coordinated Care","fmt":"sata","diff":"A","ngn":true,"exhibit":"<table><tr><th>Time</th><th>BP</th><th>HR</th><th>Pad</th><th>Client</th></tr><tr><td>0800</td><td>118/72</td><td>80</td><td>Moderate</td><td>Resting</td></tr><tr><td>0830</td><td>112/70</td><td>92</td><td>Saturated</td><td>\"I feel a little funny\"</td></tr><tr><td>0845</td><td>100/62</td><td>108</td><td>Saturated in 15 min</td><td>Lightheaded; skin cool and clammy; fundus boggy</td></tr></table>","stem":"Two hours after a vaginal birth, the nurse trends these findings. Which actions should the nurse take? Select all that apply.","opts":["Notify the primary health care provider.","Gently massage the boggy fundus and expel clots.","Give oxygen by face mask at 8–10 L/min per protocol.","Elevate the client's legs to at least 30 degrees.","Help the client walk to the bathroom to empty her bladder.","Place the client in high Fowler's position."],"key":[0,1,2,3],"rat":"A pad soaked within 15 minutes, a rising pulse, falling BP, lightheadedness, and cool clammy skin indicate hypovolemic shock. Box 28.1: notify the provider, massage an atonic uterus gently and expel clots, give oxygen at 8–10 L/min, tilt the client or elevate the right hip, and elevate the legs at least 30 degrees.","why":{"4":"Walking a client in shock risks syncope and injury.","5":"Legs are elevated; sitting upright worsens perfusion to the brain."},"aid":"Shock trend: pulse climbs while BP slides — the two lines cross like scissors.","src":"Maternal_Health_Linear_Chapter_Sources_Updated.pdf","pg":"pp. 145–156 (Box 28.1)","cj":"Generate solutions","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Intervene","comp":"intervention","traps":{"4":"unsafe","5":"unsafe"},"safer":{"s":"Select actions for the trend.","a":"Pad soaked in 15 minutes, pulse rising, BP falling, lightheaded, cool clammy skin, boggy uterus.","f":"Trend: hypovolemic shock from postpartum hemorrhage.","e":"Walking risks syncope; high Fowler's reduces brain perfusion.","r":"Notify, massage and expel clots, oxygen, elevate legs."},"principle":"Read the trend, not one value; act on the shock pattern.","takeaway":"Pulse up while BP slides: the scissors sign of shock.","xv":2},{"id":"F01","subj":"Fundamentals","topic":"DNR order","cn":"Coordinated Care","cn2":"Psychosocial Integrity","fmt":"mc","diff":"I","stem":"A client with a written do-not-resuscitate (DNR) order stops breathing. The client's son, at the bedside, shouts, \"Do something! Start CPR!\" What is the nurse's best action?","opts":["Begin chest compressions because the son, as next of kin, has requested them","Honor the DNR order, stay with the son, and notify the charge nurse and provider","Start CPR until the provider arrives and confirms the written order","Call a code and let the response team decide whether to resuscitate"],"key":1,"rat":"A written DNR order reflects the client's own choice to be allowed to die if breathing or the heart stops. The slide states that a nurse who resuscitates a client with a DNR order acts without the client's consent. Treatments the client did not refuse continue, and the nurse supports the family.","why":{"0":"The client's documented wishes, not a family request, govern resuscitation.","2":"Starting CPR 'until confirmed' is still resuscitation without consent; the order is already written.","3":"Calling a code initiates resuscitation the client refused."},"cues":"Written DNR order; family distress does not change the client's decision.","aid":"DNR = the client already answered the question. The nurse's job is to protect that answer.","src":"Fundamentals HESI review 3.pdf","pg":"p17","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Intervene","comp":"scope","traps":{"0":"scope","2":"scope","3":"scope"},"safer":{"s":"Choose the best action when a client with a DNR order stops breathing.","a":"Written DNR order; distressed son demanding CPR.","f":"Legal and ethical: the client's documented choice.","e":"Compressions, CPR until confirmation, and calling a code all resuscitate without consent.","r":"Honoring the order while supporting the son beats CPR because the client already decided."},"principle":"The client's documented decision governs, not a family member's request.","takeaway":"A DNR order is the client's answer. Protect it and support the family.","xv":2},{"id":"F02","subj":"Fundamentals","topic":"Advance directive changes","cn":"Coordinated Care","cn2":"","fmt":"mc","diff":"F","stem":"An alert, oriented client tells the nurse she wants to change the advance directive she signed last year. Her daughter, who is named as her health care proxy, objects. Which response by the nurse is accurate?","opts":["\"Your proxy must approve any changes before they take effect.\"","\"Advance directives can be updated only once a year.\"","\"You have the right to change your advance directive at any time.\"","\"Because your daughter is your proxy, she makes these decisions now.\""],"key":2,"rat":"The client has the right to change an advance directive whenever she wishes. A health care proxy (durable power of attorney for health care) makes decisions only if and when the client can no longer make them.","why":{"0":"The proxy has no authority while the client can decide for herself.","1":"No such time limit exists in the source; changes can be made any time.","3":"The proxy acts only when the client is incapacitated; this client is alert and oriented."},"cues":"Client alert and oriented = client decides.","aid":"A proxy is a spare key: it is only used when the owner can't open the door herself.","src":"Fundamentals HESI review 3.pdf","pg":"pp2-3","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Intervene","comp":"scope","traps":{"0":"otherdx","1":"unsafe","3":"otherdx"},"safer":{"s":"Choose the accurate response.","a":"Alert, oriented client; proxy objects.","f":"Legal: advance directives and proxies.","e":"Proxy approval, a yearly limit, and proxy authority now are all inaccurate.","r":"The client can change it anytime because she can still decide for herself."},"principle":"A proxy's authority starts only when the client cannot decide.","takeaway":"Alert and oriented means the client is the decision maker.","xv":2},{"id":"F03","subj":"Fundamentals","topic":"Contact precautions, drug-resistant organism","cn":"Safety and Infection Prevention and Control","cn2":"","fmt":"sata","diff":"I","stem":"A client with a drug-resistant organism is placed on contact precautions. Which actions are appropriate? Select all that apply.","opts":["Wear a gown and gloves for contact with the client.","Keep the stethoscope and blood pressure cuff in the client's room.","Use a disposable thermometer for this client.","Place the client in a semiprivate room with a client who has a different infection.","Wipe the blood pressure cuff with disinfectant and take it to the next client's room."],"key":[0,1,2],"rat":"Contact precautions call for gown and gloves, individual or disposable thermometers, and a private room or a room with a cohort client. When a drug-resistant organism is present, all equipment stays in the client's room.","why":{"3":"Rooming is private or with a cohort client (same organism), not a client with a different infection.","4":"With a drug-resistant organism, equipment is kept in the room rather than wiped and moved."},"cues":"\"Drug-resistant\" changes the equipment rule: it stays in the room.","aid":"Drug-resistant bugs get a 'stay-at-home' order: the equipment moves in and never moves out.","src":"Fundamentals HESI review 3.pdf","pg":"p15","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Intervene","comp":"intervention","traps":{"3":"otherdx","4":"otherdx"},"safer":{"s":"Select appropriate contact-precaution actions.","a":"Drug-resistant organism.","f":"Infection control: contact precautions.","e":"Rooming with a different infection and moving disinfected equipment fit other situations.","r":"Gown and gloves, equipment kept in the room, and disposable thermometers fit."},"principle":"Read the qualifier: drug-resistant changes the equipment rule.","takeaway":"With a drug-resistant organism, equipment moves in and stays.","xv":2},{"id":"F04","subj":"Fundamentals","topic":"C. difficile enteric precautions","cn":"Safety and Infection Prevention and Control","cn2":"","fmt":"mc","diff":"F","stem":"A client with Clostridioides difficile infection is on enteric contact isolation. Which action by the nurse when leaving the room is correct?","opts":["Remove gloves and gown, then use alcohol-based hand rub at the door.","Remove gown and gloves, then wash hands with soap and water.","Skip hand hygiene this time because gloves were worn during all care.","Keep the gown on to wear to the next client's room on the unit."],"key":1,"rat":"The instructor's enteric contact isolation sign requires clean hands, gown, and gloves on entry, and cleaning hands with soap and water on exit. C. difficile spreads by ingesting spores found on surfaces and unwashed hands.","why":{"0":"The sign specifies soap and water on exit for this isolation type.","2":"Gloves do not replace hand hygiene; spores spread on unwashed hands.","3":"Gowns are used for one client's care and are not worn between rooms."},"cues":"C. diff = spores = soap and water.","aid":"Spores are like glitter: you can't rub them away, you have to wash them off.","src":"Fundamentals HESI review 3.pdf","pg":"pp31-32","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Intervene","comp":"intervention","traps":{"0":"otherdx","2":"unsafe","3":"unsafe"},"safer":{"s":"Choose the correct exit action.","a":"C. difficile, enteric contact isolation.","f":"Infection control: spore-forming organism.","e":"Alcohol rub fits other isolation; skipping hygiene and reusing a gown are unsafe.","r":"Soap and water removes spores; alcohol rub does not appear on the enteric sign."},"principle":"Match the hand hygiene method to the organism.","takeaway":"C. diff spores wash off; they don't rub off.","xv":2},{"id":"F05","subj":"Fundamentals","topic":"Enemas until clear","cn":"Basic Care and Comfort","cn2":"Safety and Infection Prevention and Control","fmt":"mc","diff":"I","stem":"A client has a prescription for \"enemas until clear.\" After the third enema, the return still contains brown fecal particles and the client says he is exhausted. What should the nurse do next?","opts":["Give a fourth enema, since the order says \"until clear.\"","Raise the enema container higher so the next enema works faster.","Consult the health care provider before giving any more enemas.","Document that the order is complete and stop the enemas."],"key":2,"rat":"If three enemas have been given and the results are still not clear, the nurse consults the provider before giving more, because excessive enemas deplete fluids and electrolytes.","why":{"0":"Continuing past three without consulting risks fluid and electrolyte loss.","1":"Container height is set per procedure; raising it does not address the safety concern.","3":"The order is not complete, and stopping without communicating leaves the problem unresolved."},"cues":"Third enema not clear + fatigue = fluid and electrolyte risk.","aid":"Three strikes, then call: three enemas without clear results means phone the provider.","src":"Fundamentals HESI review 3.pdf","pg":"p20","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Intervene","comp":"intervention","traps":{"0":"unsafe","1":"irrelevant","3":"delay"},"safer":{"s":"Choose the next step after a third enema.","a":"Third enema not clear; client exhausted.","f":"Procedure safety: fluid and electrolyte loss.","e":"A fourth enema risks depletion, height doesn't address safety, stopping silently leaves it unresolved.","r":"Consulting the provider beats a fourth enema because three is the safety limit."},"principle":"Know the built-in stopping point of a procedure.","takeaway":"Three enemas not clear: call before giving more.","compare":{"a":2,"b":0,"aWhy":"It respects the three-enema limit and gets direction.","bWhy":"The order literally says until clear.","decider":"After three enemas, the procedure requires consulting the provider because of fluid and electrolyte loss.","flip":"After only one or two enemas, giving another would follow the order."},"xv":2},{"id":"F06","subj":"Fundamentals","topic":"Intake calculation","cn":"Basic Care and Comfort","cn2":"","fmt":"num","diff":"I","stem":"Calculate this client's intake for the 0700–1500 shift. Breakfast: 6 oz orange juice, 1 cup coffee, scrambled eggs, toast. Lunch: 4 oz gelatin, 8 oz milk, turkey sandwich. IV normal saline at 50 mL/hr for the full 8 hours. Use 1 oz = 30 mL and 1 cup = 250 mL. Enter a whole number.","key":1190,"unit":"mL","tol":0,"rat":"Count liquids and foods that are liquid at room temperature (gelatin counts) plus IV fluids. Juice 180 + coffee 250 + gelatin 120 + milk 240 + IV 400 (50 × 8) = 1190 mL. Eggs, toast, and the sandwich are not counted.","why":{},"cues":"Gelatin counts; solid foods do not; IV fluid counts.","aid":"If it melts or pours at room temperature, it goes on the I&O sheet.","src":"Fundamentals HESI review 3.pdf","pg":"pp28-30","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Calculate","comp":"intervention","traps":{},"safer":{"s":"Total the intake.","a":"Juice, coffee, gelatin, milk, IV 50 mL/hr × 8.","f":"I&O rules: liquids at room temperature plus IV.","e":"Eggs, toast, and sandwich don't count.","r":"180 + 250 + 120 + 240 + 400 = 1190 mL."},"principle":"Write units and convert before adding.","takeaway":"If it pours or melts at room temperature, count it; add the IV.","xv":2},{"id":"F07","subj":"Fundamentals","topic":"Treatment refusal","cn":"Coordinated Care","cn2":"Psychosocial Integrity","fmt":"mc","diff":"I","stem":"An alert, oriented client refuses a prescribed wound debridement, saying, \"I just don't want it.\" What is the nurse's best action?","opts":["Explain that the provider ordered the procedure, so it must be done today","Explore the client's concerns, review benefits and risks, and report the refusal","Give a PRN sedative so the client relaxes and agrees to the procedure","Ask a family member to sign the consent form in the client's place"],"key":1,"rat":"Patients have the right to refuse treatment based on informed consent. The nurse's role is to educate about benefits and risks, answer questions, assess capacity, document the refusal, and consult the provider.","why":{"0":"Recommended treatment can still be refused by a capable client.","2":"Sedating a client to obtain agreement violates autonomy.","3":"A capable client consents or refuses for herself."},"cues":"Alert and oriented = capacity to refuse.","aid":"Refusal workflow: Ask, Teach, Document, Report.","src":"Fundamentals HESI review 3.pdf","pg":"pp71-72","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Intervene","comp":"scope","traps":{"0":"unsafe","2":"unsafe","3":"scope"},"safer":{"s":"Choose the best response to a refusal.","a":"Alert, oriented client refusing a procedure.","f":"Ethics: informed refusal.","e":"Insisting, sedating, and family consent violate autonomy.","r":"Exploring concerns, teaching, and documenting respects the client's right."},"principle":"A capable client may refuse; the nurse informs, documents, and reports.","takeaway":"Refusal workflow: ask, teach, document, report.","xv":2},{"id":"F08","subj":"Fundamentals","topic":"Spiritual support","cn":"Psychosocial Integrity","cn2":"","fmt":"mc","diff":"F","stem":"A hospitalized client says tearfully, \"I don't know if God will ever forgive me.\" Which response by the nurse is most appropriate?","opts":["\"I'm sure God has already forgiven you, so try not to worry about it.\"","\"Would you like to talk about it? I can also call the chaplain for you.\"","\"Let me share the prayer that helps me when I feel that way.\"","\"Let's focus on getting you well; that's what matters right now.\""],"key":1,"rat":"Spiritual support includes active listening, presence, respectful communication that does not impose the nurse's beliefs, and referral to a chaplain or spiritual care provider if the client wants it.","why":{"0":"False reassurance closes the conversation.","2":"Sharing the nurse's own prayer imposes the nurse's beliefs.","3":"Redirecting dismisses the client's spiritual concern."},"cues":"Client opens a spiritual concern; the nurse invites, listens, offers referral.","aid":"Listen, be present, refer: be the bridge to the chaplain, not the sermon.","src":"Fundamentals HESI review 3.pdf","pg":"p8","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Intervene","comp":"intervention","traps":{"0":"reassure","2":"unsafe","3":"irrelevant"},"safer":{"s":"Choose the most therapeutic response.","a":"Tearful spiritual concern.","f":"Therapeutic communication and spiritual support.","e":"False reassurance, imposing beliefs, and changing the subject shut it down.","r":"Inviting the client to talk and offering the chaplain fits."},"principle":"Acknowledge and explore; refer when the client wants it.","takeaway":"Be the bridge to the chaplain, not the sermon.","xv":2},{"id":"F09","subj":"Fundamentals","topic":"Stages of grief","cn":"Psychosocial Integrity","cn2":"","fmt":"mc","diff":"F","stem":"The wife of a terminally ill client says, \"If God lets him live until our anniversary, I'll go to church every week.\" Which stage of grief is she most likely in?","opts":["Denial","Anger","Bargaining","Acceptance"],"key":2,"rat":"Bargaining involves making promises or deals with a higher power and \"what if\" or \"if only\" thinking.","why":{"0":"Denial shows disbelief, numbness, and avoiding reality.","1":"Anger would show blame or hostility, not a deal.","3":"Acceptance is acknowledging the loss and finding renewed meaning."},"cues":"\"If... then I will...\" = a deal.","aid":"Bargaining sounds like a contract: 'If you..., I'll...'","src":"Fundamentals HESI review 3.pdf","pg":"pp38-39","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Interpret","comp":"analysis","traps":{"0":"otherdx","1":"otherdx","3":"otherdx"},"safer":{"s":"Identify the stage of grief.","a":"\"If God lets him live..., I'll...\"","f":"Grief stages.","e":"Denial, anger, and acceptance sound different.","r":"A deal with a higher power is bargaining."},"principle":"Match the language pattern to the stage.","takeaway":"\"If you..., I'll...\" is bargaining.","xv":2},{"id":"F10","subj":"Fundamentals","topic":"Sleep promotion in older adults","cn":"Basic Care and Comfort","cn2":"Health Promotion and Maintenance","fmt":"mc","diff":"F","stem":"A 78-year-old client reports trouble sleeping at night. Which statement indicates the client needs further teaching?","opts":["\"I'll go to bed and get up at the same times every day.\"","\"I'll take a long nap after lunch so I'm well rested.\"","\"I'll stop drinking coffee by midafternoon.\"","\"I'll take my walk in the morning instead of after dinner.\""],"key":1,"rat":"Sleep teaching includes not napping during the day, avoiding caffeine for at least 3–4 hours before bed, exercising regularly but not within 3 hours of bedtime, and keeping consistent sleep and wake times.","why":{"0":"Consistent times are recommended.","2":"Stopping caffeine well before bedtime is recommended.","3":"Exercise is fine when it is not within 3 hours of bedtime."},"cues":"Daytime nap = undermines nighttime sleep.","aid":"Save your sleep for the night shift: no daytime naps.","src":"Fundamentals HESI review 3.pdf","pg":"p35","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Evaluate","comp":"teach","traps":{"0":"irrelevant","2":"irrelevant","3":"irrelevant"},"safer":{"s":"Find the statement needing more teaching.","a":"Older adult with insomnia.","f":"Teaching evaluation: sleep hygiene.","e":"Consistent times, early caffeine cutoff, morning exercise are correct.","r":"A long afternoon nap undermines night sleep."},"principle":"In negative stems, hunt for the unsafe or incorrect statement.","takeaway":"No daytime naps when nights are the problem.","xv":2},{"id":"F11","subj":"Fundamentals","topic":"Pressure injury prevention, supervising UAP","cn":"Coordinated Care","cn2":"Reduction of Risk Potential","fmt":"mc","diff":"I","stem":"The LVN observes a UAP caring for an immobile client at risk for pressure injury. Which action by the UAP requires the LVN to intervene?","opts":["Uses a lift sheet to move the client up in bed.","Repositions the client every 2 hours.","Massages the reddened skin over the client's sacrum.","Applies barrier cream after cleaning an incontinence episode."],"key":2,"rat":"Bony prominences are not scrubbed or rubbed. Lifting rather than pulling prevents shear and friction, repositioning every 2 hours in bed is expected, and a barrier protects skin after incontinence.","why":{"0":"Lifting with a sheet prevents shearing.","1":"Every 2 hours in bed is the standard.","3":"A barrier for incontinence is recommended."},"cues":"\"Massage\" over a bony prominence.","aid":"Red over bone? Hands off, pressure off.","src":"Fundamentals HESI review 3.pdf","pg":"p63","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Scope","comp":"scope","traps":{"0":"irrelevant","1":"irrelevant","3":"irrelevant"},"safer":{"s":"Find the UAP action that needs intervention.","a":"Reddened sacrum; massage.","f":"Supervision: pressure injury prevention.","e":"Lift sheet, 2-hour turns, and barrier cream are correct.","r":"Massaging a reddened bony prominence must be stopped."},"principle":"Supervision means stopping harmful actions, not correct ones.","takeaway":"Red over bone: hands off, pressure off.","xv":2},{"id":"G01","subj":"Adult GI","topic":"Peptic ulcer perforation","cn":"Physiological Adaptation","cn2":"","fmt":"mc","diff":"A","stem":"A client with a known duodenal ulcer suddenly reports severe abdominal pain. The abdomen is rigid and board-like, HR is 124, and BP is 88/50. Which complication does the nurse suspect?","opts":["Gastric outlet obstruction","Perforation with peritonitis","Expected duodenal ulcer pain after a meal","Acute gastritis"],"key":1,"rat":"Perforation is listed as the most lethal peptic ulcer complication and leads to bacterial peritonitis. Peritonitis presents with severe abdominal pain, rebound tenderness, board-like rigidity, tachycardia, and hypotension.","why":{"0":"Obstruction causes fullness and vomiting, not a rigid abdomen with shock signs.","2":"Duodenal ulcer pain is a burning pain 1–2 hours after eating, without rigidity or hypotension.","3":"Gastritis does not cause board-like rigidity and shock."},"cues":"Board-like abdomen + tachycardia + hypotension.","aid":"A 'board' belly is a red flag: the GI tract has sprung a leak into the abdomen.","src":"GI_Linear_Chapter_Sourcebook.pdf","pg":"pp40, 86-87","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Interpret","comp":"analysis","traps":{"0":"otherdx","2":"reassure","3":"otherdx"},"safer":{"s":"Name the complication.","a":"Sudden severe pain, board-like abdomen, HR 124, BP 88/50, known duodenal ulcer.","f":"Cue clustering: peritonitis with a shock pattern.","e":"Obstruction, expected ulcer pain, and gastritis lack rigidity and hypotension.","r":"Perforation with peritonitis explains every cue."},"principle":"Cluster cues into one physiologic story before naming it.","takeaway":"Board-like abdomen plus falling BP and rising HR means a leak into the abdomen.","xv":2},{"id":"G02","subj":"Adult GI","topic":"H2 blocker and antacid timing","cn":"Pharmacological Therapies","cn2":"","fmt":"mc","diff":"F","stem":"A client with peptic ulcer disease is prescribed famotidine and an antacid. How should the nurse schedule these medications?","opts":["Give them together so the client takes fewer doses.","Give the antacid first, then famotidine 15 minutes later.","Separate famotidine and the antacid by at least 2 hours.","Give famotidine only when the client reports heartburn."],"key":2,"rat":"The ulcer slides direct that histamine receptor blockers (cimetidine, ranitidine, famotidine, nizatidine) are not given within 2 hours of antacids.","why":{"0":"Giving them together violates the 2-hour separation.","1":"Fifteen minutes is far short of 2 hours.","3":"Famotidine is scheduled as prescribed, not only as needed."},"cues":"H2 blocker + antacid in the same order set.","aid":"Antacids are bouncers at the stomach door: give the H2 blocker 2 hours away so it isn't turned away.","src":"GI_Linear_Chapter_Sourcebook.pdf","pg":"p41","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Intervene","comp":"med","traps":{"0":"unsafe","1":"unsafe","3":"otherdx"},"safer":{"s":"Choose the correct schedule.","a":"Famotidine plus an antacid.","f":"Medication administration timing.","e":"Together, 15 minutes apart, or PRN only all break the rule.","r":"A 2-hour separation is the rule from the ulcer slides."},"principle":"Timing is part of giving a drug correctly.","takeaway":"H2 blockers and antacids: at least 2 hours apart.","med":{"why":"Peptic ulcer disease.","check":"Other GI drugs and their timing.","give":"Separate from antacids by at least 2 hours.","watch":"Ulcer symptoms.","teach":"Keep the 2-hour spacing at home."},"xv":2},{"id":"G03","subj":"Adult GI","topic":"Hepatic encephalopathy","cn":"Physiological Adaptation","cn2":"Pharmacological Therapies","fmt":"mc","diff":"I","stem":"A client with cirrhosis becomes increasingly disoriented and has a flapping tremor of the hands. Which medication does the nurse anticipate the provider will order?","opts":["Lactulose","Furosemide","Vitamin K","Propranolol"],"key":0,"rat":"Disorientation and asterixis signal hepatic encephalopathy from ammonia intoxication. Lactulose is given to reduce the ammonia level.","why":{"1":"Diuretics treat fluid retention and ascites.","2":"Vitamin K is given for abnormal coagulation results.","3":"Beta blockers are prophylaxis for esophageal varices."},"cues":"Confusion + asterixis in cirrhosis = ammonia.","aid":"Lactulose 'flushes' ammonia out: think of it as taking out the trash the liver can't.","src":"GI_Linear_Chapter_Sourcebook.pdf","pg":"pp119, 136-137","sameFormExclude":["XE08","XE13"],"ask":"Interpret","comp":"med","traps":{"1":"otherdx","2":"otherdx","3":"otherdx"},"safer":{"s":"Anticipate the medication.","a":"Cirrhosis, rising disorientation, asterixis.","f":"Hepatic encephalopathy from ammonia.","e":"Furosemide, vitamin K, and propranolol treat other cirrhosis complications.","r":"Lactulose lowers ammonia, the cause of these signs."},"principle":"Match each drug to the complication it treats.","takeaway":"Confusion and asterixis in cirrhosis point to ammonia, so lactulose.","xv":2},{"id":"G04","ver":2,"subj":"Adult GI","topic":"Ruptured esophageal varix","cn":"Physiological Adaptation","cn2":"Reduction of Risk Potential","fmt":"mc","diff":"A","ask":"Prioritize","comp":"priority","stem":"A client with cirrhosis and known esophageal varices suddenly vomits a large amount of bright red blood and becomes restless. Which action should the nurse take first?","opts":["Obtain a full set of vital signs and measure the amount of emesis","Position the client to keep the airway clear and call for help","Give the scheduled propranolol early to lower portal pressure","Reinforce teaching to avoid coughing, sneezing, and straining"],"key":1,"rat":"A ruptured esophageal varix is a medical emergency. Medical management begins with a patent airway, then bleeding control, IV access for fluids and blood, and endoscopy. Vital-sign monitoring is part of care but follows airway protection. Beta blockers are prophylaxis, and avoiding abdominal pressure is teaching for prevention.","why":{"0":"Vital signs are needed, but measuring them before protecting the airway delays the first priority in an obvious emergency.","2":"Propranolol is preventive therapy, not treatment of an active bleed.","3":"Teaching prevents rupture; it does nothing once bleeding has started."},"traps":{"0":"delay","2":"otherdx","3":"later"},"cues":"Large bright red hematemesis, restlessness, known varices.","aid":"Varix bleed: airway first, then stop the bleed, then lines and blood.","principle":"When the emergency is obvious, act before gathering more data.","takeaway":"Assessment is the default only when the problem is unclear. A massive bleed with an airway at risk is already clear.","safer":{"s":"Choose the first action.","a":"Large bright red emesis, restlessness, known varices.","f":"Emergency: airway and hemorrhage.","e":"Propranolol and teaching are preventive; vital signs come after the airway.","r":"Protecting the airway beats vital signs because blood in the airway threatens breathing now."},"compare":{"a":1,"b":0,"aWhy":"A patent airway is the first step in managing a ruptured varix.","bWhy":"Vital signs show how much the bleeding is affecting circulation.","decider":"The emergency is already obvious, and the airway is at risk from vomited blood.","flip":"If a small amount of blood-streaked emesis occurred in a calm client with a clear airway, assessing vital signs first would fit."},"src":"GI_Linear_Chapter_Sourcebook.pdf","pg":"pp122, 136","sameFormExclude":["XE23"],"cog":"judgment"},{"id":"G05","subj":"Adult GI","topic":"Ostomy pouch fitting","cn":"Basic Care and Comfort","cn2":"","fmt":"mc","diff":"F","stem":"The nurse is reinforcing teaching about changing an ostomy pouch. Which instruction is correct?","opts":["Cut the skin barrier opening exactly the size of the stoma.","Cut the opening about 1/16 inch larger than the stoma.","Cut the opening 1 inch larger to avoid touching the stoma.","Change the entire pouch system every day."],"key":1,"rat":"The pouch opening is cut 1/16 inch larger than the stoma, and the system is changed every 5–7 days, with assessment of peristomal skin for irritation and infection.","why":{"0":"An exact fit can rub and injure the stoma.","2":"A gap of 1 inch exposes skin to stool, an irritant.","3":"The usual change interval is every 5–7 days."},"cues":"Pouch fit and skin protection.","aid":"1/16: just enough room for the stoma to breathe, not enough for stool to sneak onto skin.","src":"GI_Linear_Chapter_Sourcebook.pdf","pg":"p75","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Teach","comp":"teach","traps":{"0":"unsafe","2":"unsafe","3":"otherdx"},"safer":{"s":"Choose the correct instruction.","a":"Ostomy pouch fitting.","f":"Teaching: skin protection.","e":"Exact fit rubs, 1 inch exposes skin, daily changes are too frequent.","r":"A 1/16-inch margin protects both stoma and skin."},"principle":"Precise numbers matter in procedure teaching.","takeaway":"Cut the barrier 1/16 inch larger than the stoma.","xv":2},{"id":"R01","subj":"Renal/Urinary","topic":"AV fistula assessment","cn":"Physiological Adaptation","cn2":"Reduction of Risk Potential","fmt":"mc","diff":"I","stem":"A client on hemodialysis has a left forearm arteriovenous fistula. The nurse cannot palpate a thrill or hear a bruit over the site. What should the nurse do?","opts":["Elevate the arm and reassess at the next shift.","Take a blood pressure in the left arm to check perfusion.","Report the finding to the provider promptly.","Apply a warm pack and document the finding."],"key":2,"rat":"The fistula is auscultated for a bruit and palpated for a thrill to confirm it is open. Clotting of the vascular access is a listed risk, so absent findings are reported, not watched.","why":{"0":"Waiting delays treatment of a possibly clotted access.","1":"Blood pressures and venipuncture are never done in the arm with the access.","3":"Documenting alone does not address the possible clot."},"cues":"No thrill, no bruit = no flow.","aid":"Feel the thrill, hear the bruit: if the fistula goes quiet, sound the alarm.","src":"Urinary_System_Chapters_1-6.pdf","pg":"pp91-93","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Intervene","comp":"intervention","traps":{"0":"delay","1":"unsafe","3":"delay"},"safer":{"s":"Choose the action for an absent thrill and bruit.","a":"No thrill, no bruit in the fistula.","f":"Access-site safety: possible clotting.","e":"Waiting, BP in the access arm, and warm packs delay or harm.","r":"Prompt reporting beats reassessing later because a clotted access needs treatment now."},"principle":"A missing expected finding is an abnormal finding.","takeaway":"No thrill, no bruit means no flow. Report it.","xv":2},{"id":"R02","subj":"Renal/Urinary","topic":"Peritoneal dialysis complication","cn":"Physiological Adaptation","cn2":"","fmt":"mc","diff":"I","stem":"A client on peritoneal dialysis has a cloudy outflow, abdominal pain, and a temperature of 100.9°F (38.3°C). Which complication does the nurse suspect?","opts":["Peritonitis","Hemorrhage into the peritoneum","Fluid overload","An expected finding after an overnight dwell"],"key":0,"rat":"Between dialyses, the nurse watches for peritonitis: pain, fever, and cloudy fluid. Strict aseptic care of the catheter site helps prevent it.","why":{"1":"Hemorrhage shows as smoky, pink, or red-tinged dialysate.","2":"Fluid overload is tracked by weight, not cloudy outflow with fever.","3":"Cloudy outflow with fever is never expected."},"cues":"Cloudy + fever + pain.","aid":"Cloudy bag, call it in: cloudy dialysate means infection until proven otherwise.","src":"Urinary_System_Chapters_1-6.pdf","pg":"p93","sameFormExclude":["B08"],"ask":"Interpret","comp":"analysis","traps":{"1":"otherdx","2":"otherdx","3":"reassure"},"safer":{"s":"Name the complication.","a":"Cloudy outflow, abdominal pain, fever 100.9°F.","f":"Peritoneal dialysis complications.","e":"Hemorrhage is smoky or pink, overload shows in weight, cloudy with fever is never expected.","r":"Pain, fever, and cloudy fluid are the listed signs of peritonitis."},"principle":"Use the specific appearance of drainage to tell complications apart.","takeaway":"Cloudy dialysate means peritonitis until proven otherwise.","xv":2},{"id":"R03","subj":"Renal/Urinary","topic":"AKI oliguric phase","cn":"Physiological Adaptation","cn2":"Reduction of Risk Potential","fmt":"mc","diff":"I","stem":"A client is in the oliguric phase of acute kidney injury. Which finding does the nurse expect?","opts":["Urine output of 1.5 L in 24 hours","Rising serum potassium","Falling BUN and creatinine","Signs of dehydration from heavy urination"],"key":1,"rat":"In the oliguric phase, BUN and creatinine rise, output falls below 400 mL in 24 hours, and hyperkalemia may develop.","why":{"0":"Output of 1–2 L/24 hr describes the diuretic phase.","2":"BUN and creatinine rise, not fall, in the oliguric phase.","3":"Dehydration from high output occurs in the diuretic phase."},"cues":"Oliguric = little urine = waste and potassium build up.","aid":"Oliguric: 'Oh, little' urine, so what should leave stays in, including potassium.","src":"Urinary_System_Chapters_1-6.pdf","pg":"p84","sameFormExclude":["XE12","XE25"],"ask":"Interpret","comp":"analysis","traps":{"0":"otherdx","2":"otherdx","3":"otherdx"},"safer":{"s":"Identify the expected finding.","a":"Oliguric phase of AKI.","f":"Phases of acute kidney injury.","e":"High output, falling labs, and dehydration belong to the diuretic phase.","r":"Rising potassium fits retained waste and electrolytes."},"principle":"Know which findings belong to which phase.","takeaway":"Oliguric: little urine out, potassium and waste build up.","xv":2},{"id":"R04","subj":"Renal/Urinary","topic":"Phenazopyridine","cn":"Pharmacological Therapies","cn2":"","fmt":"mc","diff":"F","stem":"A client taking phenazopyridine for UTI discomfort calls the clinic. Which report requires follow-up with the provider?","opts":["\"My urine is reddish orange.\"","\"The burning when I urinate is much better.\"","\"The whites of my eyes look yellow.\"","\"I'm not running to the bathroom as often.\""],"key":2,"rat":"Reddish-orange urine is an expected, harmless effect. A yellow tinge to the sclera or skin is a serious adverse effect that must be reported.","why":{"0":"Expected effect; reassure the client.","1":"This is the therapeutic outcome.","3":"Reduced frequency is the intended effect."},"cues":"Yellow sclera.","aid":"Orange pee is fine; yellow eyes are a sign.","src":"Pain_Management_and_Urinary_System_Combined.pdf","pg":"pp57-58, 87","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Prioritize","comp":"med","traps":{"0":"reassure","1":"irrelevant","3":"irrelevant"},"safer":{"s":"Find the report needing follow-up.","a":"Phenazopyridine; yellow sclera.","f":"Expected versus serious adverse effect.","e":"Orange urine is expected; less burning and frequency are the goal.","r":"Yellow sclera is the serious effect to report."},"principle":"Separate expected drug effects from serious adverse effects.","takeaway":"Orange urine is fine; yellow eyes are not.","med":{"why":"Urinary tract discomfort.","check":"Liver-related warning signs before continuing.","give":"As prescribed, short term.","watch":"Reddish-orange urine (expected), yellow skin or sclera (serious).","teach":"Urine color change is harmless; report yellow eyes or skin."},"xv":2},{"id":"R05","subj":"Renal/Urinary","topic":"Chronic renal failure self-care","cn":"Basic Care and Comfort","cn2":"Health Promotion and Maintenance","fmt":"mc","diff":"F","stem":"Which instruction should the nurse reinforce with a client who has chronic renal failure?","opts":["\"Weigh yourself daily at the same time, on the same scale.\"","\"Drink as much fluid as you can each day to flush out the waste.\"","\"Take over-the-counter pain relievers whenever you need them for aches.\"","\"Bathe in hot water to remove urea crystals that build up on your skin.\""],"key":0,"rat":"Renal failure care includes daily weights under the same conditions, fluid intake planned within prescribed limits, no OTC medications without consulting the provider, and bathing with tepid water to remove urea deposits.","why":{"1":"Fluids are restricted and planned per shift.","2":"OTC drugs are avoided without the provider's approval.","3":"Tepid, not hot, water is used."},"cues":"Daily weight is the best fluid gauge.","aid":"Same scale, same time, same clothes: the 'three sames' of daily weight.","src":"Urinary_System_Chapters_1-6.pdf","pg":"pp88-91","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Teach","comp":"teach","traps":{"1":"unsafe","2":"unsafe","3":"otherdx"},"safer":{"s":"Choose the correct instruction.","a":"Chronic renal failure.","f":"Teaching: fluid status and safety.","e":"Unlimited fluids, OTC pain relievers, and hot water are wrong.","r":"Daily weight under the same conditions tracks fluid status."},"principle":"In renal failure, daily weight is the best fluid gauge.","takeaway":"Same scale, same time, same clothes.","xv":2},{"id":"R06","subj":"Renal/Urinary","topic":"UTI prevention teaching","cn":"Health Promotion and Maintenance","cn2":"","fmt":"sata","diff":"F","stem":"Which measures should the nurse include when reinforcing UTI prevention teaching? Select all that apply.","opts":["Wear cotton underwear.","Urinate immediately after intercourse.","Wipe from front to back.","Drink an adequate amount of fluid each day.","Take frequent bubble baths.","Drink citrus juice to keep the bladder healthy."],"key":[0,1,2,3],"rat":"UTI prevention includes cotton underwear and avoiding tight clothing, avoiding frequent bubble baths, washing before and after intercourse and urinating afterward, wiping front to back, and adequate fluids to dilute urine and flush organisms.","why":{"4":"Frequent bubble baths are to be avoided.","5":"Citrus juices are listed as bladder irritants."},"cues":"","aid":"Cotton, Clean front-to-back, Clear it out after sex, and Keep fluids coming.","src":"Pain_Management_and_Urinary_System_Combined.pdf","pg":"pp85-87","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Teach","comp":"teach","traps":{"4":"unsafe","5":"otherdx"},"safer":{"s":"Select every prevention measure.","a":"UTI prevention teaching.","f":"Health promotion.","e":"Bubble baths and citrus irritate.","r":"Cotton, voiding after sex, front-to-back, and fluids all fit."},"principle":"In select-all items, judge each option alone.","takeaway":"Cotton, clean, clear it out, keep fluids coming.","xv":2},{"id":"R07","subj":"Renal/Urinary","topic":"Nitrofurantoin teaching","cn":"Pharmacological Therapies","cn2":"","fmt":"mc","diff":"I","stem":"A client is starting nitrofurantoin for a urinary tract infection. Which statement indicates a need for further teaching?","opts":["\"I'll take it with food or milk to keep my stomach from getting upset.\"","\"I'll space my doses evenly around the clock.\"","\"If my urine turns rust brown, I'll stop the medicine.\"","\"I'll call if I get a fever, chills, rash, or trouble breathing.\""],"key":2,"rat":"Rust-brown to yellow urine is a common, harmless effect and no cause for alarm. The drug is taken with food or milk, spaced evenly, and dyspnea, chills, fever, or rash are reported as possible allergy.","why":{"0":"Correct: reduces GI upset.","1":"Correct: maintains urine concentration.","3":"Correct: early signs of hypersensitivity."},"cues":"","aid":"Brown urine is the drug talking, not danger.","src":"Pain_Management_and_Urinary_System_Combined.pdf","pg":"pp45-46","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Evaluate","comp":"teach","traps":{"0":"irrelevant","1":"irrelevant","3":"irrelevant"},"safer":{"s":"Find the statement needing more teaching.","a":"Nitrofurantoin; plans to stop if urine turns brown.","f":"Teaching evaluation: expected effect versus warning sign.","e":"Food, even spacing, and reporting allergy signs are correct.","r":"Stopping for brown urine is wrong because it is an expected effect."},"principle":"Teach which changes are expected so clients don't stop therapy.","takeaway":"Brown urine on nitrofurantoin is the drug, not danger.","med":{"why":"Urinary tract infection.","check":"Allergy history.","give":"With food or milk, evenly spaced.","watch":"Rust-brown urine (harmless); fever, chills, rash, dyspnea (allergy).","teach":"Finish the course; report allergy signs."},"xv":2},{"id":"P01","subj":"Psychiatric","topic":"Suicide precautions","cn":"Psychosocial Integrity","cn2":"Safety and Infection Prevention and Control","fmt":"sata","diff":"I","stem":"A client is admitted on suicide precautions. Which actions should the nurse include in the plan of care? Select all that apply.","opts":["Remove belts, shoelaces, and razor blades from the client's belongings.","Check the client about every 15 minutes on an irregular schedule.","Make sure the client swallows all oral medications.","Assign a room at the far end of the hall for privacy.","Check the client at exactly :00, :15, :30, and :45 each hour.","Let the client eat meals alone in the room."],"key":[0,1,2],"rat":"Suicide precautions include removing items that can be used for self-harm, checking about every 15 minutes on an irregular schedule, confirming medications are swallowed, placing the client near the front desk, and attending the client during meals while tracking utensils.","why":{"3":"The room should be close to the front desk or monitored by camera.","4":"A predictable schedule lets the client plan around checks; checks are irregular.","5":"Staff attend meals and account for utensils and dinnerware."},"cues":"","aid":"Make checks unpredictable, like a pop quiz: about every 15 minutes, never on the dot.","src":"Psychiatric_Nursing_Complete_Linear_Study_Packet.pdf","pg":"Teaching slides 19–20 (slide sections pp. 25–164)","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Intervene","comp":"intervention","traps":{"3":"unsafe","4":"unsafe","5":"unsafe"},"safer":{"s":"Select every suicide-precaution action.","a":"Admission on suicide precautions.","f":"Psychiatric safety.","e":"A distant room, predictable checks, and meals alone reduce supervision.","r":"Removing hazards, irregular 15-minute checks, and confirming swallowed meds fit."},"principle":"Precautions work by removing means and keeping supervision unpredictable.","takeaway":"Checks about every 15 minutes, never on the dot.","xv":2},{"id":"P02","subj":"Psychiatric","topic":"Responding to suicidal statements","cn":"Psychosocial Integrity","cn2":"Safety and Infection Prevention and Control","fmt":"mc","diff":"I","stem":"A client with major depression has been giving away prized possessions and says, \"I won't be a burden to anyone much longer.\" What is the nurse's best response?","opts":["\"You have so much to live for.\"","\"Are you thinking about killing yourself? Do you have a plan?\"","\"Let's talk about this tomorrow when you're feeling better.\"","\"Please don't talk like that; it upsets your family.\""],"key":1,"rat":"Giving away possessions and talking about death are warning signs. If the client is suicidal, the nurse asks directly for details about the plan so safety measures can follow.","why":{"0":"False reassurance dismisses the feeling and ends the assessment.","2":"Delaying ignores a warning sign that needs action now.","3":"Discouraging the client from talking closes communication."},"cues":"Giving away possessions + \"won't be a burden much longer.\"","aid":"Ask it plainly: asking about suicide opens the door; it does not plant the idea.","src":"Psychiatric_Nursing_Complete_Linear_Study_Packet.pdf","pg":"pp. 177–193 (Depressive and Bipolar Disorders); slide 19","sameFormExclude":["B30","XE15"],"ask":"Intervene","comp":"cue","traps":{"0":"reassure","2":"delay","3":"irrelevant"},"safer":{"s":"Choose the best response.","a":"Giving away possessions; \"won't be a burden much longer.\"","f":"Suicide risk assessment.","e":"Reassurance, delaying, and shutting down talk miss the warning.","r":"Asking directly about suicide and a plan comes first."},"principle":"Warning signs call for direct assessment now.","takeaway":"Ask plainly about suicide; it opens the door rather than planting the idea.","xv":2},{"id":"P03","subj":"Psychiatric","topic":"Manic phase communication","cn":"Psychosocial Integrity","cn2":"","fmt":"mc","diff":"F","stem":"A client in the manic phase of bipolar disorder argues loudly with the nurse about the unit's quiet-hours rule. Which approach is best?","opts":["Explain the reasons for the rule in detail until the client agrees with it.","State the rule calmly, firmly, and matter-of-factly without arguing.","Let the client choose which unit rules to follow tonight to reduce conflict.","Ignore the client until the arguing stops, then restate the rule."],"key":1,"rat":"Manic clients tend to be argumentative. The nurse remains calm, direct, and firm, states unit rules matter-of-factly, enforces them, and gives concise, simple directions.","why":{"0":"Getting drawn into an argument escalates the client.","2":"Structure and consistent limits are needed during mania.","3":"Ignoring removes needed supervision and structure."},"cues":"Manic + argumentative.","aid":"Mania needs a lighthouse, not a debate partner: calm, firm, brief.","src":"Psychiatric_Nursing_Complete_Linear_Study_Packet.pdf","pg":"pp. 177–193","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Intervene","comp":"intervention","traps":{"0":"unsafe","2":"unsafe","3":"unsafe"},"safer":{"s":"Choose the best approach.","a":"Manic client arguing about rules.","f":"Therapeutic communication in mania.","e":"Debating, giving choices, and ignoring remove structure.","r":"Calm, firm, matter-of-fact limits fit mania."},"principle":"Mania needs structure, not debate.","takeaway":"Be the lighthouse: calm, firm, brief.","xv":2},{"id":"P04","ver":2,"subj":"Psychiatric","topic":"Responding to suspected NMS","cn":"Pharmacological Therapies","cn2":"Physiological Adaptation","fmt":"mc","diff":"A","ask":"Intervene","comp":"med","stem":"A client has received intramuscular haloperidol for 4 days. Today the client's temperature is 103.8°F (39.9°C), pulse is 118, BP has ranged from 168/96 to 132/70 over the past hour, the client is sweating heavily, the limbs are rigid, and the client responds only to a loud voice. Benztropine is ordered PRN for extrapyramidal symptoms. Which action is the priority?","opts":["Give the PRN benztropine for the muscle rigidity","Hold the next antipsychotic dose and report the findings immediately","Apply a cooling blanket and recheck the temperature in 1 hour","Encourage oral fluids and reassess mental status at the end of the shift"],"key":1,"rat":"Neuroleptic malignant syndrome is a potentially fatal reaction appearing 3–9 days into antipsychotic therapy, most often with high-potency IM agents. It combines fever, severe EPS such as lead-pipe rigidity, autonomic instability (tachycardia, labile blood pressure, diaphoresis), and altered consciousness, and symptoms progress over 24–72 hours. Treatment is medical (bromocriptine or amantadine, dantrolene, cooling blankets, hydration, antipyretics), so prompt recognition and reporting are what reduce mortality.","why":{"0":"Benztropine is ordered for EPS such as dystonia or parkinsonian effects; NMS treatment is different and provider-directed.","2":"Cooling is part of treatment, but cooling alone with a 1-hour recheck delays the medical response to a fatal syndrome.","3":"Waiting until the end of the shift lets a rapidly progressing syndrome advance unrecognized."},"traps":{"0":"otherdx","2":"delay","3":"delay"},"cues":"Fever, rigidity, labile BP, diaphoresis, decreasing responsiveness, day 4 of IM antipsychotic.","aid":"NMS = 'FEVER': Fever, Encephalopathy, Vitals unstable, Elevated sweating, Rigidity.","principle":"When findings suggest a drug reaction, stop the suspected drug and escalate; supportive steps follow orders.","takeaway":"Rigidity on an antipsychotic is not always routine EPS. Fever, unstable vitals, and falling LOC change the meaning.","safer":{"s":"Choose the priority action.","a":"Fever 103.8°F, rigidity, labile BP, tachycardia, heavy sweating, falling LOC, day 4 IM haloperidol.","f":"Medication safety: suspected life-threatening adverse reaction.","e":"Benztropine fits another problem; cooling alone and end-of-shift reassessment delay care.","r":"Holding the drug and reporting beats cooling because the syndrome needs medical treatment now."},"compare":{"a":1,"b":2,"aWhy":"It stops further exposure to the likely cause and brings in the provider.","bWhy":"Fever is dangerous, and cooling blankets are part of NMS treatment.","decider":"The full cue cluster points to NMS, which needs provider-directed treatment; cooling alone treats one feature.","flip":"Once the provider is notified and orders cooling, applying the cooling blanket becomes the right next step."},"med":{"why":"Psychosis managed with a high-potency antipsychotic.","check":"Baseline vital signs and blood counts; watch for EPS.","give":"IM as ordered; give PRN benztropine for EPS when ordered.","watch":"NMS (hyperthermia, rigidity, labile BP, confusion), EPS, tardive dyskinesia.","teach":"Take on time, do not stop abruptly, rise slowly, use sunscreen."},"src":"Psychiatric_Nursing_Complete_Linear_Study_Packet.pdf","pg":"pp. 289–306 (NMS); pp. 66–82 (antipsychotic nursing implications)","sameFormExclude":[],"cog":"judgment"},{"id":"P05","subj":"Psychiatric","topic":"MAOI teaching","cn":"Pharmacological Therapies","cn2":"","fmt":"mc","diff":"I","stem":"A client is starting tranylcypromine, a monoamine oxidase inhibitor (MAOI). Which statement indicates a need for further teaching?","opts":["\"I'll limit tyramine-rich foods.\"","\"I'll tell any new prescriber that I take an MAOI.\"","\"I can take an over-the-counter cold medicine if I get sick.\"","\"If I'm ever switched to an SSRI, there must be at least 14 days between them.\""],"key":2,"rat":"Hypertensive crisis is the major MAOI complication. Clients restrict tyramine-rich foods, consult the provider before taking OTC cough or cold medicines, review drug interactions, and allow at least 14 days between stopping an MAOI and starting an SSRI.","why":{"0":"Correct teaching.","1":"Correct teaching.","3":"Correct teaching."},"cues":"OTC cold medicine with an MAOI.","aid":"MAOI = 'Must Ask Our Intern' before anything new, including the cold aisle.","src":"Psychiatric_Nursing_Complete_Linear_Study_Packet.pdf","pg":"pp. 240–256; pp. 66–82","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Evaluate","comp":"teach","traps":{"0":"irrelevant","1":"irrelevant","3":"irrelevant"},"safer":{"s":"Find the statement needing more teaching.","a":"MAOI; plans OTC cold medicine.","f":"Teaching evaluation: drug interactions.","e":"Tyramine limits, telling prescribers, and the 14-day gap are correct.","r":"OTC cold medicine without asking risks hypertensive crisis."},"principle":"In negative stems, look for the unsafe statement.","takeaway":"On an MAOI, ask before anything new, including the cold aisle.","med":{"why":"Depression.","check":"Diet and all other drugs, including OTC.","give":"As prescribed.","watch":"Hypertensive crisis.","teach":"Limit tyramine; ask before OTC cold medicines; 14 days before an SSRI."},"xv":2},{"id":"P06","subj":"Psychiatric","topic":"MAOI–meperidine interaction","cn":"Pharmacological Therapies","cn2":"Safety and Infection Prevention and Control","fmt":"mc","diff":"A","stem":"A client who takes an MAOI for depression is admitted after surgery. Which postoperative prescription requires clarification with the provider?","opts":["Meperidine IM every 4 hours as needed for pain","Acetaminophen PO every 6 hours as needed for mild pain","Incentive spirometer 10 times every hour while awake","Sequential compression devices while in bed"],"key":0,"rat":"With MAOIs, meperidine can cause hyperpyrexia, restlessness, hypertension or hypotension, convulsions, and coma, and the effect may persist weeks after stopping the MAOI. The order must be clarified.","why":{"1":"No MAOI interaction is listed for acetaminophen.","2":"Routine pulmonary care.","3":"Routine prevention measure."},"cues":"MAOI + meperidine.","aid":"MAOI and meperidine: two M's that should never meet.","src":"Psychiatric_Nursing_Complete_Linear_Study_Packet.pdf","pg":"pp. 240–256","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Clarify","comp":"med","traps":{"1":"irrelevant","2":"irrelevant","3":"irrelevant"},"safer":{"s":"Find the order to clarify.","a":"Client on an MAOI; meperidine ordered.","f":"Medication safety: interaction.","e":"Acetaminophen, spirometry, and SCDs are routine.","r":"Meperidine with an MAOI risks a dangerous reaction."},"principle":"Screen every new order against existing high-risk drugs.","takeaway":"MAOI and meperidine: two M's that should never meet.","med":{"why":"Postoperative pain.","check":"Current MAOI therapy, even if recently stopped.","give":"Clarify before giving.","watch":"Hyperpyrexia, BP changes, seizures.","teach":"Tell every provider about the MAOI."},"xv":2},{"id":"P07","subj":"Psychiatric","topic":"SSRI onset and timing","cn":"Pharmacological Therapies","cn2":"","fmt":"mc","diff":"F","stem":"A client who started sertraline 1 week ago says, \"This isn't working. I'm going to stop it.\" What is the nurse's best response?","opts":["\"Go ahead and stop it, and we'll ask your provider for a different one.\"","\"Full effect takes 2 to 4 weeks. Talk with your provider before stopping.\"","\"Double the dose for a few days to help it start working faster.\"","\"Switch to taking it at bedtime so it can work while you sleep.\""],"key":1,"rat":"Antidepressants take 2 to 4 weeks for full benefit. SSRIs are taken in the morning or early afternoon because of insomnia, and the client should consult the provider before changing therapy.","why":{"0":"Stopping early prevents an adequate trial.","2":"Changing the dose without an order is unsafe.","3":"SSRIs can cause insomnia; morning dosing is advised."},"cues":"One week in.","aid":"Antidepressants are a slow sunrise, not a light switch: 2 to 4 weeks.","src":"Psychiatric_Nursing_Complete_Linear_Study_Packet.pdf","pg":"pp. 66–82; pp. 240–256","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Teach","comp":"med","traps":{"0":"unsafe","2":"scope","3":"otherdx"},"safer":{"s":"Choose the best response.","a":"Sertraline started 1 week ago; wants to stop.","f":"Medication teaching: onset.","e":"Stopping, doubling, and bedtime dosing are wrong.","r":"Explaining the 2–4 week onset and consulting the provider fits."},"principle":"Teach the expected onset so clients don't quit early.","takeaway":"Antidepressants are a slow sunrise: 2 to 4 weeks.","med":{"why":"Depression.","check":"Expectations about onset.","give":"Morning or early afternoon.","watch":"Insomnia, mood changes.","teach":"Full effect in 2–4 weeks; talk to the provider before stopping."},"xv":2},{"id":"P08","subj":"Psychiatric","topic":"Crisis intervention","cn":"Psychosocial Integrity","cn2":"","fmt":"mc","diff":"I","stem":"A client whose home burned down yesterday is tearful and says, \"I don't know what to do first.\" Which goal is the nurse's priority?","opts":["The client will gain insight into lifelong coping patterns through long-term therapy.","The client's stress will decrease and the client will mobilize resources.","The client will make all decisions without help to build independence.","The client will return to full-time work by the end of this week."],"key":1,"rat":"Crisis intervention goals are to decrease emotional stress and protect the person, help organize and mobilize resources, and return to the precrisis level or higher. A crisis often resolves in 4 to 6 weeks.","why":{"0":"Crisis intervention is short-term and problem-focused, not long-term insight work.","2":"The nurse actively supports problem solving during the crisis.","3":"This deadline is unrealistic and not a crisis goal."},"cues":"Acute event + overwhelmed.","aid":"Crisis care is first aid for the mind: calm, protect, mobilize.","src":"Psychiatric_Nursing_Complete_Linear_Study_Packet.pdf","pg":"pp. 58–65 (Box 34.6)","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Prioritize","comp":"priority","traps":{"0":"otherdx","2":"unsafe","3":"irrelevant"},"safer":{"s":"Choose the priority goal.","a":"House fire yesterday; overwhelmed.","f":"Crisis intervention.","e":"Long-term insight, full independence now, and a 1-week work deadline don't fit a crisis.","r":"Lowering stress and mobilizing resources are the crisis goals."},"principle":"Crisis care is short-term and problem-focused.","takeaway":"Calm, protect, mobilize.","xv":2},{"id":"P09","subj":"Psychiatric","topic":"Alcohol withdrawal timing","cn":"Physiological Adaptation","cn2":"Safety and Infection Prevention and Control","fmt":"mc","diff":"A","stem":"A client admitted for pneumonia had his last alcoholic drink 18 hours ago. He now has hand tremors, sweating, and a heart rate of 112. For which complication is this client at greatest risk right now?","opts":["Seizures","Wernicke encephalopathy","Korsakoff psychosis","Tardive dyskinesia"],"key":0,"rat":"Withdrawal tremors occur 6 to 48 hours after the last drink, and seizures, usually tonic-clonic without aura, occur 12 to 24 hours after stopping. At 18 hours he is in the seizure window. An acute illness also raises withdrawal risk.","why":{"1":"Wernicke encephalopathy relates to chronic thiamine deficiency, not the withdrawal timeline.","2":"Korsakoff psychosis is a chronic alcohol-related brain disorder.","3":"TD is an antipsychotic adverse effect."},"cues":"18 hours since last drink + tremors.","aid":"Withdrawal clock: shakes start at 6 hours, seizures 12–24 hours, DTs days 1–4.","src":"Psychiatric_Nursing_Complete_Linear_Study_Packet.pdf","pg":"pp. 328–351","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Prioritize","comp":"cue","traps":{"1":"otherdx","2":"otherdx","3":"otherdx"},"safer":{"s":"Identify the greatest current risk.","a":"Last drink 18 hours ago; tremors, sweating, HR 112.","f":"Alcohol withdrawal timeline.","e":"Wernicke, Korsakoff, and TD are chronic or unrelated.","r":"At 18 hours he is in the 12–24 hour seizure window."},"principle":"Timing since the last drink tells you which complication is next.","takeaway":"Shakes at 6 hours, seizures at 12–24 hours.","xv":2},{"id":"P10","subj":"Psychiatric","topic":"Opioid withdrawal","cn":"Psychosocial Integrity","cn2":"Physiological Adaptation","fmt":"sata","diff":"I","stem":"Which findings indicate opioid withdrawal? Select all that apply.","opts":["Yawning","Rhinorrhea","Gooseflesh","Lacrimation","Pinpoint pupils","Respiratory depression"],"key":[0,1,2,3],"rat":"Opioid withdrawal includes restlessness, yawning, lacrimation, diaphoresis, rhinorrhea, abdominal cramping, gooseflesh, tremors, and drug craving.","why":{"4":"Pinpoint pupils occur with opioid intoxication.","5":"Respiratory depression occurs with intoxication."},"cues":"","aid":"Withdrawal is the opposite of the drug: opioids dry and slow you down, withdrawal makes you drip and jitter.","src":"Psychiatric_Nursing_Complete_Linear_Study_Packet.pdf","pg":"Ch. 36 notes, Table 36.2 (pp. 352–414); Pain_Management_and_Urinary p2","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Interpret","comp":"cue","traps":{"4":"otherdx","5":"otherdx"},"safer":{"s":"Select withdrawal findings.","a":"Opioid withdrawal.","f":"Withdrawal versus intoxication.","e":"Pinpoint pupils and respiratory depression are intoxication.","r":"Yawning, rhinorrhea, gooseflesh, and tearing are withdrawal."},"principle":"Withdrawal looks like the opposite of the drug's effects.","takeaway":"Opioids dry and slow; withdrawal drips and jitters.","xv":2},{"id":"C01","subj":"Cardiovascular","topic":"Nitroglycerin teaching","cn":"Pharmacological Therapies","cn2":"Health Promotion and Maintenance","fmt":"mc","diff":"I","stem":"The nurse reinforces teaching with a client newly prescribed sublingual nitroglycerin for angina. Which statement indicates a need for further teaching?","opts":["\"A burning feeling under my tongue means the tablet is working.\"","\"I'll sit or stand up slowly after I take a tablet so I don't get dizzy.\"","\"I'll keep my tablets in a clear pill box in my car.\"","\"I won't take sildenafil or similar drugs while I'm using nitroglycerin.\""],"key":2,"rat":"Nitroglycerin is stored in a dark bottle in a dry place, always carried on the person, and replaced every 6 months or by its expiration date. Burning on the tongue shows it is active, postural hypotension is a side effect, and PDE5 inhibitors with nitrates can cause severe hypotension.","why":{"0":"Correct: shows the tablet is active.","1":"Correct: prevents falls from postural hypotension.","3":"Correct: the combination can cause severe hypotension."},"cues":"Clear container + car storage.","aid":"Nitro is a vampire: keep it in the dark, keep it dry, keep it with you.","src":"Cardiovascular_Complete_Combined_Updated.pdf","pg":"Ch. 48, Patient Teaching: Angina Pectoris (no page numbers in file)","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Evaluate","comp":"teach","traps":{"0":"irrelevant","1":"irrelevant","3":"irrelevant"},"safer":{"s":"Find the statement needing more teaching.","a":"Clear pill box in the glove compartment.","f":"Teaching evaluation: storage.","e":"Burning, rising slowly, and avoiding sildenafil are correct.","r":"Clear container in a car fails the dark, dry, on-your-person rule."},"principle":"In negative stems, look for the one unsafe statement.","takeaway":"Nitro: dark bottle, dry place, on your person.","med":{"why":"Acute angina.","check":"BP before use; no PDE5 inhibitors.","give":"Under the tongue; repeat every 5 minutes up to 3.","watch":"Burning (active), headache, postural hypotension.","teach":"Dark, dry, carried; replace every 6 months."},"xv":2},{"id":"C02","ver":2,"subj":"Cardiovascular","topic":"Nitroglycerin: the next dose","cn":"Pharmacological Therapies","cn2":"Physiological Adaptation","fmt":"mc","diff":"A","ask":"Intervene","comp":"med","stem":"A client with stable angina develops chest pain rated 7/10 while walking in the hall. The nurse has the client sit down and gives one sublingual nitroglycerin tablet as ordered. Five minutes later the pain is 5/10. BP is 112/70 (126/78 before the dose) and pulse is 92. Which action should the nurse take next?","opts":["Notify the provider that the first tablet did not relieve the pain","Hold further nitroglycerin because the blood pressure has dropped","Recheck BP and pulse and give a second tablet","Walk the client back to bed so the client can rest"],"key":2,"rat":"For angina, the nurse gives prescribed nitroglycerin and repeats it every 5 minutes, up to three times, monitoring BP and pulse before and after each dose. The provider is notified if pain is unrelieved after the third tablet. Activity stops at the first sign of chest pain.","why":{"0":"Notification comes if pain is unrelieved after the third tablet; one dose has been given and pain is easing.","1":"A small drop to 112/70 is still adequate, and BP monitoring before each dose is exactly what allows the next tablet.","3":"Walking increases cardiac workload; activity stops when chest pain is present."},"traps":{"0":"later","1":"isolated","3":"unsafe"},"cues":"One tablet given, pain still 5/10, BP adequate.","aid":"Nitro rule of 3s: 3 tablets, 5 minutes apart, check BP each time, then call.","principle":"Follow the protocol sequence; escalation belongs after the protocol has run its course unless the client is unstable.","takeaway":"Unrelieved after one tablet is not the same as unrelieved after three. Know where you are in the sequence.","safer":{"s":"Choose the next step in an angina episode.","a":"Pain improving but present, one tablet given, BP 112/70, pulse 92.","f":"Medication protocol: WHY ischemic pain, CHECK BP and pulse, GIVE every 5 minutes up to 3, WATCH relief and hypotension.","e":"Calling now is early, holding reacts to one acceptable number, and walking adds workload.","r":"A second tablet must come before notification because the protocol allows three doses first."},"compare":{"a":2,"b":0,"aWhy":"The protocol allows a second tablet 5 minutes after the first when pain continues and BP is adequate.","bWhy":"Notifying the provider about chest pain is always a reasonable instinct.","decider":"Only one of three permitted doses has been given, and the BP supports another dose.","flip":"If pain persisted after the third tablet, or BP were too low to give another dose, notification would come first."},"med":{"why":"Myocardial ischemia causing chest pain.","check":"BP and pulse before and after each tablet.","give":"Sublingual, every 5 minutes, up to three tablets, with the client resting.","watch":"Pain relief, postural hypotension, throbbing headache, flushing.","teach":"Rise slowly, call for help or the ED if pain persists after the third tablet, keep tablets in a dark container."},"src":"Cardiovascular_Complete_Combined_Updated.pdf","pg":"Ch. 48, Angina patient problems and interventions; Patient Teaching: Angina Pectoris (no page numbers in file)","sameFormExclude":[],"cog":"judgment"},{"id":"C03","subj":"Cardiovascular","topic":"Subcutaneous heparin technique","cn":"Pharmacological Therapies","cn2":"Safety and Infection Prevention and Control","fmt":"mc","diff":"I","stem":"The nurse is giving subcutaneous heparin. Which action is correct?","opts":["Aspirate before injecting to make sure the needle is not in a vessel.","Massage the site gently afterward to help the drug absorb.","Inject into abdominal tissue 2 inches from the umbilicus, without aspirating.","Inject into an existing bruise so new bruising is less noticeable."],"key":2,"rat":"Subcutaneous heparin goes into abdominal tissue, not within 2 inches of the umbilicus, usually at 90 degrees. Do not aspirate or massage, rotate sites, avoid hematomas, and apply gentle pressure for 1–2 minutes.","why":{"0":"Aspirating increases tissue damage and hematoma risk.","1":"Massaging promotes hematoma formation.","3":"Never inject into a hematoma."},"cues":"","aid":"Heparin is shy: no aspirating, no rubbing, and keep 2 inches from the belly button.","src":"Cardiovascular_Complete_Combined_Updated.pdf","pg":"Heparin: additional nursing implications","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Intervene","comp":"med","traps":{"0":"unsafe","1":"unsafe","3":"unsafe"},"safer":{"s":"Choose the correct technique.","a":"Subcutaneous heparin.","f":"Medication administration technique.","e":"Aspirating, massaging, and injecting a bruise cause hematomas.","r":"Abdomen, 2 inches from the umbilicus, no aspiration."},"principle":"Anticoagulant injections are about preventing hematomas.","takeaway":"Heparin: no aspirating, no rubbing, 2 inches from the navel.","med":{"why":"Clot prevention.","check":"Bleeding signs; site free of bruising.","give":"Abdomen, at least 2 inches from the umbilicus; no aspiration or massage; rotate sites.","watch":"Bleeding and hematomas.","teach":"Report unusual bleeding."},"xv":2},{"id":"C04","subj":"Cardiovascular","topic":"Heparin antidote","cn":"Pharmacological Therapies","cn2":"Reduction of Risk Potential","fmt":"mc","diff":"F","stem":"A client receiving a continuous heparin infusion develops bleeding gums and hematuria, and the aPTT is well above the therapeutic range. Which antidote does the nurse anticipate?","opts":["Vitamin K","Protamine sulfate","Naloxone","Calcium gluconate"],"key":1,"rat":"Protamine sulfate is the heparin antagonist; 1 mg neutralizes about 100 units of heparin. Vitamin K reverses warfarin.","why":{"0":"Vitamin K is the warfarin antagonist.","2":"Naloxone reverses opioids.","3":"Calcium gluconate is the magnesium sulfate antidote."},"cues":"Heparin + bleeding.","aid":"Protamine for heParin (P with P); vitamin K for warfarin.","src":"Cardiovascular_Complete_Combined_Updated.pdf","pg":"Heparin: antidote; Med_Surg pp. 126–278 (PE management)","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Interpret","comp":"med","traps":{"0":"otherdx","2":"otherdx","3":"otherdx"},"safer":{"s":"Anticipate the antidote.","a":"Heparin infusion, bleeding gums, hematuria, aPTT high.","f":"Antidote matching.","e":"Vitamin K, naloxone, and calcium gluconate reverse other drugs.","r":"Protamine sulfate reverses heparin."},"principle":"Know each high-alert drug's antidote.","takeaway":"Protamine for heParin; vitamin K for warfarin.","xv":2},{"id":"C05","subj":"Cardiovascular","topic":"Warfarin teaching","cn":"Reduction of Risk Potential","cn2":"Pharmacological Therapies","fmt":"mc","diff":"I","stem":"Which statement by a client starting warfarin indicates understanding of the teaching?","opts":["\"I'll keep the amount of vitamin K foods I eat about the same each day.\"","\"I can eat as many leafy greens as I want to stay healthy.\"","\"Bleeding gums are normal on this drug, so I won't bother reporting them.\"","\"I'll only need one blood test to check my dose.\""],"key":0,"rat":"Vitamin K–rich foods are kept consistently limited because changes make levels fluctuate. PT/INR is monitored frequently, and bleeding from the gums, nose, or anywhere else is reported immediately.","why":{"1":"Large swings in vitamin K intake make warfarin levels fluctuate.","2":"Gum bleeding must be reported immediately.","3":"PT/INR is checked frequently during therapy."},"cues":"","aid":"Warfarin likes routine: same vitamin K, every day.","src":"Cardiovascular_Complete_Combined_Updated.pdf","pg":"Table 48.1; angina teaching (anticoagulants)","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Evaluate","comp":"teach","traps":{"1":"unsafe","2":"unsafe","3":"unsafe"},"safer":{"s":"Find the statement showing understanding.","a":"Warfarin teaching.","f":"Teaching evaluation.","e":"Unlimited greens, ignoring bleeding, one blood test are wrong.","r":"Consistent vitamin K intake keeps levels steady."},"principle":"Consistency, not avoidance, is the vitamin K rule.","takeaway":"Warfarin likes routine: same vitamin K every day.","med":{"why":"Clot prevention.","check":"PT/INR, bleeding.","give":"As prescribed, same time daily.","watch":"Bleeding from gums, nose, urine, stool.","teach":"Keep vitamin K steady; frequent PT/INR checks; report bleeding."},"xv":2},{"id":"C06","subj":"Cardiovascular","topic":"Arterial vs venous disorder","cn":"Physiological Adaptation","cn2":"","fmt":"mc","diff":"I","stem":"A client reports calf pain that comes on while walking and eases with rest. Which additional finding supports peripheral arterial disease rather than venous insufficiency?","opts":["Leg pain that is relieved by elevating the legs on pillows","Ankle edema that worsens toward the end of the day","Diminished pedal pulses with cool, shiny, hairless skin","Darkened skin pigmentation near the ankle with a stasis ulcer"],"key":2,"rat":"Arterial disorders cause pain with exercise relieved by rest, diminished or absent pulses, and cool, dry, shiny, hairless skin. Venous disorders cause pain relieved by activity or elevation, edema, warm thickened skin, darkened pigmentation, and stasis ulcers.","why":{"0":"Relief with elevation is venous.","1":"Edema worse at day's end is venous.","3":"Pigmentation and stasis ulcers are venous."},"cues":"Claudication pattern.","aid":"Arteries carry blood DOWN to the feet; when they fail, feet go cold, pale, pulseless. Veins carry blood UP; when they fail, blood pools and swells.","src":"Cardiovascular_Complete_Combined_Updated.pdf","pg":"Table 48.9; peripheral arterial disease section","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Interpret","comp":"analysis","traps":{"0":"otherdx","1":"otherdx","3":"otherdx"},"safer":{"s":"Pick the finding that supports arterial disease.","a":"Pain with walking, relieved by rest.","f":"Arterial versus venous comparison.","e":"Elevation relief, edema, and stasis ulcers are venous.","r":"Weak pulses and cool, shiny, hairless skin are arterial."},"principle":"Build comparison tables for look-alike disorders.","takeaway":"Arteries fail: cold, pale, pulseless. Veins fail: warm, swollen, pigmented.","xv":2},{"id":"C07","subj":"Cardiovascular","topic":"Beta blocker in diabetes","cn":"Pharmacological Therapies","cn2":"Reduction of Risk Potential","fmt":"mc","diff":"I","stem":"A client with type 2 diabetes and angina is started on metoprolol. Which instruction is most important for the nurse to reinforce?","opts":["\"Check your blood glucose often; this drug can hide low-sugar signs.\"","\"Stop the medication if you feel tired or sluggish during the first week.\"","\"Take it only on the days when you notice any chest pain.\"","\"Double the dose before you exercise so you don't get chest pain.\""],"key":0,"rat":"Beta blockers mask hypoglycemic symptoms, so clients with diabetes check glucose regularly. They are taken every day as prescribed and are not stopped abruptly, which can worsen angina.","why":{"1":"Abrupt discontinuation can worsen angina.","2":"Metoprolol is taken daily, not as needed.","3":"Dose changes require an order; nitroglycerin, not metoprolol, is taken before exertion."},"cues":"Diabetes + beta blocker.","aid":"Beta blockers put a blindfold on hypoglycemia's warning signs, so check the meter.","src":"Cardiovascular_Complete_Combined_Updated.pdf","pg":"Ch. 48, angina medication management teaching","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Teach","comp":"med","traps":{"1":"unsafe","2":"unsafe","3":"scope"},"safer":{"s":"Choose the most important instruction.","a":"Diabetes plus a new beta blocker.","f":"Medication teaching: interaction with a condition.","e":"Stopping, PRN use, and doubling are unsafe.","r":"Glucose checks matter most because hypoglycemia signs are masked."},"principle":"Teach the risk created by the drug in this client's condition.","takeaway":"Beta blockers blindfold hypoglycemia: check the meter.","med":{"why":"Angina.","check":"Apical pulse and BP; glucose in diabetes.","give":"Daily as prescribed; don't stop abruptly.","watch":"Bradycardia, hypotension, masked hypoglycemia.","teach":"Check glucose regularly; never stop suddenly."},"xv":2},{"id":"C08","subj":"Cardiovascular","topic":"Antiembolism stockings","cn":"Health Promotion and Maintenance","cn2":"Reduction of Risk Potential","fmt":"mc","diff":"F","stem":"A client recovering from a deep vein thrombosis is prescribed antiembolism (TED) stockings. When should the client put them on?","opts":["In the evening, after showering and drying the legs","In the morning before getting out of bed","Only on days when the legs feel swollen or heavy","After walking for 30 minutes to warm up the legs"],"key":1,"rat":"TED hose are applied in the morning before getting out of bed. Related teaching includes changing positions, avoiding crossed legs and prolonged standing, and wearing nonrestrictive clothing.","why":{"0":"By evening, venous pooling has already occurred.","2":"Waiting for swelling misses the point of prevention.","3":"Walking first lets veins fill and legs swell."},"cues":"","aid":"Stockings go on before the feet hit the floor, while the legs are still 'empty.'","src":"Med_Surg_All_Sources_Linear_Master_2026.pdf","pg":"Respiratory readings pp. 126–278 (pulmonary embolism teaching)","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Teach","comp":"teach","traps":{"0":"later","2":"later","3":"later"},"safer":{"s":"Choose the correct timing.","a":"TED stockings after DVT.","f":"Teaching: timing of a preventive measure.","e":"Evening, when swollen, and after walking are all too late.","r":"Before getting out of bed, legs are least swollen."},"principle":"Preventive measures go on before the problem starts each day.","takeaway":"Stockings on before feet hit the floor.","xv":2},{"id":"M01","subj":"Maternal-Newborn","topic":"Prolapsed umbilical cord","cn":"Physiological Adaptation","cn2":"Safety and Infection Prevention and Control","fmt":"mc","diff":"A","stem":"After her membranes rupture, a laboring client says she feels something in her vagina. The nurse sees the umbilical cord at the introitus. What should the nurse do first?","opts":["Ask the client to push with her next contraction to speed the delivery.","With a sterile gloved hand, lift the presenting part off the cord; call for help.","Raise the head of the bed to high Fowler's position to ease breathing.","Document the time and continue monitoring the fetal heart rate closely."],"key":1,"rat":"Cord compression for more than 5 minutes usually causes CNS damage or fetal death. The examiner relieves pressure by holding the presenting part off the cord with a sterile gloved hand, and the client is positioned in modified Sims, Trendelenburg, or knee-chest so gravity keeps pressure off the cord.","why":{"0":"Pushing drives the presenting part down onto the cord.","2":"Head-up positions let gravity press the fetus onto the cord.","3":"Monitoring without relieving compression wastes critical minutes."},"cues":"Visible cord after rupture of membranes.","aid":"Cord out? Lift the baby UP off it, and tip mom so gravity works for you.","src":"Maternal_Health_Linear_Chapter_Sources_Updated.pdf","pg":"pp. 91–131 (Ch. 27 Labor and Delivery, Prolapsed Umbilical Cord)","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Intervene","comp":"intervention","traps":{"0":"unsafe","2":"unsafe","3":"delay"},"safer":{"s":"Choose the first action.","a":"Membranes ruptured; cord visible.","f":"Obstetric emergency.","e":"Pushing and head-up positions compress the cord; monitoring delays.","r":"Lifting the presenting part off the cord comes first."},"principle":"Relieve the compression before anything else.","takeaway":"Cord out: lift the baby off it and tip mom so gravity helps.","xv":2},{"id":"M02","ver":2,"subj":"Maternal-Newborn","topic":"Excessive contractions on oxytocin","cn":"Reduction of Risk Potential","cn2":"Pharmacological Therapies","fmt":"mc","diff":"A","ask":"Intervene","comp":"intervention","exhibit":"<table><tr><th>Time</th><th>Contractions</th><th>Duration</th><th>FHR baseline</th><th>Pattern</th></tr><tr><td>1300</td><td>Every 3 min</td><td>60 sec</td><td>140</td><td>Accelerations with movement</td></tr><tr><td>1330</td><td>Every 2 min</td><td>80 sec</td><td>138</td><td>No decelerations</td></tr><tr><td>1400</td><td>Every 1.5 min</td><td>100 sec</td><td>128</td><td>Late decelerations with each contraction</td></tr></table>","stem":"A client at term is receiving an oxytocin infusion for induction. The nurse reviews the monitor trend shown. Which set of actions should the nurse take?","opts":["Keep the oxytocin running, turn her to her left side, give oxygen, and recheck in 15 minutes","Stop the oxytocin per policy, place her flat on her back, give oxygen, and notify the provider","Stop the oxytocin per policy, turn her to her left side, give oxygen, and notify the provider","Coach her to bear down with the next contraction and recheck the strip in 30 minutes"],"key":2,"rat":"Contractions longer than 90 seconds require the oxytocin rate to be slowed or stopped, and late decelerations signal decreased placental blood flow. The obstetric medication safety alert directs the nurse to reduce the oxytocin to the slowest rate per policy (Box 27.2 says stop), turn the mother to the left lateral position, give oxygen, and call the provider immediately.","why":{"0":"Left side and oxygen help, but leaving the oxytocin running continues the overstimulation causing the problem.","1":"Lying flat compresses the vena cava and reduces placental perfusion.","3":"Bearing down is not indicated, and waiting 30 minutes delays the response to fetal distress."},"traps":{"0":"delay","1":"unsafe","3":"delay"},"cues":"Contractions every 1.5 min lasting 100 sec, new late decelerations, falling baseline.","aid":"Oxytocin trouble? Stop the squeeze, tip her left, oxygen on, call it in.","principle":"Treat the mechanism, not just the symptom: remove the stimulus that is causing fetal distress.","takeaway":"When a drug causes the problem, the drug is the first thing you change. Supportive steps alone leave the cause running.","safer":{"s":"Choose the complete correct response.","a":"Contractions every 1.5 min lasting 100 sec, late decelerations, baseline drifting down on oxytocin.","f":"Obstetric medication safety: excessive uterine stimulation with fetal distress.","e":"Continuing oxytocin, supine positioning, and delayed rechecks are unsafe.","r":"Stopping the infusion with left-side positioning beats supportive care alone because it removes the cause."},"compare":{"a":2,"b":0,"aWhy":"It removes the oxytocin stimulus and adds left-side positioning, oxygen, and notification.","bWhy":"Left-side positioning and oxygen are also correct intrauterine resuscitation steps.","decider":"Contractions over 90 seconds and late decelerations mean the oxytocin itself must be slowed or stopped.","flip":"Without oxytocin running and with normal contraction length, there would be no infusion to stop, so care would center on positioning, oxygen, and notification."},"med":{"why":"Induction of labor at term.","check":"Baseline vital signs, I&O, contraction frequency and duration, fetal heart rate.","give":"IV on an infusion pump, increased no more often than every 15–30 minutes.","watch":"Contractions over 90 seconds, fetal distress, never leave the client unattended.","teach":"Report pain changes or leaking fluid; explain continuous monitoring."},"src":"Maternal_Health_Linear_Chapter_Sources_Updated.pdf","pg":"pp. 91–143 (Box 27.2; fetal distress); pp. 331–381 (Ch. 39 oxytocin safety alert)","sameFormExclude":[],"cog":"judgment"},{"id":"M03","subj":"Maternal-Newborn","topic":"Nonstress test interpretation","cn":"Health Promotion and Maintenance","cn2":"Reduction of Risk Potential","fmt":"mc","diff":"I","stem":"A client at 34 weeks has a nonstress test. Over 20 minutes, the fetal heart rate rises 20 bpm for 15–20 seconds with each of three fetal movements. How does the nurse interpret this?","opts":["Reactive, indicating a healthy fetus","Nonreactive, needing further evaluation","Positive, indicating placental insufficiency","Unsatisfactory, needing a repeat test"],"key":0,"rat":"A reactive NST shows at least two fetal movements with two increases of 15 bpm lasting at least 15 seconds in 20 minutes. It indicates a healthy fetus.","why":{"1":"Nonreactive means the FHR does not rise with movement.","2":"\"Positive\" describes a contraction stress test with late decelerations.","3":"\"Unsatisfactory\" describes a CST without adequate contractions."},"cues":"Accelerations with movement.","aid":"Reactive is Reassuring: the baby 'reacts' to moving with a heart-rate pickup, like a kid excited to play.","src":"Maternal_Health_Linear_Chapter_Sources_Updated.pdf","pg":"pp. 17–50 (Ch. 26, Nonstress Test)","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Interpret","comp":"analysis","traps":{"1":"otherdx","2":"otherdx","3":"otherdx"},"safer":{"s":"Interpret the NST.","a":"Three accelerations of 20 bpm for 15–20 seconds in 20 minutes.","f":"Fetal testing criteria.","e":"Nonreactive, positive, and unsatisfactory describe other results or tests.","r":"Meets the reactive criteria."},"principle":"Apply the exact criteria, and know which terms belong to which test.","takeaway":"Reactive is reassuring.","xv":2},{"id":"M04","subj":"Maternal-Newborn","topic":"Nägele's rule","cn":"Health Promotion and Maintenance","cn2":"","fmt":"mc","diff":"F","stem":"A client's last normal menstrual period began on September 10. Using Nägele's rule, what is her estimated date of delivery?","opts":["June 3","June 17","July 17","December 17"],"key":1,"rat":"Nägele's rule: from the first day of the last normal menstrual period, count back 3 months and add 7 days. September 10 → June 10 → June 17.","why":{"0":"Subtracts 7 days instead of adding.","2":"Counts back 2 months instead of 3.","3":"Adds 3 months instead of counting back."},"cues":"","aid":"Back 3, add 7: 'minus three, plus seven' like a countdown with a bonus week.","src":"Maternal_Health_Linear_Chapter_Sources_Updated.pdf","pg":"pp. 5–16 (Ch. 26, due date calculation)","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Calculate","comp":"analysis","traps":{},"safer":{"s":"Calculate the due date.","a":"LMP September 10.","f":"Nägele's rule.","e":"Wrong directions or month counts.","r":"Back 3 months, add 7 days: June 17."},"principle":"Write each step of a rule-based calculation.","takeaway":"Minus three months, plus seven days.","xv":2},{"id":"M05","subj":"Maternal-Newborn","topic":"GTPAL","cn":"Health Promotion and Maintenance","cn2":"","fmt":"mc","diff":"I","stem":"A pregnant client's history: one baby born at 39 weeks (living), one miscarriage at 10 weeks, and twins born at 34 weeks (both living). How does the nurse record her GTPAL?","opts":["G4 T1 P1 A1 L3","G5 T1 P2 A1 L3","G3 T1 P1 A1 L3","G4 T2 P1 A1 L3"],"key":0,"rat":"Gravida counts all pregnancies including the current one (4). Term is delivery after 37 weeks (1). Preterm is 20 through 37 weeks; twins count as one event (1). Abortion is loss before 20 weeks (1). Living children (3).","why":{"1":"Counts the twins as two pregnancies and two preterm births.","2":"Leaves out the current pregnancy.","3":"Counts the 34-week twins as a term birth."},"cues":"Twins = one pregnancy, one birth event; living children counted individually.","aid":"Twins share one 'ticket' for G, T, P, and A, but each gets a seat in L.","src":"Maternal_Health_Linear_Chapter_Sources_Updated.pdf","pg":"pp. 5–16 (Box 26.5)","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Calculate","comp":"analysis","traps":{},"safer":{"s":"Record GTPAL.","a":"Term birth, miscarriage, twins at 34 weeks, current pregnancy.","f":"Obstetric history notation.","e":"Twins counted twice, current pregnancy omitted, preterm counted as term.","r":"G4 T1 P1 A1 L3."},"principle":"Count events, then people.","takeaway":"Twins share one ticket for G, T, P, A; each gets a seat in L.","xv":2},{"id":"M06","subj":"Maternal-Newborn","topic":"Postpartum bladder distention","cn":"Basic Care and Comfort","cn2":"Reduction of Risk Potential","fmt":"mc","diff":"I","stem":"Three hours after a vaginal birth, the nurse finds the fundus firm, 2 cm above the umbilicus, and displaced to the right. Lochia is moderate rubra. The client last voided 5 hours ago. What should the nurse do?","opts":["Massage the fundus firmly for several minutes.","Help the client to the bathroom to void.","Notify the provider of a postpartum hemorrhage.","Document these as expected findings."],"key":1,"rat":"Decreased urge to void and postpartum diuresis lead to bladder distention, which keeps the uterus from contracting and can cause excessive bleeding. The fundus is palpated gently only until firm, because overstimulation fatigues the muscle.","why":{"0":"The fundus is already firm; vigorous massage causes atony.","2":"Moderate lochia with a firm fundus does not indicate hemorrhage.","3":"A high, displaced fundus after 5 hours without voiding is not expected."},"cues":"High and to the right + long time since voiding.","aid":"Fundus off to the side? The bladder is pushing it aside like a full backpack: empty the backpack.","src":"Maternal_Health_Linear_Chapter_Sources_Updated.pdf","pg":"pp. 145–156 (Ch. 28); pp. 91–131 (placental delivery nurse's role)","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Intervene","comp":"intervention","traps":{"0":"otherdx","2":"otherdx","3":"reassure"},"safer":{"s":"Choose the action.","a":"Firm fundus, 2 cm above umbilicus, deviated right, 5 hours since voiding.","f":"Postpartum assessment: bladder distention.","e":"Massage fits a boggy fundus, hemorrhage doesn't fit moderate lochia, calling it expected misses the cause.","r":"Voiding fixes the displaced fundus."},"principle":"Find the cause of the abnormal finding before treating the finding.","takeaway":"A fundus pushed to the side means a full bladder.","compare":{"a":1,"b":0,"aWhy":"Emptying the bladder lets the uterus return to position and contract.","bWhy":"Fundal massage is a standard postpartum intervention.","decider":"The fundus is already firm; the displacement comes from the bladder.","flip":"If the fundus were boggy, gentle massage until firm would come first."},"xv":2},{"id":"M07","subj":"Maternal-Newborn","topic":"Rho(D) immune globulin","cn":"Pharmacological Therapies","cn2":"","fmt":"mc","diff":"F","stem":"Which postpartum client should receive Rho(D) immune globulin (RhoGAM)?","opts":["Rh-positive mother of an Rh-negative newborn","Rh-negative mother of an Rh-negative newborn","Rh-negative mother, not sensitized, of an Rh-positive newborn","Rh-negative mother already sensitized, of an Rh-positive newborn"],"key":2,"rat":"RhoGAM is given within 72 hours of delivery to mothers who are Rh negative, not sensitized, and gave birth to an Rh-positive infant.","why":{"0":"An Rh-positive mother cannot form anti-Rh antibodies.","1":"No Rh-positive fetal blood exposure occurs.","3":"Once sensitized, RhoGAM cannot prevent isoimmunization."},"cues":"","aid":"RhoGAM rule: Negative mom, Positive baby, Not yet sensitized, within 72 hours.","src":"Maternal_Health_Linear_Chapter_Sources_Updated.pdf","pg":"pp. 145–156 (Ch. 28, immune system changes)","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Interpret","comp":"med","traps":{"0":"otherdx","1":"otherdx","3":"otherdx"},"safer":{"s":"Identify the client who needs RhoGAM.","a":"Mother and baby Rh status, sensitization.","f":"RhoGAM criteria.","e":"Rh-positive mothers, Rh-negative babies, and sensitized mothers don't qualify.","r":"Rh-negative, unsensitized mother of an Rh-positive baby."},"principle":"Check every criterion, not just one.","takeaway":"Negative mom, positive baby, not yet sensitized, within 72 hours.","xv":2},{"id":"K01","ver":2,"subj":"Pediatrics","topic":"Suspected epiglottitis","cn":"Physiological Adaptation","cn2":"Safety and Infection Prevention and Control","fmt":"mc","diff":"A","ask":"Intervene","comp":"intervention","stem":"A 4-year-old arrives with a sudden high fever. The child is sitting upright, drooling, and speaking in a muffled voice, and is anxious. The provider is on the way. The parent asks the nurse to look at the child's throat to see how bad it is. Which action is most appropriate?","opts":["Gently depress the tongue to look at the throat before the provider arrives","Obtain a throat culture now so IV antibiotics can be started sooner","Keep the child upright with the parent, defer any throat exam, ready airway equipment","Give the ordered sedative so the child is calm for the provider's exam"],"key":2,"rat":"A child with epiglottitis is acutely ill with high fever, muffled voice, drooling, progressive distress, anxiety, and fear; it is a medical emergency that can cause total airway obstruction. The pharynx is examined only where equipment and personnel for immediate intubation or tracheostomy are ready, cultures are taken from tracheal secretions at intubation, and sedatives are contraindicated because they mask restlessness, a sign of hypoxia.","why":{"0":"Throat examination outside a prepared setting can trigger total airway obstruction.","1":"Cultures are collected at intubation; manipulating the throat now risks obstruction.","3":"Sedatives mask restlessness, a key sign of worsening hypoxia."},"traps":{"0":"unsafe","1":"later","3":"unsafe"},"cues":"Drooling, muffled voice, high fever, upright posture, sudden onset.","aid":"Epiglottitis: hands off the throat, eyes on the airway, tube at the bedside.","principle":"When an airway can close, avoid any action that provokes it and prepare to secure it.","takeaway":"A helpful-sounding step (culture, calm the child) is wrong if it threatens the airway or hides deterioration.","safer":{"s":"Choose the safest action while waiting for the provider.","a":"High fever, drooling, muffled voice, upright posture, anxiety.","f":"Airway emergency in a child.","e":"Throat exam and culture provoke obstruction; sedation hides hypoxia.","r":"Upright positioning with airway equipment ready beats starting antibiotics sooner because the airway threat comes first."},"compare":{"a":2,"b":1,"aWhy":"It protects the airway and prepares for obstruction.","bWhy":"Epiglottitis is a bacterial infection, so early antibiotics sound helpful.","decider":"Any throat manipulation can cause total obstruction, and cultures are taken once the airway is secured.","flip":"After intubation, tracheal secretions are collected for culture and IV antibiotics follow."},"src":"PEDS_Complete_Linear_Word_Sources_2026.pdf","pg":"pp. 154–210 (Croup and acute epiglottitis)","sameFormExclude":[],"cog":"judgment"},{"id":"K02","subj":"Pediatrics","topic":"Infant nutrition","cn":"Health Promotion and Maintenance","cn2":"","fmt":"mc","diff":"F","stem":"The nurse reinforces feeding teaching with the parent of a 6-month-old. Which statement indicates a need for further teaching?","opts":["\"I'll start with iron-fortified cereal.\"","\"I'll wait until after her first birthday to give cow's milk.\"","\"I'll sweeten her cereal with a little honey.\"","\"If I stop breastfeeding, I'll use iron-fortified formula.\""],"key":2,"rat":"Honey is avoided before age 1 because of botulism risk. Solids start at 4–6 months with iron-fortified cereal, cow's milk waits until 12 months, and iron-fortified formula is used if not breastfeeding.","why":{"0":"Correct teaching.","1":"Correct teaching.","3":"Correct teaching."},"cues":"Honey under age 1.","aid":"No honey till one: 'bees after birthday.'","src":"Pediatric_Nursing_Complete_Linear_Study_Packet.pdf","pg":"Nutrition slide (no page numbers in file)","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Evaluate","comp":"teach","traps":{"0":"irrelevant","1":"irrelevant","3":"irrelevant"},"safer":{"s":"Find the statement needing more teaching.","a":"Honey for a 6-month-old.","f":"Teaching evaluation: infant nutrition.","e":"Iron cereal, no cow's milk until 1, iron formula are correct.","r":"Honey under 1 risks botulism."},"principle":"Negative stems hide one unsafe statement among true ones.","takeaway":"No honey till one.","xv":2},{"id":"K03","subj":"Pediatrics","topic":"Immunization schedule","cn":"Health Promotion and Maintenance","cn2":"","fmt":"mc","diff":"F","stem":"A healthy child is at the 12-month well visit and is up to date. Which vaccines does the nurse expect the child to receive for the first time at this visit?","opts":["Hepatitis B and rotavirus","MMR and varicella","DTaP and IPV","Hib and rotavirus"],"key":1,"rat":"The schedule in your packet gives the live vaccines MMR and varicella at 12 months, along with HepA, Hib, and PCV. Hepatitis B starts at birth, and DTaP, IPV, Hib, PCV, and rotavirus start at 2 months.","why":{"0":"HepB starts at birth and rotavirus at 2 months.","2":"DTaP and IPV start at 2 months.","3":"Hib and rotavirus start at 2 months."},"cues":"\"For the first time\" at 12 months.","aid":"First birthday = first live vaccines: MMR and varicella.","src":"Pediatric_Nursing_Complete_Linear_Study_Packet.pdf","pg":"Immunizations table (no page numbers in file)","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Interpret","comp":"analysis","traps":{"0":"otherdx","2":"otherdx","3":"otherdx"},"safer":{"s":"Identify first-time vaccines at 12 months.","a":"\"For the first time\" at 12 months.","f":"Immunization schedule.","e":"HepB, rotavirus, DTaP, IPV, Hib start earlier.","r":"MMR and varicella start at 12 months."},"principle":"Read the qualifier: first time, not just given.","takeaway":"First birthday, first live vaccines.","xv":2},{"id":"K04","subj":"Pediatrics","topic":"SIDS prevention","cn":"Safety and Infection Prevention and Control","cn2":"Health Promotion and Maintenance","fmt":"mc","diff":"F","stem":"The nurse reviews safe sleep with the parent of a 2-month-old. Which statement indicates a need for further teaching?","opts":["\"I'll lay her on her back to sleep, even for naps.\"","\"I'll offer a pacifier at nap time and bedtime.\"","\"I'll put a soft bumper and quilt in her crib.\"","\"I'll keep her from getting overheated with too many layers.\""],"key":2,"rat":"SIDS prevention includes supine sleep, no soft bedding, no co-sleeping, pacifier use during naps and bedtime, and avoiding overheating.","why":{"0":"Correct teaching.","1":"Correct teaching.","3":"Correct teaching."},"cues":"Soft bedding.","aid":"Safe sleep ABCs: Alone, on the Back, in a bare Crib.","src":"Pediatric_Nursing_Complete_Linear_Study_Packet.pdf","pg":"Common Health Concerns: SIDS (no page numbers in file)","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Evaluate","comp":"teach","traps":{"0":"irrelevant","1":"irrelevant","3":"irrelevant"},"safer":{"s":"Find the statement needing more teaching.","a":"Bumper and quilt in the crib.","f":"Teaching evaluation: safe sleep.","e":"Back to sleep, pacifier, avoiding overheating are correct.","r":"Soft bedding raises SIDS risk."},"principle":"In negative stems, find the unsafe statement.","takeaway":"Alone, on the back, in a bare crib.","xv":2},{"id":"K05","subj":"Pediatrics","topic":"Suspected child maltreatment","cn":"Coordinated Care","cn2":"Psychosocial Integrity","fmt":"mc","diff":"I","stem":"A 3-year-old has bruises in several stages of healing. The parent's explanation does not match the injuries. What should the nurse do?","opts":["Wait for the x-ray results to prove abuse before making any report","Interview the parent and child together so the child feels safe and supported","Confront the parent calmly about the story not matching the injuries","Document objective findings and the parent's exact words, and report"],"key":3,"rat":"All states mandate reporting. The reporter does not need to prove maltreatment; doubting the caretaker's explanation is enough. Document objective findings and use direct quotes, question child and parent separately, and maintain a nonjudgmental attitude.","why":{"0":"Proof is not required to report.","1":"Child and parent are questioned separately.","2":"A nonjudgmental attitude is maintained toward caretakers."},"cues":"Injury inconsistent with history.","aid":"Suspect, Document (quotes), Report: you're a smoke detector, not the fire investigator.","src":"PEDS_Complete_Linear_Word_Sources_2026.pdf","pg":"pp. 90–112 (Child Maltreatment)","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Intervene","comp":"scope","traps":{"0":"delay","1":"unsafe","2":"unsafe"},"safer":{"s":"Choose the action.","a":"Bruises in stages; explanation doesn't fit.","f":"Legal duty: mandatory reporting.","e":"Waiting for proof, joint interviews, and confrontation are wrong.","r":"Objective documentation and reporting meet the legal duty."},"principle":"Suspicion, not proof, triggers the duty to report.","takeaway":"Be the smoke detector, not the fire investigator.","xv":2},{"id":"K06","subj":"Pediatrics","topic":"Weight-based dose calculation","cn":"Pharmacological Therapies","cn2":"","fmt":"num","diff":"I","stem":"A child weighs 44 lb. The order is nitrofurantoin 6 mg/kg/24 hr, divided into four equal doses. Available: nitrofurantoin suspension 25 mg/5 mL. Use 2.2 lb = 1 kg. How many mL will the nurse give per dose? Enter a whole number.","key":6,"unit":"mL","tol":0,"rat":"44 lb ÷ 2.2 = 20 kg. 6 mg × 20 kg = 120 mg per 24 hours. 120 ÷ 4 = 30 mg per dose. 30 mg ÷ 25 mg × 5 mL = 6 mL. The ordered 6 mg/kg/24 hr falls within the source's pediatric range of 5–7 mg/kg/24 hr in four divided doses.","why":{},"cues":"Daily dose divided by four.","aid":"Weigh → kg → daily mg → divide → convert to mL. Do one step per line.","src":"Pain_Management_and_Urinary_System_Combined.pdf","pg":"pp. 45–46; Pediatric packet (weight-based dosing)","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Calculate","comp":"med","traps":{},"safer":{"s":"Calculate mL per dose.","a":"44 lb; 6 mg/kg/24 hr in 4 doses; 25 mg/5 mL.","f":"Weight-based dosing.","e":"Check units at each step.","r":"20 kg → 120 mg/day → 30 mg/dose → 6 mL."},"principle":"One step per line, units on every line.","takeaway":"Weight, kg, daily mg, divide, convert to mL.","xv":2},{"id":"K07","subj":"Pediatrics","topic":"Play and development","cn":"Health Promotion and Maintenance","cn2":"Psychosocial Integrity","fmt":"mc","diff":"F","stem":"Which activity best supports the developmental stage of a hospitalized 8-year-old?","opts":["A rattle and soft blocks","A push-pull toy","A board game with rules","Peek-a-boo with the nurse"],"key":2,"rat":"School-age children (6–12) are in industry vs. inferiority. Board games, collections, team sports, and hobbies support rule-following and achievement.","why":{"0":"Sensory toys suit infants.","1":"Push-pull toys suit toddlers.","3":"Peek-a-boo suits infants."},"cues":"Age 8 = school age.","aid":"School age = rules and results: games with winners build 'industry.'","src":"Pediatric_Nursing_Complete_Linear_Study_Packet.pdf","pg":"Activities by Age; Erikson table (no page numbers in file)","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Interpret","comp":"analysis","traps":{"0":"otherdx","1":"otherdx","3":"otherdx"},"safer":{"s":"Match the activity to the stage.","a":"Age 8.","f":"Growth and development: Erikson.","e":"Rattle, push-pull toy, peek-a-boo suit younger children.","r":"Board games with rules support industry."},"principle":"Age sets the developmental lens.","takeaway":"School age = rules and results.","xv":2},{"id":"K08","subj":"Pediatrics","topic":"Pediatric pain scale","cn":"Basic Care and Comfort","cn2":"","fmt":"mc","diff":"F","stem":"Which tool is most appropriate for assessing pain in an alert, verbal 4-year-old?","opts":["0–10 numeric rating scale","Wong-Baker FACES scale","The parent's rating alone","Vital signs alone"],"key":1,"rat":"The Wong-Baker FACES scale is used for children 3 and older; numeric and FLACC scales are also used in pediatric settings.","why":{"0":"A 4-year-old usually cannot use abstract numbers reliably.","2":"A verbal child can self-report with an age-appropriate tool.","3":"Vital signs alone do not measure the child's pain experience."},"cues":"Age 4, verbal.","aid":"Faces for 3-and-up: kids point to how they feel.","src":"Pediatric_Nursing_Complete_Linear_Study_Packet.pdf","pg":"Common Procedures: Pain Assessment (no page numbers in file)","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Intervene","comp":"intervention","traps":{"0":"otherdx","2":"otherdx","3":"otherdx"},"safer":{"s":"Choose the pain tool.","a":"Alert, verbal 4-year-old.","f":"Pediatric pain assessment.","e":"Numbers are too abstract, parent or vitals alone skip self-report.","r":"FACES fits ages 3 and up."},"principle":"Use a self-report tool that matches the child's development.","takeaway":"Faces for 3 and up.","xv":2},{"id":"K09","ver":2,"subj":"Pediatrics","topic":"Bleeding after tonsillectomy","cn":"Reduction of Risk Potential","cn2":"Physiological Adaptation","fmt":"mc","diff":"A","ask":"Prioritize","comp":"cue","stem":"Four hours after a tonsillectomy, a 6-year-old is swallowing frequently and restless. The pulse is 128/min (104/min an hour ago), the child looks pale, and BP is 98/62, unchanged. The parent says, \"He's just uncomfortable.\" Which action should the nurse take first?","opts":["Give the ordered analgesic because restlessness reflects postoperative pain","Look at the throat with a good light for bleeding and notify the provider","Reassure the parent because the blood pressure has not changed","Offer apple juice to soothe the throat and encourage drinking"],"key":1,"rat":"Signs of post-tonsillectomy hemorrhage include a pulse above 120, pallor, frequent swallowing or throat clearing, and vomiting bright red blood. Restlessness can signal hemorrhage and is hard to tell apart from discomfort. Decreasing blood pressure is a late sign of shock. The nurse observes the throat directly with a good light and notifies the provider.","why":{"0":"Restlessness here is part of a hemorrhage pattern; treating it as pain delays recognition.","2":"Blood pressure falls late in shock, so an unchanged BP is not reassuring.","3":"Fluids are encouraged in recovery, but not before ruling out active bleeding."},"traps":{"0":"otherdx","2":"reassure","3":"later"},"cues":"Frequent swallowing, rising pulse over 120, pallor, restlessness; BP unchanged.","aid":"After tonsils: swallowing, swallowing, swallowing = bleeding until proven otherwise.","principle":"Read the trend: a rising pulse with frequent swallowing outranks a stable blood pressure.","takeaway":"In children, BP holds until late. Pulse, pallor, and swallowing tell you about bleeding first.","safer":{"s":"Choose the first action.","a":"Pulse 104 to 128, frequent swallowing, pallor, restlessness, BP unchanged, 4 hours postop.","f":"Postoperative hemorrhage pattern in a child.","e":"Analgesic misreads the cue, unchanged BP is falsely reassuring, fluids come later.","r":"Inspecting and notifying beats the analgesic because the cue cluster points to bleeding."},"compare":{"a":1,"b":0,"aWhy":"It confirms bleeding and gets the provider involved quickly.","bWhy":"Restlessness after surgery often reflects pain, and the child has had surgery on a painful area.","decider":"Frequent swallowing, a pulse over 120, and pallor together point to hemorrhage, not pain.","flip":"If the pulse were steady, the child pink, and not swallowing repeatedly, treating pain would be appropriate."},"src":"PEDS_Complete_Linear_Word_Sources_2026.pdf","pg":"pp. 154–210 (Tonsillectomy)","sameFormExclude":["V07"],"cog":"judgment"},{"id":"N01","subj":"Neuro & Sensory","topic":"Care during a seizure","cn":"Safety and Infection Prevention and Control","cn2":"","fmt":"mc","diff":"F","stem":"A client begins having a generalized tonic-clonic seizure in bed. What should the nurse do?","opts":["Insert a padded tongue blade between the teeth to protect the tongue","Hold the arms and legs firmly to keep the client from injury","Turn the client to the side, protect the head, and time the seizure","Leave the room quickly to bring the charge nurse to the bedside"],"key":2,"rat":"During a seizure: turn the client to the side, protect the head, do not restrain, place nothing in the mouth, time the seizure, and maintain the airway with suction afterward if needed.","why":{"0":"Nothing is placed in the mouth.","1":"Restraint is not used.","3":"The nurse stays with the client and calls for help from the bedside."},"cues":"","aid":"Seizure care is 'Side, Safe, Stopwatch': side-lying, head protected, time it.","src":"Neuro_Nursing_Complete_Combined_Study_Sources.pdf","pg":"pp. 29–199 (seizure slides)","sameFormExclude":["CASE2"],"ask":"Intervene","comp":"intervention","traps":{"0":"unsafe","1":"invasive","3":"unsafe"},"safer":{"s":"Choose the correct seizure care.","a":"Generalized tonic-clonic seizure in bed.","f":"Safety during a seizure.","e":"Objects in the mouth and restraint cause injury; leaving abandons the client.","r":"Side-lying, head protection, and timing protect the airway and record the event."},"principle":"Protect, don't restrain.","takeaway":"Side, safe, stopwatch.","xv":2},{"id":"N02","subj":"Neuro & Sensory","topic":"Phenytoin level","cn":"Pharmacological Therapies","cn2":"Reduction of Risk Potential","fmt":"mc","diff":"I","stem":"A client taking phenytoin has a serum phenytoin level of 14 mcg/mL. How should the nurse interpret this result?","opts":["Below the therapeutic range; the dose is too low.","Within the therapeutic range.","Toxic; hold the next dose.","The level cannot be interpreted without a CBC."],"key":1,"rat":"The therapeutic phenytoin level in your source is 10–20 mcg/mL. Other teaching points are gingival hyperplasia and folic acid supplementation.","why":{"0":"14 is above the lower limit of 10.","2":"14 is below the upper limit of 20.","3":"A CBC is monitored for carbamazepine, not to interpret a phenytoin level."},"cues":"","aid":"Phenytoin's 'ten to twenty': the number range is as easy as counting fingers and toes.","src":"Neuro_Nursing_Complete_Combined_Study_Sources.pdf","pg":"pp. 29–199 (Antiepileptics table); pp. 218–274","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Interpret","comp":"med","traps":{"0":"otherdx","2":"otherdx","3":"irrelevant"},"safer":{"s":"Interpret the level.","a":"Phenytoin 14 mcg/mL.","f":"Therapeutic drug monitoring.","e":"Below, toxic, and needing a CBC don't fit.","r":"14 lies within 10–20."},"principle":"Compare the value to the specific therapeutic range.","takeaway":"Phenytoin: 10 to 20.","med":{"why":"Seizure control.","check":"Serum level 10–20 mcg/mL.","give":"As prescribed.","watch":"Gingival hyperplasia; toxicity above range.","teach":"Oral hygiene; folic acid as prescribed."},"xv":2},{"id":"N03","subj":"Neuro & Sensory","topic":"Dysphagia after stroke","cn":"Reduction of Risk Potential","cn2":"Safety and Infection Prevention and Control","fmt":"mc","diff":"I","stem":"A client recovering from a stroke coughs while drinking water and has a gurgling voice afterward. What is the nurse's best action?","opts":["Give the rest of the water more slowly, in small sips with a spoon.","Keep the client NPO and report the need for a swallow evaluation.","Lower the head of the bed so liquid flows back out more easily.","Switch to thin apple juice, which is easier for the client to swallow."],"key":1,"rat":"Coughing or choking while drinking and a gurgling voice after swallowing indicate dysphagia. The client stays NPO until a speech-language pathologist completes a swallow evaluation, then high Fowler's position, chin tuck, and thickened liquids or pureed food are used as recommended.","why":{"0":"Continuing oral intake risks aspiration.","2":"High Fowler's, not a lowered head, is used for meals.","3":"Thickened, not thin, liquids are used when recommended."},"cues":"Cough + gurgle after swallowing.","aid":"Gurgle means 'stop the sip': NPO until the swallow expert says go.","src":"Neuro_Nursing_Complete_Combined_Study_Sources.pdf","pg":"pp. 29–199 (Dysphagia slide)","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Intervene","comp":"cue","traps":{"0":"unsafe","2":"unsafe","3":"unsafe"},"safer":{"s":"Choose the best action.","a":"Cough while drinking, gurgly voice after a stroke.","f":"Aspiration risk: dysphagia.","e":"More water, lowering the head, and thin juice raise aspiration risk.","r":"NPO and a swallow evaluation come first."},"principle":"Stop the risky intake and get the right evaluation.","takeaway":"Gurgle means stop the sip.","xv":2},{"id":"N04","subj":"Neuro & Sensory","topic":"Unilateral neglect","cn":"Safety and Infection Prevention and Control","cn2":"Basic Care and Comfort","fmt":"mc","diff":"I","stem":"After a right-hemisphere stroke, a client ignores the left side of the body and eats only from the right side of the plate. Where should the nurse initially place the call light and personal items?","opts":["On the client's right (unaffected) side","On the client's left (affected) side","At the foot of the bed","Wherever the family prefers"],"key":0,"rat":"With neglect syndrome, the nurse first approaches from and places the call light, personal items, and meal tray on the unaffected side, then gradually encourages attention to the affected side with cueing and mirrors.","why":{"1":"Items on the neglected side will be missed at first, creating a safety risk.","2":"Items out of reach are a fall risk.","3":"Placement follows the client's deficit, not preference."},"cues":"Right-brain stroke → left neglect.","aid":"Start where they can see, then coach them across: safety first, then rehab.","src":"Neuro_Nursing_Complete_Combined_Study_Sources.pdf","pg":"pp. 29–199 (Neglect Syndrome slides)","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Intervene","comp":"intervention","traps":{"1":"later","2":"unsafe","3":"irrelevant"},"safer":{"s":"Choose initial item placement.","a":"Left neglect after right-hemisphere stroke.","f":"Safety, then rehabilitation.","e":"Affected side is later rehab; foot of bed is out of reach; preference ignores the deficit.","r":"Unaffected side first keeps the call light usable."},"principle":"Safety first, then gradually rehabilitate.","takeaway":"Start where they can see, then coach them across.","xv":2},{"id":"N05","subj":"Neuro & Sensory","topic":"Retinal detachment","cn":"Physiological Adaptation","cn2":"","fmt":"mc","diff":"I","stem":"A client reports sudden flashes of light, floating spots, and loss of part of the visual field in one eye. Which condition does the nurse suspect?","opts":["Cataract","Retinal detachment","Open-angle glaucoma","Conjunctivitis"],"key":1,"rat":"Retinal detachment, separation of the retina from the choroid, presents with flashes of light, floating spots, and loss of a specific field of vision.","why":{"0":"Cataracts cause gradual clouding, not flashes and floaters.","2":"Open-angle glaucoma has no early symptoms and progresses to tunnel vision.","3":"Conjunctivitis causes redness and drainage without field loss."},"cues":"Flashes + floaters + curtain-like field loss.","aid":"Detachment = a curtain falling with fireworks (flashes) and confetti (floaters).","src":"Eyes And Ears Disorders - Patrick Liquete.pdf","pg":"p44; pp48–49","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Interpret","comp":"analysis","traps":{"0":"otherdx","2":"otherdx","3":"otherdx"},"safer":{"s":"Name the condition.","a":"Flashes, floaters, field loss.","f":"Eye disorder comparison.","e":"Cataract, glaucoma, conjunctivitis present differently.","r":"Retinal detachment fits all three cues."},"principle":"Match the full symptom cluster.","takeaway":"Detachment: curtain with fireworks and confetti.","xv":2},{"id":"N06","subj":"Neuro & Sensory","topic":"Hearing aid care","cn":"Basic Care and Comfort","cn2":"","fmt":"sata","diff":"F","stem":"Which actions are appropriate when caring for a client's hearing aid? Select all that apply.","opts":["Wash the ear mold daily in mild soap and water and dry it thoroughly.","Turn the aid off and open the battery compartment when it is not in use.","Reinsert the ear mold if the aid is whistling.","Let the client wear the aid overnight.","Wash the hearing aid itself in water.","Dry the aid on a heated surface."],"key":[0,1,2],"rat":"Do: wash the ear mold daily and dry it, keep extra batteries, turn the aid off and open the battery compartment when not in use, and reinsert the ear mold if it whistles. Don't: wash the aid, put it on a heated surface, drop it, wear it in the shower or overnight, or ignore whistling.","why":{"3":"The aid is not worn overnight.","4":"Only the ear mold is washed, not the aid.","5":"Heated surfaces damage the aid."},"cues":"","aid":"Wash the mold, never the machine.","src":"Eyes And Ears Disorders - Patrick Liquete.pdf","pg":"pp61–63","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Intervene","comp":"intervention","traps":{"3":"unsafe","4":"unsafe","5":"unsafe"},"safer":{"s":"Select appropriate hearing aid care.","a":"Hearing aid care.","f":"Basic care: device handling.","e":"Overnight wear, washing the aid, and heat damage it.","r":"Washing the mold, opening the battery door, and reseating a whistling mold are correct."},"principle":"Judge each option on its own.","takeaway":"Wash the mold, never the machine.","xv":2},{"id":"N07","subj":"Neuro & Sensory","topic":"Lumbar puncture contraindication","cn":"Reduction of Risk Potential","cn2":"Coordinated Care","fmt":"mc","diff":"A","stem":"Lumbar punctures are scheduled for four clients. For which client should the nurse question the order?","opts":["A client with suspected meningeal infection whose vital signs are stable","A client with a head injury who has a worsening headache and vomiting and is drowsier","A client with new numbness being evaluated for multiple sclerosis","A client who needs CSF pressure measured and is alert and oriented"],"key":1,"rat":"Your instructor's slide states lumbar puncture is not done with increased ICP. Worsening headache, vomiting, and declining LOC after head injury are signs of increased ICP.","why":{"0":"LP is used to look for infection (WBCs, glucose changes in CSF).","2":"LP is a standard diagnostic study with no ICP signs present.","3":"Measuring CSF pressure is a listed LP purpose."},"cues":"Signs of increased ICP.","aid":"Never pull the plug from below when the pressure above is high.","src":"Neuro AP and diagnostics student copy.pdf","pg":"pp24–25; Neuro combined pp. 29–199 (IICP)","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Clarify","comp":"cue","traps":{"0":"irrelevant","2":"irrelevant","3":"irrelevant"},"safer":{"s":"Find the LP order to question.","a":"Worsening headache, vomiting, falling LOC after head injury.","f":"Diagnostic test safety: contraindication.","e":"Infection workup, MS evaluation, and pressure measurement are listed purposes.","r":"Signs of increased ICP contraindicate LP."},"principle":"Check each client for the test's contraindication.","takeaway":"Never pull from below when pressure above is high.","xv":2},{"id":"Q01","subj":"Respiratory","topic":"Chest tube water-seal assessment","cn":"Reduction of Risk Potential","cn2":"","fmt":"mc","diff":"I","stem":"The nurse checks a client's chest tube with water-seal drainage. Which finding requires follow-up?","opts":["The water level fluctuates with respirations.","The drainage system is below the level of the chest.","There is continuous bubbling in the water-seal chamber.","There is occasional, intermittent bubbling in the water-seal chamber."],"key":2,"rat":"Bubbling in the water seal may occur intermittently, but continuous bubbling means an air leak. Fluctuation with breathing is expected, and the drainage system is never raised to chest level because fluid drains back into the pleural space.","why":{"0":"Fluctuation is an expected finding.","1":"The system belongs below chest level.","3":"Intermittent bubbling can occur normally."},"cues":"Continuous bubbling.","aid":"Water seal bubbling: a hiccup now and then is OK; nonstop bubbling is a leak.","src":"Respiratory System Disorders pt 2.pdf","pg":"p17","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Prioritize","comp":"cue","traps":{"0":"reassure","1":"reassure","3":"reassure"},"safer":{"s":"Find the finding needing follow-up.","a":"Continuous bubbling in the water seal.","f":"Expected versus abnormal chest tube findings.","e":"Fluctuation, low placement, and intermittent bubbling are expected.","r":"Continuous bubbling means an air leak."},"principle":"Know the normal behavior of the equipment.","takeaway":"A hiccup of bubbles is fine; nonstop bubbling is a leak.","xv":2},{"id":"Q02","subj":"Respiratory","topic":"Pulmonary embolism recognition","cn":"Physiological Adaptation","cn2":"Coordinated Care","fmt":"mc","diff":"A","stem":"On postoperative day 3 after hip surgery, a client suddenly becomes short of breath with chest pain. RR is 32 and SpO₂ is 88%. What should the nurse do first?","opts":["Help the client walk in the hall to improve circulation in the legs","Raise the head of the bed, apply oxygen as ordered, and notify the provider","Give the PRN oral analgesic for chest pain and recheck in 30 minutes","Encourage the client to cough forcefully to clear the airway"],"key":1,"rat":"Sudden, unexplained dyspnea, tachypnea, and chest pain suggest pulmonary embolism. Management includes oxygen, head of bed up 30 degrees, and anticoagulants, with careful assessment and pulse oximetry.","why":{"0":"Activity raises oxygen demand during an acute event.","2":"Treating pain alone delays care for a possible PE.","3":"Coughing does not address an embolus and adds work of breathing."},"cues":"Sudden dyspnea + chest pain + low SpO₂ after surgery.","aid":"Sudden breathless after surgery = think clot to the lungs: sit up, oxygen on, call.","src":"Respiratory System Disorders pt 2.pdf","pg":"pp36, 39","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Intervene","comp":"intervention","traps":{"0":"unsafe","2":"delay","3":"irrelevant"},"safer":{"s":"Choose the first action.","a":"Postop day 3, sudden dyspnea, chest pain, RR 32, SpO₂ 88%.","f":"Possible pulmonary embolism: oxygenation emergency.","e":"Walking adds demand, analgesia delays, coughing doesn't treat a clot.","r":"Positioning, oxygen, and notification address the emergency."},"principle":"Sudden respiratory change after surgery is an emergency until proven otherwise.","takeaway":"Sudden breathlessness after surgery: sit up, oxygen on, call.","xv":2},{"id":"Q03","subj":"Respiratory","topic":"Emphysema nutrition","cn":"Basic Care and Comfort","cn2":"","fmt":"mc","diff":"F","stem":"A client with emphysema has been losing weight. Which instruction should the nurse reinforce?","opts":["\"Eat three large meals a day so you get enough calories.\"","\"Rest before meals and eat 5 to 6 small high-protein, high-calorie meals.\"","\"Follow a low-calorie diet so your lungs don't have to work as hard.\"","\"Exercise right before meals to help build up your appetite.\""],"key":1,"rat":"Clients with emphysema rest 30 minutes before eating to conserve energy and eat a high-protein, high-calorie diet divided into 5–6 meals a day.","why":{"0":"Large meals increase fatigue and dyspnea.","2":"Calories are increased, not restricted.","3":"Exertion before meals leaves the client too tired to eat."},"cues":"Weight loss + emphysema.","aid":"Emphysema eating: rest first, then small, rich, and frequent.","src":"Respiratory System Disorders pt 2.pdf","pg":"p54","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Teach","comp":"teach","traps":{"0":"unsafe","2":"unsafe","3":"unsafe"},"safer":{"s":"Choose the correct instruction.","a":"Emphysema with weight loss.","f":"Nutrition and energy conservation.","e":"Large meals, low calories, and exercise before meals worsen it.","r":"Rest first, then small, frequent, high-calorie, high-protein meals."},"principle":"Teach around the client's limited energy.","takeaway":"Rest first, then small, rich, frequent meals.","xv":2},{"id":"Q04","subj":"Respiratory","topic":"Asthma rescue vs maintenance","cn":"Pharmacological Therapies","cn2":"","fmt":"mc","diff":"I","stem":"A client having an acute asthma attack has prescriptions for several inhalers. Which inhaler should the nurse have the client use now?","opts":["Salmeterol (Serevent)","Albuterol (Proventil)","An inhaled corticosteroid","Any of these, since all treat asthma"],"key":1,"rat":"Albuterol (Proventil) is the acute or rescue inhalant. Salmeterol (Serevent) and inhaled corticosteroids are maintenance or prophylactic therapy.","why":{"0":"Salmeterol is a prophylactic maintenance drug.","2":"Inhaled corticosteroids are maintenance therapy.","3":"Only the rescue inhaler acts quickly in an acute attack."},"cues":"\"Acute attack.\"","aid":"ProVENTil prevents the emergency right now; SereVENT keeps the vents calm long-term.","src":"Respiratory System Disorders pt 2.pdf","pg":"p57","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Intervene","comp":"med","traps":{"0":"otherdx","2":"otherdx","3":"irrelevant"},"safer":{"s":"Choose the inhaler for now.","a":"Acute asthma attack.","f":"Rescue versus maintenance medication.","e":"Salmeterol and steroids are maintenance; 'any' ignores onset.","r":"Albuterol is the rescue inhaler."},"principle":"Match the drug's onset to the urgency.","takeaway":"Rescue now: albuterol. Prevention later: salmeterol, steroids.","med":{"why":"Acute bronchospasm.","check":"Respiratory status before and after.","give":"Inhaled rescue dose.","watch":"Relief of wheezing; tachycardia.","teach":"Carry the rescue inhaler; maintenance inhalers don't treat attacks."},"xv":2},{"id":"Q05","subj":"Respiratory","topic":"TB infection vs active disease","cn":"Health Promotion and Maintenance","cn2":"Safety and Infection Prevention and Control","fmt":"mc","diff":"I","stem":"A client has a positive tuberculin skin test, a negative chest x-ray, and no symptoms. How does the nurse interpret these findings?","opts":["Active tuberculosis disease that needs airborne isolation","Tuberculosis infection without active disease","A false-positive skin test that needs no further follow-up","Tuberculosis that was cured by an earlier course of treatment"],"key":1,"rat":"TB infection shows antibodies to mycobacteria (positive skin test) with a negative chest x-ray and no signs or symptoms. Active disease has signs and symptoms, and infection can activate when the immune system is compromised.","why":{"0":"Active disease would show signs and symptoms.","2":"The source treats this pattern as infection, which precedes active disease.","3":"Nothing indicates prior treatment or cure."},"cues":"Positive skin test, clear x-ray, no symptoms.","aid":"Infected = the seed is planted; active = the plant has grown.","src":"Respiratory System Disorders pt 2.pdf","pg":"p78","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Interpret","comp":"analysis","traps":{"0":"otherdx","2":"reassure","3":"otherdx"},"safer":{"s":"Interpret the findings.","a":"Positive skin test, negative x-ray, no symptoms.","f":"TB infection versus disease.","e":"Active disease has symptoms; false positive and cured are unsupported.","r":"Infection without active disease."},"principle":"Combine all results before naming the status.","takeaway":"Infected: seed planted. Active: plant grown.","xv":2},{"id":"H01","subj":"Hematology","topic":"Oral iron teaching","cn":"Pharmacological Therapies","cn2":"Health Promotion and Maintenance","fmt":"mc","diff":"I","stem":"A client with iron deficiency anemia is starting liquid ferrous sulfate. Which statement indicates a need for further teaching?","opts":["\"I'll dilute it and drink it through a straw, then rinse my mouth.\"","\"Taking it with orange juice can help me absorb it.\"","\"If my stools turn black, I'll stop the iron and call the doctor.\"","\"I'll report diarrhea or nausea.\""],"key":2,"rat":"Iron turns stools green to black; black, tarry stools are expected with iron therapy. Liquid iron is diluted and taken through a straw with oral hygiene afterward, vitamin C enhances absorption, and diarrhea or nausea are reported.","why":{"0":"Correct: prevents tooth staining.","1":"Correct: vitamin C enhances absorption.","3":"Correct: these are significant findings to report."},"cues":"","aid":"Iron = black stools, stained teeth (use a straw), and vitamin C as its best friend.","src":"Blood_and_Lymph_Complete_Linear_Source_Packet.pdf","pg":"pp10–11","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Evaluate","comp":"teach","traps":{"0":"irrelevant","1":"irrelevant","3":"irrelevant"},"safer":{"s":"Find the statement needing more teaching.","a":"Plans to stop iron for black stools.","f":"Teaching evaluation: expected effect.","e":"Straw, vitamin C, reporting GI upset are correct.","r":"Black stools are expected with iron."},"principle":"Teach expected effects so clients don't stop therapy.","takeaway":"Iron: black stools are normal; use a straw.","med":{"why":"Iron deficiency anemia.","check":"GI tolerance.","give":"Diluted, through a straw; with vitamin C.","watch":"Black stools (expected), nausea, diarrhea.","teach":"Rinse mouth after; report GI upset."},"xv":2},{"id":"H02","subj":"Hematology","topic":"Sickle cell crisis care","cn":"Physiological Adaptation","cn2":"Basic Care and Comfort","fmt":"sata","diff":"I","stem":"A client is admitted in sickle cell pain crisis. Which interventions should the nurse anticipate? Select all that apply.","opts":["Increase fluid intake as ordered.","Give analgesics as ordered.","Administer oxygen as ordered.","Offer iced drinks to cool the client.","Apply cold packs to painful joints.","Encourage vigorous exercise to improve circulation."],"key":[0,1,2],"rat":"Crisis care includes hydration to dilute blood and reverse sickling, analgesia (NSAIDs, then opiates or PCA), and oxygen, which can reverse sickling. Clients avoid extreme temperatures, iced liquids, dehydration, and vigorous exercise.","why":{"3":"Iced liquids are listed among triggers to avoid.","4":"Extreme temperatures are avoided.","5":"Vigorous exercise is a trigger to avoid."},"cues":"","aid":"Sickle crisis 'HOP': Hydrate, Oxygenate, Pain control. Keep it warm, wet, and calm.","src":"Blood_and_Lymph_Complete_Linear_Source_Packet.pdf","pg":"pp27–29","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Intervene","comp":"intervention","traps":{"3":"unsafe","4":"unsafe","5":"unsafe"},"safer":{"s":"Select anticipated interventions.","a":"Sickle cell pain crisis.","f":"Crisis management: reverse sickling.","e":"Iced drinks, cold packs, and vigorous exercise are triggers.","r":"Fluids, analgesia, and oxygen reverse sickling and relieve pain."},"principle":"Avoid anything listed as a trigger.","takeaway":"Hydrate, oxygenate, pain control; warm, wet, calm.","xv":2},{"id":"H03","ver":2,"subj":"Hematology","topic":"New fever in agranulocytosis","cn":"Safety and Infection Prevention and Control","cn2":"Reduction of Risk Potential","fmt":"mc","diff":"I","ask":"Prioritize","comp":"cue","stem":"A client with agranulocytosis has an absolute neutrophil count of 100 cells/mm³. Which finding should the nurse report to the provider first?","opts":["A sore throat that has been present since admission and is unchanged","Fatigue after walking to the bathroom","A request for a fresh fruit salad from the cafeteria","Temperature 100.8°F (38.2°C) with new chills"],"key":3,"rat":"A client with a compromised WBC system is susceptible to life-threatening infections, so the nurse closely monitors for signs of infection and reports them promptly to start therapy. Fever, usually over 100.6°F, is a key finding. Raw foods are avoided and rest is balanced with activity, but these are teaching and comfort needs.","why":{"0":"A sore throat is a symptom of agranulocytosis, but it is unchanged since admission; the new fever is the change.","1":"Fatigue is expected; balancing rest and activity addresses it.","2":"Raw foods are avoided, which calls for teaching rather than urgent reporting."},"traps":{"0":"isolated","1":"later","2":"later"},"cues":"New fever with chills when neutrophils are nearly absent.","aid":"No neutrophils, no warning signs except fever: treat a fever like a fire alarm.","principle":"New outranks unchanged; a sign of systemic infection outranks a stable local symptom.","takeaway":"In neutropenia, a low-grade fever can be the only sign of serious infection. New fever gets reported now.","safer":{"s":"Choose the finding to report first.","a":"ANC 100, new fever 100.8°F with chills, unchanged sore throat, expected fatigue, raw-food request.","f":"Infection risk with compromised immunity; new versus baseline.","e":"Fatigue and the food request are teaching or comfort needs.","r":"The new fever beats the unchanged sore throat because it is new and suggests systemic infection."},"compare":{"a":3,"b":0,"aWhy":"Fever is the key sign of infection and must be reported promptly.","bWhy":"Sore throat is also a listed symptom of agranulocytosis.","decider":"The fever is new with chills; the sore throat has not changed since admission.","flip":"If the sore throat were new and worsening with white patches while temperature stayed normal, it would move up."},"src":"Blood_and_Lymph_Complete_Linear_Source_Packet.pdf","pg":"pp37–38","sameFormExclude":["V09"],"cog":"judgment"},{"id":"H04","subj":"Hematology","topic":"Hemophilia joint bleed","cn":"Reduction of Risk Potential","cn2":"Health Promotion and Maintenance","fmt":"mc","diff":"I","stem":"A teenager with hemophilia A bumps his knee, which is now swollen and painful. What should the parent do first?","opts":["Apply a heating pad to the knee to increase circulation.","Give aspirin for the pain and keep the knee elevated.","Immobilize the knee, apply ice, and notify the provider.","Have him walk it off so the joint doesn't stiffen up overnight."],"key":2,"rat":"For bleeding, immobilize the affected part, apply ice and pressure, and notify the provider. Aspirin and aspirin products are avoided because they worsen bleeding.","why":{"0":"Heat increases bleeding; cold is applied.","1":"Aspirin worsens the bleeding tendency.","3":"The part is immobilized, not used."},"cues":"Joint swelling after minor trauma.","aid":"Hemophilia bleed: Still it (immobilize), Squeeze it (pressure), Ice it, then Call.","src":"Blood_and_Lymph_Complete_Linear_Source_Packet.pdf","pg":"p54","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Intervene","comp":"intervention","traps":{"0":"unsafe","1":"unsafe","3":"unsafe"},"safer":{"s":"Choose the first action.","a":"Swollen painful knee in hemophilia.","f":"Bleeding control.","e":"Heat, aspirin, and walking worsen bleeding.","r":"Immobilize, ice, pressure, and notify."},"principle":"In bleeding disorders, stop the bleed and avoid anything that worsens it.","takeaway":"Still it, squeeze it, ice it, call.","xv":2},{"id":"A01","ver":2,"subj":"Pain & Opioids","topic":"Opioid hold parameter versus unrelieved pain","cn":"Pharmacological Therapies","cn2":"Safety and Infection Prevention and Control","fmt":"mc","diff":"A","ask":"Intervene","comp":"med","stem":"A postoperative client has a prescription for morphine 10 mg PO every 4 hours PRN pain, hold for respirations below 12/min. The client rates pain 7/10, is drowsy but arousable, and has a respiratory rate of 11/min. Which action should the nurse take?","opts":["Give half the dose so pain is treated with less sedation","Give the dose because untreated pain slows postoperative recovery","Give the dose and keep naloxone and airway equipment at the bedside","Withhold the dose and report the findings to the provider"],"key":3,"rat":"Before an opiate agonist, the nurse obtains vital signs and holds the medication if respirations are below 12/min, then consults the provider. Opiates make the respiratory center less sensitive to carbon dioxide. Poor pain control is reported promptly so orders can be modified. Naloxone reverses opiates, but its availability does not make a held dose safe to give.","why":{"0":"Changing a dose without a prescription is outside the nurse's role.","1":"Pain control matters, but not by violating the hold parameter in a sedated client.","2":"Having the antidote nearby does not make it safe to give a dose the prescription says to hold."},"traps":{"0":"scope","1":"irrelevant","2":"unsafe"},"cues":"RR 11 under a hold-for-12 prescription, drowsy, pain 7/10.","aid":"Count the breaths before you count the pills.","principle":"Honor the safety parameter, then escalate the unmet need with useful data.","takeaway":"Untreated pain is a real problem, but the answer is to report it for new orders, not to override a hold parameter.","safer":{"s":"Decide what to do with a PRN opioid dose.","a":"RR 11, hold if below 12, drowsy, pain 7/10.","f":"Medication safety: CHECK before GIVE.","e":"Half dose is out of scope; giving with or without naloxone ignores the hold order.","r":"Withholding and reporting beats giving because the prescription itself says hold, and the provider can change the plan."},"compare":{"a":3,"b":1,"aWhy":"It follows the hold parameter and gets the pain addressed through the provider.","bWhy":"Pain of 7/10 is significant, and relieving pain is part of postoperative care.","decider":"The respiratory rate is below the prescribed hold parameter in a drowsy client.","flip":"If the respiratory rate were 16 and the client alert, giving the dose for pain of 7/10 would be appropriate."},"med":{"why":"Moderate to severe postoperative pain.","check":"Baseline neuro status, vital signs (hold if RR below 12), bowel sounds, prior analgesics, pain score.","give":"As prescribed; never change the dose without an order.","watch":"Respiratory depression, sedation, orthostatic hypotension, constipation, urinary retention.","teach":"Call before getting up, report breathing changes, increase fiber and fluids."},"src":"Pain_Management_and_Urinary_System_Combined.pdf","pg":"pp3–5","sameFormExclude":["CASE5"],"cog":"judgment"},{"id":"A02","subj":"Pain & Opioids","topic":"Acetaminophen limit with alcohol use","cn":"Pharmacological Therapies","cn2":"Reduction of Risk Potential","fmt":"mc","diff":"I","stem":"A client with long-standing heavy alcohol use asks how much acetaminophen is safe to take in a day. According to the source guidance, what is the maximum?","opts":["2 g per day","3.25 g per day","4 g per day","6 g per day"],"key":0,"rat":"Adults should receive no more than 4 g daily, but patients with chronic alcohol use should take no more than 2 g per day. Hepatotoxicity is more frequent with heavy alcohol use, and the antidote is acetylcysteine.","why":{"1":"3.25 g is the general OTC maximum without provider direction.","2":"4 g is the general adult ceiling, not for chronic alcohol use.","3":"Doses of 5–8 g daily cause severe hepatotoxicity."},"cues":"Heavy alcohol use + acetaminophen.","aid":"Alcohol halves the limit: 4 g becomes 2 g.","src":"Pain_Management_and_Urinary_System_Combined.pdf","pg":"pp22–23","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Teach","comp":"med","traps":{"1":"otherdx","2":"otherdx","3":"unsafe"},"safer":{"s":"Identify the safe daily maximum.","a":"Heavy alcohol use.","f":"Medication safety: dose limit by risk.","e":"3.25 g and 4 g apply to others; 6 g is hepatotoxic.","r":"2 g per day with chronic alcohol use."},"principle":"Patient risk factors change the safe dose.","takeaway":"Alcohol halves the limit: 4 g becomes 2 g.","med":{"why":"Pain or fever.","check":"Alcohol use, liver disease.","give":"No more than 2 g/day with chronic alcohol use.","watch":"Hepatotoxicity; antidote acetylcysteine.","teach":"Count acetaminophen in combination products."},"xv":2},{"id":"A03","subj":"Pain & Opioids","topic":"Placebo use in pain management","cn":"Coordinated Care","cn2":"Basic Care and Comfort","fmt":"mc","diff":"I","stem":"A coworker suggests giving a client a saline injection \"to see if the pain is real.\" What is the nurse's best response?","opts":["\"Good idea; the result will show us whether the client really needs opioids.\"","\"Placebos are unethical and imply we doubt the client. Let's reassess the pain.\"","\"We can try it, as long as the client doesn't find out what it is.\"","\"Let's wait an hour and see whether the pain goes away on its own.\""],"key":1,"rat":"Placebo therapy should never be used in pain management; it implies disbelief in the client's description and the American Pain Society calls it unethical. Clients have the right to have pain taken seriously, assessed thoroughly, and treated promptly.","why":{"0":"Placebo response does not show whether pain is real.","2":"Deception violates the client's rights.","3":"Pain is assessed and treated promptly."},"cues":"","aid":"Pain is what the client says it is; we treat the report, not our doubts.","src":"Pain_Management_and_Urinary_System_Combined.pdf","pg":"pp64–65","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Intervene","comp":"scope","traps":{"0":"unsafe","2":"unsafe","3":"delay"},"safer":{"s":"Choose the best response.","a":"Suggestion of a saline injection to test pain.","f":"Ethics: client rights in pain management.","e":"Agreeing, deceiving, and waiting all fail the client.","r":"Refusing placebo and reassessing respects the client."},"principle":"Pain is what the client says it is.","takeaway":"Treat the report, not your doubts.","xv":2},{"id":"A04","subj":"Pain & Opioids","topic":"Timing analgesia before activity","cn":"Basic Care and Comfort","cn2":"","fmt":"mc","diff":"F","stem":"A client with an abdominal incision needs to walk and do deep-breathing exercises this morning. Which plan best manages pain?","opts":["Give the analgesic after the walk so the relief lasts longer afterward","Give the analgesic beforehand to peak during activity, and splint the incision","Skip the deep-breathing exercises until the client is completely pain-free","Withhold analgesics this morning so the client stays alert for walking"],"key":1,"rat":"Give analgesics before painful activities and schedule the activity for the medication's peak action. Splint an incisional area before deep breathing and coughing.","why":{"0":"Pain during the activity makes the client less able to participate.","2":"Deep breathing is still needed after surgery.","3":"Untreated pain limits mobility and breathing."},"cues":"","aid":"Medicate, wait for the peak, then move: like warming up the car before driving.","src":"Pain_Management_and_Urinary_System_Combined.pdf","pg":"p72","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Intervene","comp":"intervention","traps":{"0":"later","2":"unsafe","3":"unsafe"},"safer":{"s":"Choose the best pain plan.","a":"Walking and deep breathing with an incision.","f":"Timing analgesia to activity.","e":"After the walk is late, skipping breathing and withholding analgesia are harmful.","r":"Medicate before and time activity to the peak; splint for coughing."},"principle":"Time the intervention so it peaks when needed.","takeaway":"Medicate, wait for the peak, then move.","xv":2},{"id":"S01","ver":2,"subj":"Musculoskeletal","topic":"Compartment syndrome under a cast","cn":"Physiological Adaptation","cn2":"Reduction of Risk Potential","fmt":"mc","diff":"A","ask":"Intervene","comp":"intervention","stem":"Six hours after a long-leg cast was applied for a tibial fracture, the client rates pain 9/10 despite IV morphine given 30 minutes ago. Pain increases when the nurse gently moves the toes, and the toes are tingling. The pedal pulse is palpable. Which action is the priority?","opts":["Elevate the leg above the heart and apply ice packs to the cast","Notify the provider immediately and prepare for the cast to be split","Reassure the client that circulation is adequate because the pulse is palpable","Give the PRN oral oxycodone and reassess the pain in 1 hour"],"key":1,"rat":"Pain that is deep, unrelenting, and unrelieved by analgesics, pain with passive movement, and numbness or tingling indicate compartment syndrome; irreversible muscle ischemia can occur within 6 hours. The instructor's slides direct no elevation above the heart, no ice, loosening the bandage and splitting (bivalving) the cast, and fasciotomy if needed. Pulselessness is a late sign.","why":{"0":"Elevation above the heart and ice further reduce arterial flow in compartment syndrome.","2":"A palpable pulse does not rule it out; pulselessness is a late sign.","3":"Pain unrelieved by analgesics is a warning sign, and another hour risks irreversible ischemia."},"traps":{"0":"otherdx","2":"reassure","3":"delay"},"cues":"Pain out of proportion, unrelieved by morphine, worse with passive movement, tingling.","aid":"Pain with passive stretch is the earliest alarm; pulses vanish last.","principle":"A reassuring single finding does not cancel a dangerous cue cluster.","takeaway":"In compartment syndrome the pulse is the last thing to go. Act on the pain pattern, not the pulse.","safer":{"s":"Choose the priority action.","a":"Pain 9/10 unrelieved by morphine, pain with passive toe movement, tingling, pulse still present, 6 hours after casting.","f":"Neurovascular emergency.","e":"Elevation and ice worsen flow, the pulse is falsely reassuring, more analgesic delays care.","r":"Notifying for cast splitting beats more analgesia because tissue ischemia is time-critical."},"compare":{"a":1,"b":3,"aWhy":"It starts the treatment that relieves pressure.","bWhy":"The client is in severe pain, and pain relief is a nursing responsibility.","decider":"Pain unrelieved by analgesics with passive-stretch pain and tingling signals compartment syndrome, which analgesics cannot fix.","flip":"If the pain responded to the analgesic and neurovascular checks were normal, continued pain management and monitoring would fit."},"src":"Musculoskeletal_Complete_Project_Combined (2).pdf","pg":"pp. 283–313 (Ch. 44 compartment syndrome); slides pp. 3–249 (Ways to prevent amputation)","sameFormExclude":["XE16"],"cog":"judgment"},{"id":"S02","subj":"Musculoskeletal","topic":"Total hip replacement precautions","cn":"Reduction of Risk Potential","cn2":"Safety and Infection Prevention and Control","fmt":"mc","diff":"I","stem":"A client is being discharged after a total hip replacement. Which statement indicates a need for further teaching?","opts":["\"I'll use a raised toilet seat.\"","\"I won't cross my legs.\"","\"I'll lean forward to pull on my socks and shoes.\"","\"I'll use my walker every time I get up.\""],"key":2,"rat":"After hip replacement, the client avoids adduction (crossing the legs), limits hip flexion to 90 degrees for 2–3 months with a raised toilet seat, avoids leaning forward, uses assistive devices to avoid bending at the hip, and uses a walker or crutches.","why":{"0":"Correct: prevents hyperflexion.","1":"Correct: crossing adducts the hip and can dislocate it.","3":"Correct: limits weight bearing."},"cues":"Leaning forward = hip flexion past 90°.","aid":"New hip rules: no crossing, no bending past 90 (a right angle), no leaning forward.","src":"Musculoskeletal_Complete_Project_Combined (2).pdf","pg":"pp. 314–351 (hip arthroplasty)","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Evaluate","comp":"teach","traps":{"0":"irrelevant","1":"irrelevant","3":"irrelevant"},"safer":{"s":"Find the statement needing more teaching.","a":"Leaning forward for socks.","f":"Teaching evaluation: hip precautions.","e":"Raised seat, no crossing, walker are correct.","r":"Leaning forward flexes the hip past 90°."},"principle":"Negative stems: hunt for the unsafe statement.","takeaway":"No crossing, no bending past 90, no leaning forward.","xv":2},{"id":"S03","subj":"Musculoskeletal","topic":"Bisphosphonate administration","cn":"Pharmacological Therapies","cn2":"","fmt":"mc","diff":"F","stem":"A client with osteoporosis is starting a bisphosphonate. Which instruction should the nurse reinforce?","opts":["\"Take it with breakfast so it doesn't upset your stomach.\"","\"Take it with a full glass of water 30 minutes before eating.\"","\"Take it at bedtime with a glass of milk for the extra calcium.\"","\"Take it with your other morning pills so it's easy to remember.\""],"key":1,"rat":"Bisphosphonates prevent calcium from being taken from bone. They are taken with a full glass of water, 30 minutes before food or other medications.","why":{"0":"Food is taken at least 30 minutes after the dose.","2":"Milk is a food; the dose is taken before food with water.","3":"Other medications are taken at least 30 minutes later."},"cues":"","aid":"Bisphosphonate: 'water, wait, then eat' (full glass, 30 minutes).","src":"Musculoskeletal_Complete_Project_Combined (2).pdf","pg":"Slides pp. 3–249 (Osteoporosis drug therapy)","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Teach","comp":"med","traps":{"0":"unsafe","2":"unsafe","3":"unsafe"},"safer":{"s":"Choose the correct instruction.","a":"New bisphosphonate.","f":"Administration timing.","e":"With food, milk, or other pills interferes.","r":"Full glass of water, 30 minutes before food or other medicines."},"principle":"Administration rules are part of the drug's safety.","takeaway":"Water, wait 30 minutes, then eat.","med":{"why":"Osteoporosis.","check":"Ability to follow timing.","give":"Full glass of water, 30 minutes before food or other drugs.","watch":"GI upset.","teach":"Water, wait, then eat."},"xv":2},{"id":"S04","subj":"Musculoskeletal","topic":"Allopurinol and early gout flares","cn":"Pharmacological Therapies","cn2":"","fmt":"mc","diff":"I","stem":"Three weeks after starting allopurinol, a client calls to report a painful gout flare and says, \"This medicine is making it worse. I'm going to stop it.\" What is the nurse's best response?","opts":["\"Stop taking it, and we'll schedule an appointment for a new prescription.\"","\"Early flares are common. Keep the same dose; I'll tell the provider about this one.\"","\"Double your dose until the flare is over, then go back to your usual dose.\"","\"Cut back on fluids so the medicine stays more concentrated in your body.\""],"key":1,"rat":"Gout attacks may increase for the first few months of xanthine oxidase inhibitor therapy; clients continue therapy without changing the dose, and flares may be treated with NSAIDs or colchicine. Fluid intake is kept at 8–12 eight-ounce glasses daily.","why":{"0":"Stopping loses the long-term benefit of lowering uric acid.","2":"The dose is not changed during attacks.","3":"Fluids are increased, not reduced."},"cues":"Early flare on a new urate-lowering drug.","aid":"Allopurinol is a slow cleanup crew: things get stirred up before they get better.","src":"Musculoskeletal_Complete_Project_Combined (2).pdf","pg":"pp. 425–435 (xanthine oxidase inhibitors)","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Teach","comp":"med","traps":{"0":"unsafe","2":"scope","3":"unsafe"},"safer":{"s":"Choose the best response.","a":"Flare 3 weeks after starting allopurinol.","f":"Medication teaching: expected early flares.","e":"Stopping, doubling, and cutting fluids are wrong.","r":"Continue at the same dose and report the flare."},"principle":"Teach expected early effects so clients stay on therapy.","takeaway":"Allopurinol stirs things up before it settles them.","med":{"why":"Lower uric acid in gout.","check":"Fluid intake.","give":"Same dose during flares.","watch":"Early flares in the first months.","teach":"Keep taking it; 8–12 glasses of fluid daily; flares may be treated separately."},"xv":2},{"id":"S05","subj":"Musculoskeletal","topic":"Osteoporosis exercise","cn":"Health Promotion and Maintenance","cn2":"","fmt":"mc","diff":"F","stem":"Which exercise should the nurse recommend to a postmenopausal client to help maintain bone mass?","opts":["Swimming laps","Daily brisk walking","Bed rest on high-pain days","Seated stretching only"],"key":1,"rat":"Weight-bearing exercise builds and maintains bone mass; walking, hiking, weight training, stair climbing, tennis, and dancing are listed. Swimming is a non-weight-bearing activity.","why":{"0":"Swimming is non-weight-bearing.","2":"Inactivity accelerates bone loss; the goal is to keep the client ambulatory.","3":"Stretching alone does not load the bones."},"cues":"\"Maintain bone mass\" = weight-bearing.","aid":"Bones get stronger when they carry weight, like a muscle lifting.","src":"Musculoskeletal_Complete_Project_Combined (2).pdf","pg":"Slides pp. 3–249 (Osteoporosis); Maternal Box 26.10","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Teach","comp":"teach","traps":{"0":"otherdx","2":"unsafe","3":"otherdx"},"safer":{"s":"Choose the bone-building exercise.","a":"Maintain bone mass.","f":"Health promotion: weight-bearing exercise.","e":"Swimming and stretching don't load bone; bed rest accelerates loss.","r":"Brisk walking is weight-bearing."},"principle":"Match the activity to the goal.","takeaway":"Bones get stronger when they carry weight.","xv":2},{"id":"E01","ver":2,"subj":"Endocrine","topic":"Hypoglycemia before a delayed meal","cn":"Physiological Adaptation","cn2":"Coordinated Care","fmt":"mc","diff":"A","ask":"Intervene","comp":"intervention","stem":"A client with type 1 diabetes received regular insulin 2 hours ago, and the lunch tray is late. The client is sweating, trembling, and irritable. Fingerstick glucose is 58 mg/dL. The client is alert and swallowing normally. Which action should the LVN take first?","opts":["Call dietary to send the lunch tray right away","Give 4–6 oz of orange juice","Ask the RN to give 50% dextrose IV","Prepare glucagon for intramuscular injection"],"key":1,"rat":"The endocrine slides define hypoglycemia as glucose below 70 mg/dL and direct the nurse to decide first whether the client is conscious. A conscious client gets 15–20 g of quick carbohydrate by mouth, such as 4–6 oz of orange juice. IV 50% dextrose is given only by the RN, and IM glucagon is the other route when oral intake is not possible.","why":{"0":"The meal helps later, but a tray on its way does not raise glucose now.","2":"IV dextrose is an RN intervention reserved for clients who cannot take glucose by mouth; this client can.","3":"Glucagon IM is for a client who cannot safely take oral carbohydrate."},"traps":{"0":"delay","2":"invasive","3":"invasive"},"cues":"Glucose 58 mg/dL with symptoms, alert, swallowing normally.","aid":"Low and awake? Juice it. Low and can't swallow? Glucagon or the RN's IV.","principle":"Match the treatment route to the client's level of consciousness; use the least invasive effective option.","takeaway":"The first question in hypoglycemia is whether the client can swallow. Awake means oral glucose now; the meal comes after.","safer":{"s":"Pick the first action for symptomatic low glucose.","a":"Glucose 58 mg/dL, sweating, trembling, irritable, alert, swallowing normally, insulin given 2 hours ago, meal delayed.","f":"Emergency treatment of hypoglycemia, chosen by level of consciousness.","e":"Calling for the tray delays treatment. IV dextrose and IM glucagon are more invasive than this client needs.","r":"Oral juice beats glucagon because the client is awake and can swallow."},"compare":{"a":1,"b":3,"aWhy":"Orange juice delivers quick carbohydrate by mouth.","bWhy":"Glucagon also raises glucose and is a listed treatment.","decider":"The client is alert and swallowing, which makes the oral route safe and least invasive.","flip":"If the client were unconscious or could not swallow, IM glucagon (or IV dextrose by the RN) would be the right choice."},"src":"Med_Surg_All_Sources_Linear_Master_2026.pdf","pg":"pp. 349–415 (Endocrine/DM slides 44–46)","sameFormExclude":["XE19"],"cog":"judgment"},{"id":"E02","subj":"Endocrine","topic":"Mixing two insulins","cn":"Pharmacological Therapies","cn2":"","fmt":"mc","diff":"F","stem":"The nurse is preparing regular insulin and an intermediate-acting insulin in one syringe. Which insulin is drawn up first?","opts":["The intermediate-acting insulin","The regular insulin","Either one, as long as the total dose is correct","Whichever vial is fuller"],"key":1,"rat":"When two insulins are combined, regular insulin is withdrawn first, then the longer-acting insulin.","why":{"0":"The longer-acting insulin is drawn second.","2":"Order matters to avoid contaminating the regular insulin vial.","3":"Vial volume does not determine order."},"cues":"","aid":"Clear before cloudy: regular first, then the longer-acting.","src":"Med_Surg_All_Sources_Linear_Master_2026.pdf","pg":"pp. 349–415 (Insulin Administration slide 52)","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Intervene","comp":"med","traps":{"0":"unsafe","2":"irrelevant","3":"irrelevant"},"safer":{"s":"Choose which insulin to draw first.","a":"Regular plus intermediate-acting in one syringe.","f":"Medication preparation sequence.","e":"Intermediate first, either, or by volume are wrong.","r":"Regular first keeps the regular vial uncontaminated."},"principle":"Sequence protects the integrity of the drug.","takeaway":"Clear before cloudy.","med":{"why":"Glucose control.","check":"Glucose, meal timing.","give":"Draw regular first, then the longer-acting.","watch":"Hypoglycemia at peak times.","teach":"Same order every time at home."},"xv":2},{"id":"E03","subj":"Endocrine","topic":"Diabetes insipidus","cn":"Physiological Adaptation","cn2":"","fmt":"mc","diff":"I","stem":"Two days after a head injury, a client is extremely thirsty and voiding large amounts of very pale urine. Urine specific gravity is 1.003. Which condition does the nurse suspect?","opts":["Diabetes insipidus","Syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone (SIADH)","Diabetic ketoacidosis","Urinary tract infection"],"key":0,"rat":"Diabetes insipidus is deficient ADH, causing marked polyuria, intense thirst, and dilute urine with low specific gravity (1.001–1.005). Head injury is a listed cause.","why":{"1":"SIADH causes water retention, decreased output, and dark urine with specific gravity above 1.032.","2":"DKA presents with hyperglycemia, ketones, and acetone breath.","3":"UTI does not produce massive dilute output with intense thirst."},"cues":"Head injury + huge pale output + low specific gravity.","aid":"DI = 'Dry Inside': the body pours water out. SIADH = 'Soaked Inside': it holds water in.","src":"Med_Surg_All_Sources_Linear_Master_2026.pdf","pg":"pp. 416–457 (Pituitary disorders)","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Interpret","comp":"analysis","traps":{"1":"otherdx","2":"otherdx","3":"otherdx"},"safer":{"s":"Name the condition.","a":"Head injury, intense thirst, large pale output, specific gravity 1.003.","f":"ADH disorders comparison.","e":"SIADH retains water; DKA and UTI don't fit.","r":"Diabetes insipidus explains every cue."},"principle":"Use opposite pairs: DI pours water out, SIADH holds it in.","takeaway":"Dry inside (DI) versus soaked inside (SIADH).","xv":2},{"id":"E04","subj":"Endocrine","topic":"SIADH care","cn":"Reduction of Risk Potential","cn2":"Physiological Adaptation","fmt":"sata","diff":"I","stem":"A client with SIADH has a serum sodium of 128 mEq/L. Which interventions should the nurse anticipate? Select all that apply.","opts":["Restrict fluids as ordered.","Weigh the client daily.","Monitor neurologic status.","Encourage the client to drink extra water.","Expect large amounts of pale, dilute urine."],"key":[0,1,2],"rat":"SIADH causes water retention and hyponatremia (sodium below 135). Care includes fluid restriction (800–1000 mL/day), daily weights, I&O, monitoring neuro status as brain cells swell, lung sounds, electrolytes, and urine specific gravity.","why":{"3":"Extra water worsens water retention and hyponatremia.","4":"SIADH produces decreased, dark, concentrated urine."},"cues":"Low sodium + excess ADH.","aid":"SIADH: Soaked Inside, so Shut the tap (restrict fluids).","src":"Med_Surg_All_Sources_Linear_Master_2026.pdf","pg":"pp. 416–457 (SIADH slides)","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Intervene","comp":"intervention","traps":{"3":"unsafe","4":"otherdx"},"safer":{"s":"Select anticipated interventions.","a":"SIADH, sodium 128.","f":"Water retention and hyponatremia.","e":"Extra water worsens it; dilute urine belongs to DI.","r":"Fluid restriction, daily weights, neuro checks."},"principle":"Treat the mechanism: too much water retained.","takeaway":"SIADH: shut the tap.","xv":2},{"id":"E05","subj":"Endocrine","topic":"Levothyroxine teaching","cn":"Pharmacological Therapies","cn2":"Health Promotion and Maintenance","fmt":"mc","diff":"F","stem":"A client with hypothyroidism is starting thyroid hormone replacement. Which statement indicates a need for further teaching?","opts":["\"I'll take it before breakfast.\"","\"I'll report a racing heart, chest pain, or feeling too hot.\"","\"I can stop taking it once I feel back to normal.\"","\"I'll ask before taking sedatives or opioid pain medicine.\""],"key":2,"rat":"Thyroid replacement is lifelong. It is taken before breakfast, signs of overdose such as tachycardia, heat intolerance, and chest pain are reported, and sedatives and opioids are avoided because of increased sensitivity.","why":{"0":"Correct teaching.","1":"Correct: signs of too much hormone.","3":"Correct: hypothyroid clients are more sensitive to these drugs."},"cues":"\"Stop when I feel normal.\"","aid":"Thyroid replacement is a lifelong subscription, not a free trial.","src":"Med_Surg_All_Sources_Linear_Master_2026.pdf","pg":"pp. 458–500 (Hypothyroid interventions)","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Evaluate","comp":"teach","traps":{"0":"irrelevant","1":"irrelevant","3":"irrelevant"},"safer":{"s":"Find the statement needing more teaching.","a":"Plans to stop when feeling normal.","f":"Teaching evaluation: lifelong therapy.","e":"Before breakfast, reporting overdose signs, and caution with sedatives are correct.","r":"Stopping is wrong because replacement is lifelong."},"principle":"Feeling better means the drug is working, not that it's done.","takeaway":"Thyroid replacement is a lifelong subscription.","med":{"why":"Hypothyroidism.","check":"Pulse, signs of excess hormone.","give":"Before breakfast.","watch":"Tachycardia, chest pain, heat intolerance.","teach":"Lifelong; ask before sedatives or opioids."},"xv":2},{"id":"E06","subj":"Endocrine","topic":"Post-thyroidectomy hypocalcemia","cn":"Physiological Adaptation","cn2":"Reduction of Risk Potential","fmt":"mc","diff":"A","stem":"The day after a thyroidectomy, a client reports tingling in the fingers and around the mouth. The nurse observes a carpal spasm when the blood pressure cuff is inflated. What should the nurse do?","opts":["Report the findings immediately and have calcium gluconate available.","Give the PRN analgesic for incision pain and reassess in 1 hour.","Encourage the client to breathe slowly into a paper bag.","Document the findings as expected after neck surgery and continue to monitor."],"key":0,"rat":"Damage to the parathyroids during thyroidectomy causes hypocalcemia: numbness and tingling, Trousseau's sign, and tetany that can progress to laryngeal spasm. Calcium gluconate IV treats hypocalcemic tetany, and a patent airway is maintained.","why":{"1":"Pain medication does not address a dropping calcium level.","2":"The source does not support this; the problem is low calcium, not breathing pattern.","3":"Tetany signs are a complication, not expected."},"cues":"Tingling + Trousseau's sign after neck surgery.","aid":"After a thyroidectomy, think 'calcium CATS': Convulsions, Arrhythmias, Tetany, Spasms and Stridor.","src":"Med_Surg_All_Sources_Linear_Master_2026.pdf","pg":"pp. 458–500 (Hypoparathyroid); Fundamentals review p46","sameFormExclude":["B07"],"ask":"Intervene","comp":"cue","traps":{"1":"otherdx","2":"otherdx","3":"reassure"},"safer":{"s":"Choose the action.","a":"Day after thyroidectomy; tingling, carpal spasm with BP cuff.","f":"Postoperative complication: hypocalcemia.","e":"Analgesia, paper bag, and documenting as expected miss it.","r":"Report now with calcium gluconate ready."},"principle":"Recognize a complication that can progress to airway compromise.","takeaway":"Tingling after thyroidectomy means low calcium until proven otherwise.","xv":2},{"id":"I01","ver":2,"subj":"Integumentary","topic":"Inhalation injury with a normal SpO₂","cn":"Physiological Adaptation","cn2":"Reduction of Risk Potential","fmt":"mc","diff":"A","ask":"Interpret","comp":"cue","stem":"A client rescued from a house fire in a closed room 30 minutes ago has singed nasal hair, soot around the mouth, and a hoarse voice. The client reports headache and nausea. SpO₂ is 98% on room air. Which action does the nurse anticipate first?","opts":["Reassure the client that oxygenation is adequate because SpO₂ is 98%","Apply cool ice packs to the face to limit swelling","Give 100% oxygen as ordered and watch closely for stridor","Begin teaching about burn wound care and infection prevention"],"key":2,"rat":"Singed facial hair, soot, hoarseness, and face or neck burns signal inhalation injury; stridor is life-threatening. A closed-room fire makes carbon monoxide poisoning likely, and its early signs include headache and nausea. Pulse oximetry cannot tell oxyhemoglobin from carboxyhemoglobin, so a normal SpO₂ does not rule it out; treatment is 100% oxygen. Ice is never applied to burns.","why":{"0":"Pulse oximeters cannot distinguish carboxyhemoglobin, so 98% can be falsely reassuring.","1":"Ice causes vasoconstriction and can deepen the burn.","3":"Teaching comes after the airway and oxygenation are secured."},"traps":{"0":"reassure","1":"unsafe","3":"later"},"cues":"Closed room, singed hair, soot, hoarseness, headache and nausea despite SpO₂ 98%.","aid":"Smoke in a closed room: the pulse ox can lie; trust the soot.","principle":"Know when a normal number is unreliable; let the cue cluster drive the decision.","takeaway":"A normal SpO₂ after smoke exposure does not clear the client. Headache and nausea in a closed-room fire point to carbon monoxide.","safer":{"s":"Choose the first anticipated action.","a":"Closed-room fire, singed nasal hair, soot, hoarseness, headache, nausea, SpO₂ 98%.","f":"Airway and oxygenation emergency (inhalation injury and CO poisoning).","e":"The SpO₂ is unreliable, ice is harmful, teaching is later.","r":"100% oxygen with airway monitoring beats reassurance because CO poisoning hides behind a normal SpO₂."},"compare":{"a":2,"b":0,"aWhy":"It treats carbon monoxide poisoning and watches for airway swelling.","bWhy":"An SpO₂ of 98% would normally mean adequate oxygenation.","decider":"Pulse oximetry cannot distinguish carboxyhemoglobin, and headache and nausea suggest CO poisoning.","flip":"With no smoke exposure and no inhalation signs, an SpO₂ of 98% would be reassuring."},"src":"Integumentary_Complete_Linear_Combined.pdf","pg":"pp. 82–109 (Burns: emergent phase, inhalation injury, CO poisoning)","sameFormExclude":[],"cog":"analysis"},{"id":"I02","subj":"Integumentary","topic":"Burn acute phase fluid shift","cn":"Reduction of Risk Potential","cn2":"Physiological Adaptation","fmt":"mc","diff":"I","stem":"On day 4 after a major burn, which change does the nurse expect as the client enters the acute phase?","opts":["Urine output decreases sharply","Urine output increases and edema begins to decrease","Fluid continues to shift out of the vessels into the tissues","Metabolism slows"],"key":1,"rat":"The acute phase begins about 72 hours after the burn, when fluid shifts back into the intravascular compartment. Urine output increases, edema decreases, and metabolism increases. Infection is the most common complication and cause of death after the first 72 hours.","why":{"0":"Output rises as fluid returns to circulation.","2":"This describes the emergent phase.","3":"Metabolism increases in the acute phase."},"cues":"After 72 hours.","aid":"Burn fluid is a tide: first 72 hours it goes out into the tissues; then it comes back in and out through the kidneys.","src":"Integumentary_Complete_Linear_Combined.pdf","pg":"pp. 82–109 (acute phase)","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Interpret","comp":"analysis","traps":{"0":"otherdx","2":"otherdx","3":"otherdx"},"safer":{"s":"Identify the expected change.","a":"Day 4 after a major burn.","f":"Burn phases: fluid shifts.","e":"Falling output and ongoing leak belong to the emergent phase; metabolism rises.","r":"Output rises and edema falls as fluid returns."},"principle":"Match the finding to the phase.","takeaway":"Burn fluid is a tide: out for 72 hours, then back in.","xv":2},{"id":"I03","subj":"Integumentary","topic":"Curling's ulcer","cn":"Reduction of Risk Potential","cn2":"","fmt":"mc","diff":"I","stem":"Ten days after severe burns, a client vomits bright red blood. Which complication does the nurse suspect?","opts":["Paralytic ileus","Curling's ulcer","Expected response to burn medications","Contracture"],"key":1,"rat":"Curling's ulcer is a duodenal ulcer that develops 8 to 14 days after severe burns; the first sign is usually vomiting bright red blood.","why":{"0":"Paralytic ileus causes gastric dilation, not bloody vomiting.","2":"Vomiting blood is never an expected response.","3":"Contractures affect muscle extension, not the GI tract."},"cues":"Day 8–14 + bright red vomitus.","aid":"Curling's ulcer 'curls' up in week 2: bleeding from the gut 8–14 days after a burn.","src":"Integumentary_Complete_Linear_Combined.pdf","pg":"pp. 82–109 (acute phase complications)","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Interpret","comp":"analysis","traps":{"0":"otherdx","2":"reassure","3":"otherdx"},"safer":{"s":"Name the complication.","a":"Day 10 after burns, bright red vomitus.","f":"Burn complications by timing.","e":"Ileus, expected drug effect, and contracture don't fit.","r":"Curling's ulcer appears days 8–14."},"principle":"Timing narrows the differential.","takeaway":"Curling's ulcer curls up in week 2.","xv":2},{"id":"I04","subj":"Integumentary","topic":"Scabies teaching","cn":"Safety and Infection Prevention and Control","cn2":"Health Promotion and Maintenance","fmt":"mc","diff":"F","stem":"A client is diagnosed with scabies. Which statement indicates a need for further teaching?","opts":["\"I'll wash my sheets and clothes in hot water and dry them on high heat.\"","\"Only I need to use the cream, since I'm the one who's itching.\"","\"I'll apply the medicine to my whole body.\"","\"Our dog doesn't need treatment.\""],"key":1,"rat":"Treatment extends to sexual and other close contacts, and each family member treats the whole body with a scabicide. Linens and clothing are washed in hot water and dried on the hot cycle; pets do not need treatment.","why":{"0":"Correct teaching.","2":"Correct teaching.","3":"Correct: treatment of pets is not necessary."},"cues":"\"Only I need...\"","aid":"Scabies is a household guest: everyone treats, everything gets hot-washed, the dog gets a pass.","src":"Integumentary_Complete_Linear_Combined.pdf","pg":"pp. 55–81 (Parasitic disorders)","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Evaluate","comp":"teach","traps":{"0":"irrelevant","2":"irrelevant","3":"irrelevant"},"safer":{"s":"Find the statement needing more teaching.","a":"Only the client will treat.","f":"Teaching evaluation: household treatment.","e":"Hot wash, whole-body application, no pet treatment are correct.","r":"Contacts must be treated too."},"principle":"Negative stems: find the one wrong statement.","takeaway":"Scabies: everyone treats, everything hot-washed.","xv":2},{"id":"I05","subj":"Integumentary","topic":"Tetanus prophylaxis with burns","cn":"Health Promotion and Maintenance","cn2":"Safety and Infection Prevention and Control","fmt":"mc","diff":"I","stem":"A client with a partial-thickness burn received a tetanus toxoid booster 3 years ago. What does the nurse anticipate?","opts":["A tetanus toxoid booster before discharge today","No booster, since the last one was under 5 years ago","Tetanus serum plus a series of active immunizations","A booster only if the burn wound becomes infected"],"key":1,"rat":"Immunized clients do not need a tetanus toxoid booster unless the last injection was more than 5 years ago. Clients never immunized receive tetanus serum and active immunization in the emergency department.","why":{"0":"Three years is within the 5-year window.","2":"Serum is for clients who were never immunized.","3":"The rule is based on time since the last dose, not infection."},"cues":"Last booster 3 years ago.","aid":"Burn + tetanus: 5-year rule. Under 5, you're covered.","src":"Integumentary_Complete_Linear_Combined.pdf","pg":"pp. 82–109 (emergency burn care)","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Interpret","comp":"analysis","traps":{"0":"otherdx","2":"otherdx","3":"irrelevant"},"safer":{"s":"Anticipate tetanus care.","a":"Booster 3 years ago.","f":"Tetanus prophylaxis rule.","e":"Booster now, serum, and infection-based rules don't fit.","r":"Under 5 years since the booster: none needed."},"principle":"Apply the rule to the exact interval.","takeaway":"Burn plus tetanus: the 5-year rule.","xv":2},{"id":"I06","subj":"Integumentary","topic":"Pressure injury staging","cn":"Reduction of Risk Potential","cn2":"","fmt":"mc","diff":"I","stem":"A sacral wound has full-thickness skin loss with visible subcutaneous fat. No bone, muscle, or tendon is exposed, and there is no eschar. How should the nurse document the stage?","opts":["Stage 1","Stage 2","Stage 3","Stage 4"],"key":2,"rat":"Stage 3 is full-thickness skin loss with damage to subcutaneous tissue, without exposed bone, muscle, or tendon. Stage 2 is partial-thickness loss with no fat visible, and Stage 4 exposes bone, muscle, or tendon.","why":{"0":"Stage 1 is intact skin with nonblanchable redness.","1":"Stage 2 is partial thickness, with no fatty tissue visible.","3":"Stage 4 exposes bone, muscle, or tendon."},"cues":"Fat visible, no bone.","aid":"Staging by depth: 1 red, 2 shallow, 3 fat, 4 bone.","src":"Fundamentals HESI review 3.pdf","pg":"p63","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Interpret","comp":"analysis","traps":{"0":"otherdx","1":"otherdx","3":"otherdx"},"safer":{"s":"Stage the wound.","a":"Full thickness, fat visible, no bone, muscle, or tendon.","f":"Pressure injury staging.","e":"Stages 1, 2, and 4 have different depths.","r":"Stage 3."},"principle":"Stage by the deepest tissue visible.","takeaway":"1 red, 2 shallow, 3 fat, 4 bone.","xv":2},{"id":"X01","subj":"Reproductive","topic":"Post-mastectomy arm protection","cn":"Coordinated Care","cn2":"Reduction of Risk Potential","fmt":"mc","diff":"F","stem":"A client had a left modified radical mastectomy with lymph node removal. Which action by a UAP requires the nurse to intervene?","opts":["Taking the blood pressure on the left arm","Placing the call light on the right side","Supporting the left arm on a pillow","Helping the client walk the first day after surgery"],"key":0,"rat":"Blood pressures, injections, IV fluids, and blood draws are avoided on the affected side because lymph node removal raises the risk of lymphedema and infection. Early ambulation is encouraged, and some providers prefer the arm propped on a pillow.","why":{"1":"Appropriate placement.","2":"A pillow can promote lymph fluid movement.","3":"Early ambulation is encouraged."},"cues":"Affected arm.","aid":"Mastectomy arm = 'no-stick, no-squeeze zone.'","src":"Reproductive_Health_Combined_Chapters.pdf","pg":"Ch. 52, postoperative complications; Box 52.6 (no page numbers in file)","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Scope","comp":"scope","traps":{"1":"irrelevant","2":"irrelevant","3":"irrelevant"},"safer":{"s":"Find the UAP action needing intervention.","a":"Left mastectomy with node removal; BP on left arm.","f":"Supervision: lymphedema prevention.","e":"Call light placement, pillow support, early ambulation are appropriate.","r":"BP on the affected arm must be stopped."},"principle":"Intervene on the unsafe action only.","takeaway":"Mastectomy arm: no stick, no squeeze.","xv":2},{"id":"X02","subj":"Reproductive","topic":"Alpha-1 blocker first-dose effect","cn":"Pharmacological Therapies","cn2":"Safety and Infection Prevention and Control","fmt":"mc","diff":"I","stem":"A client with benign prostatic hyperplasia is starting terazosin. Which statement shows understanding of the teaching?","opts":["\"I'll take my first dose on an empty stomach so it absorbs better.\"","\"If I feel dizzy after the first doses, I'll lie down right away.\"","\"I'll stop the medicine on my own if it makes me feel drowsy.\"","\"This drug will shrink my prostate so I can urinate more easily.\""],"key":1,"rat":"First doses of alpha-1 blockers can cause dizziness, hypotension, and fainting 15–90 minutes after the dose. Giving the first doses with food minimizes this, and the client lies down immediately if symptoms develop. Alpha-1 blockers relax muscle; they do not shrink the prostate.","why":{"0":"First doses are given with food.","2":"Drowsiness tends to be self-limiting; the client should not stop without consulting the provider.","3":"5-alpha reductase inhibitors shrink the prostate; alpha-1 blockers do not."},"cues":"","aid":"First dose: feed it and be ready to lie down.","src":"Reproductive_Health_Combined_Chapters.pdf","pg":"Ch. 40, alpha-1 blockers Safety Alert (no page numbers in file)","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Evaluate","comp":"med","traps":{"0":"unsafe","2":"unsafe","3":"otherdx"},"safer":{"s":"Find the statement showing understanding.","a":"Starting terazosin.","f":"Teaching evaluation: first-dose effect.","e":"Empty stomach, stopping for drowsiness, and shrinking the prostate are wrong.","r":"Lying down for dizziness shows understanding."},"principle":"Teach the predictable early adverse effect.","takeaway":"First dose: feed it and be ready to lie down.","med":{"why":"BPH symptoms.","check":"BP; fall risk.","give":"First doses with food.","watch":"Dizziness, hypotension, fainting 15–90 minutes after.","teach":"Lie down if dizzy; don't stop without asking."},"xv":2},{"id":"X03","subj":"Reproductive","topic":"Testicular self-examination","cn":"Health Promotion and Maintenance","cn2":"","fmt":"mc","diff":"F","stem":"The nurse reinforces testicular self-examination teaching with a 19-year-old. Which instruction is correct?","opts":["Perform it once a year during a routine physical exam.","Perform it monthly after a warm bath or shower.","Perform it only if pain or swelling develops in the scrotum.","Expect a normal testis to feel hard and fixed in place."],"key":1,"rat":"TSE is done monthly, beginning at puberty, after a warm bath or shower when the scrotum is relaxed. Normal testicles are firm but somewhat resilient, smooth, and mobile; a hardened testicle suggests carcinoma.","why":{"0":"TSE is monthly; professional exams are separate.","2":"Testicular cancer often presents as a painless mass.","3":"A hard, fixed testis is abnormal."},"cues":"","aid":"TSE: Monthly, after Shower, feel for Smooth.","src":"Reproductive_Health_Combined_Chapters.pdf","pg":"Ch. 52, Testicular cancer Patient Teaching (no page numbers in file)","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Teach","comp":"teach","traps":{"0":"otherdx","2":"unsafe","3":"unsafe"},"safer":{"s":"Choose the correct instruction.","a":"TSE teaching for a 19-year-old.","f":"Health promotion screening.","e":"Yearly, only with pain, and hard and fixed as normal are wrong.","r":"Monthly after a warm shower."},"principle":"Screening teaching includes timing and what normal feels like.","takeaway":"Monthly, after a shower, feel for smooth.","xv":2},{"id":"B01","subj":"Fundamentals","topic":"LVN scope: blood products","cn":"Coordinated Care","cn2":"","fmt":"mc","diff":"I","stem":"The charge nurse assigns the LVN four tasks. Which task should the LVN ask the RN to perform?","opts":["Give a scheduled IM opioid injection","Start a unit of packed red blood cells","Perform an indwelling urinary catheter insertion","Check a feeding tube for patency before a tube feeding"],"key":1,"rat":"On the instructor's scope slide, starting blood products is an RN task. The LPN/LVN monitors IV fluids and blood products, gives injections and narcotics (not IV medications or the first IV bag), performs routine procedures such as catheterization, and checks tube patency for enteral feedings.","why":{"0":"Injections and narcotics are in the PN column.","2":"Catheterization is a routine PN procedure.","3":"Tube patency and enteral feedings are in the PN column."},"cues":"\"Start\" a blood product.","aid":"RN starts it, LVN watches it: blood is started by the RN, monitored by the LVN.","src":"Fundamentals HESI review 3.pdf","pg":"p59","sameFormExclude":["XE18"],"ask":"Scope","comp":"scope","traps":{"0":"scope","2":"scope","3":"scope"},"safer":{"s":"Find the task to hand back to the RN.","a":"\"Start\" a blood product.","f":"Scope: RN versus PN columns.","e":"Injections, catheterization, and tube patency are PN tasks.","r":"Starting blood products is an RN task."},"principle":"Label the role before choosing.","takeaway":"RN starts blood; the LVN monitors it.","xv":2},{"id":"B02","subj":"Fundamentals","topic":"Assignment to the LVN","cn":"Coordinated Care","cn2":"","fmt":"mc","diff":"I","stem":"Which client is most appropriate for the RN to assign to the LVN?","opts":["A client just returned from surgery who needs the initial postoperative assessment","A newly admitted client who needs initial teaching about a new diagnosis","A stable client 2 days after surgery who needs a dressing change","A client being discharged today who needs discharge education"],"key":2,"rat":"The scope slide assigns the PN stable clients, routine procedures such as wound care, data gathering, and reinforcement of teaching. Postoperative assessment, initial client teaching, and discharge education are RN tasks.","why":{"0":"Initial postoperative assessment is an RN task.","1":"Initial teaching is an RN task; the PN reinforces it.","3":"Discharge education is an RN task."},"cues":"Stable + routine procedure.","aid":"RN does the 'firsts' (first assessment, first teaching, last teaching at discharge); LVN does the 'routine.'","src":"Fundamentals HESI review 3.pdf","pg":"p59","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Scope","comp":"scope","traps":{"0":"scope","1":"scope","3":"scope"},"safer":{"s":"Choose the client for the LVN.","a":"Stable client needing a dressing change.","f":"Assignment by stability and task type.","e":"Initial postop assessment, initial teaching, and discharge education are RN tasks.","r":"A stable client with a routine procedure fits the PN role."},"principle":"RN keeps the firsts and the lasts; LVN takes the stable and routine.","takeaway":"Stable plus routine = LVN.","xv":2},{"id":"B03","subj":"Fundamentals","topic":"SBAR components","cn":"Coordinated Care","cn2":"","fmt":"mc","diff":"F","stem":"The nurse calls the provider about a client whose condition has changed. Which statement belongs in the Recommendation part of SBAR?","opts":["\"This is Jamie, the LVN on 4 West, calling about Mr. Lee, date of birth 3/2/1951.\"","\"He was admitted yesterday with pneumonia and has a history of COPD.\"","\"I'm concerned his breathing is getting worse.\"","\"I'd like you to come evaluate him, and I'm asking whether you want a chest x-ray.\""],"key":3,"rat":"SBAR: Situation identifies the caller, unit, client, and symptom onset; Background gives admission date, diagnosis, history, and labs; Assessment states the suspected cause or concern; Recommendation states what the nurse recommends and expects.","why":{"0":"Situation.","1":"Background.","2":"Assessment."},"cues":"","aid":"SBAR: Situation (who/what now), Background (history), Assessment (what I think), Recommendation (what I want).","src":"Fundamentals HESI review 3.pdf","pg":"p67","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Interpret","comp":"scope","traps":{"0":"otherdx","1":"otherdx","2":"otherdx"},"safer":{"s":"Identify the Recommendation statement.","a":"Four SBAR statements.","f":"Communication: SBAR structure.","e":"Caller ID is Situation, history is Background, concern is Assessment.","r":"Asking the provider to evaluate and order a test is the Recommendation."},"principle":"Know what each part of a structured handoff contains.","takeaway":"R = what I want you to do.","xv":2},{"id":"B04","subj":"Fundamentals","topic":"Hospice eligibility","cn":"Coordinated Care","cn2":"Psychosocial Integrity","fmt":"mc","diff":"F","stem":"A family asks when hospice care is typically offered. Which response is accurate?","opts":["\"Only during the last 48 hours of life, when death is very near.\"","\"Usually when someone terminally ill is expected to live 6 months or less.\"","\"Only after all treatment has failed and the person is in a coma.\"","\"Only to people who have no family caregivers at home to help them.\""],"key":1,"rat":"Hospice is typically offered to people with a terminal illness expected to live 6 months or less if the disease runs its normal course. Its goals include managing pain and symptoms, emotional and spiritual support, and helping families cope.","why":{"0":"Hospice begins much earlier than the final days.","2":"No such requirement exists in the source.","3":"Hospice supports families; it does not require their absence."},"cues":"","aid":"Hospice: the 6-month window for comfort, not cure.","src":"Fundamentals HESI review 3.pdf","pg":"pp40–42","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Teach","comp":"teach","traps":{"0":"otherdx","2":"unsafe","3":"unsafe"},"safer":{"s":"Choose the accurate response.","a":"Family asks when hospice is offered.","f":"End-of-life care facts.","e":"Last 48 hours, coma only, and no caregivers are inaccurate.","r":"Terminal illness with 6 months or less expected."},"principle":"Give accurate information without false limits.","takeaway":"Hospice: the 6-month window for comfort.","xv":2},{"id":"B05","subj":"Fundamentals","topic":"Nurse practice act","cn":"Coordinated Care","cn2":"","fmt":"mc","diff":"F","stem":"A new LVN asks who is responsible for knowing what the LVN is legally allowed to do in California. What is the best answer?","opts":["\"The charge nurse decides your scope each shift through the assignment.\"","\"Each nurse must know the nurse practice act of the state where they work.\"","\"Your nursing school's policies set your legal scope after graduation.\"","\"Scope is the same in every state, so any national textbook will do.\""],"key":1,"rat":"A nurse practice act is a set of statutes enacted by a state legislature to regulate nursing in that state. Each nurse is responsible for knowing the provisions of the act for the state in which he or she works.","why":{"0":"Assignments cannot expand legal scope.","2":"School policy does not define legal scope of practice.","3":"Practice acts are state-specific."},"cues":"","aid":"Your license, your state, your responsibility.","src":"Fundamentals HESI review 3.pdf","pg":"p58","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Scope","comp":"scope","traps":{"0":"scope","2":"otherdx","3":"unsafe"},"safer":{"s":"Choose who is responsible for scope.","a":"Legal scope in California.","f":"Legal: nurse practice act.","e":"Charge nurse, school policy, and 'same everywhere' are wrong.","r":"Each nurse knows the practice act of the state where they work."},"principle":"Assignments never expand legal scope.","takeaway":"Your license, your state, your responsibility.","xv":2},{"id":"B06","subj":"Fundamentals","topic":"Documenting an enema","cn":"Coordinated Care","cn2":"Basic Care and Comfort","fmt":"mc","diff":"I","stem":"Which entry is the most complete documentation of an enema?","opts":["\"Enema given at 0900 per order. Good results noted. Client resting.\"","\"Tap-water enema given per order at 0900. Client tolerated procedure well.\"","\"1000 mL tap-water enema at 0900, held 10 min; large soft brown stool; no cramping.\"","\"Client had a large bowel movement after the enema and is now comfortable.\""],"key":2,"rat":"Documentation includes date and time, amount and type of solution, how long it was retained, amount, color, and consistency of the results, and any difficulties or the client's tolerance.","why":{"0":"Lacks time, solution, retention, and result details.","1":"Lacks solution, retention time, and results.","3":"Lacks almost all required details."},"cues":"","aid":"Enema charting: When, What & how much, How long held, What came out, How they tolerated it.","src":"Fundamentals HESI review 3.pdf","pg":"p23","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Intervene","comp":"intervention","traps":{"0":"irrelevant","1":"irrelevant","3":"irrelevant"},"safer":{"s":"Choose the most complete note.","a":"Enema documentation elements.","f":"Documentation standards.","e":"The others omit time, solution, retention, and results.","r":"The full entry covers every required element."},"principle":"Complete documentation is specific and measurable.","takeaway":"When, what and how much, how long, what came out, how tolerated.","xv":2},{"id":"B07","subj":"Fundamentals","topic":"Prioritizing clients","cn":"Coordinated Care","cn2":"Physiological Adaptation","fmt":"mc","diff":"A","stem":"After shift report, which client should the LVN see first?","opts":["A client with COPD whose SpO₂ is 89% at rest and who reports usual shortness of breath","A client the day after thyroidectomy who reports tingling around the mouth and fingers","A client with a new cast who reports itching under the cast","A client who rates incision pain 4/10 and asks when the next dose is due"],"key":1,"rat":"Tingling of the fingers and mouth after thyroidectomy signals hypocalcemia from parathyroid damage, which can progress to tetany and laryngeal spasm. The vital-signs slide notes that clients with COPD are expected to run oxygen levels as low as 88%.","why":{"0":"89% is within the expected range for COPD on the instructor's slide, and the dyspnea is the client's usual.","2":"Itching is uncomfortable but not an emergency.","3":"Pain is addressed after the client at risk for airway compromise."},"cues":"Post-thyroidectomy + perioral tingling.","aid":"First see the client whose problem can close an airway.","src":"Med_Surg_All_Sources_Linear_Master_2026.pdf","pg":"pp. 458–500; Fundamentals p73","sameFormExclude":["E06"],"ask":"Prioritize","comp":"priority","traps":{"0":"isolated","2":"lower","3":"lower"},"safer":{"s":"Choose the client to see first.","a":"Perioral and finger tingling after thyroidectomy; COPD at usual SpO₂; cast itching; pain 4/10.","f":"Priority ladder: new and potentially airway-threatening.","e":"COPD baseline is expected, itching and moderate pain are lower.","r":"Post-thyroidectomy tingling can progress to laryngeal spasm."},"principle":"New and potentially dangerous outranks chronic and expected.","takeaway":"See first the client whose problem can close an airway.","compare":{"a":1,"b":0,"aWhy":"Tingling signals hypocalcemia that can progress to tetany and laryngeal spasm.","bWhy":"An SpO₂ of 89% looks low.","decider":"The COPD client is at a stated baseline and the slide accepts SpO₂ as low as 88%; the tingling is new.","flip":"If the COPD client's SpO₂ were dropping from baseline with new distress, that client could come first."},"xv":2},{"id":"B08","subj":"Renal/Urinary","topic":"Prioritizing clients on dialysis","cn":"Coordinated Care","cn2":"Physiological Adaptation","fmt":"mc","diff":"I","stem":"Which client should the nurse assess first?","opts":["A client on hemodialysis whose AV fistula has a palpable thrill and audible bruit","A client on peritoneal dialysis with cloudy outflow, abdominal pain, and a fever of 101°F","A client taking phenazopyridine who reports reddish-orange urine","A client with chronic renal failure asking about the day's fluid allowance"],"key":1,"rat":"Cloudy dialysate with pain and fever signals peritonitis, a serious complication. A thrill and bruit are expected and show a patent fistula, and reddish-orange urine is an expected phenazopyridine effect.","why":{"0":"Expected finding.","2":"Expected drug effect.","3":"Routine teaching need."},"cues":"Cloudy + fever + pain.","aid":"Expected findings can wait; infection can't.","src":"Urinary_System_Chapters_1-6.pdf","pg":"pp91–93; Pain_Management_and_Urinary p58","sameFormExclude":["R02"],"ask":"Prioritize","comp":"priority","traps":{"0":"reassure","2":"reassure","3":"lower"},"safer":{"s":"Choose the client to assess first.","a":"Cloudy outflow, pain, fever; expected thrill and bruit; orange urine; fluid question.","f":"Expected versus complication.","e":"Thrill and bruit and orange urine are expected; the fluid question is routine.","r":"Signs of peritonitis come first."},"principle":"Complications outrank expected findings.","takeaway":"Expected findings can wait; infection can't.","xv":2},{"id":"B09","subj":"Reproductive","topic":"Consent before cystoscopy","cn":"Coordinated Care","cn2":"","fmt":"mc","diff":"F","stem":"A client scheduled for cystoscopy says, \"I'm not sure what they're going to do to me.\" What should the nurse do?","opts":["Have the client sign the consent form now so the procedure isn't delayed.","Notify the provider that the client needs the procedure explained first.","Tell the client it's a minor test and there is nothing to worry about.","Ask a family member to sign the consent form instead of the client."],"key":1,"rat":"Before cystoscopy, a signed consent form is obtained and the patient is educated about the procedure. A client who does not understand the procedure has not given informed consent, so the provider must explain it before the form is signed.","why":{"0":"Signing without understanding is not informed consent.","2":"False reassurance does not provide information.","3":"A capable adult consents for himself or herself."},"cues":"\"I'm not sure what they're going to do.\"","aid":"No understanding, no signature.","src":"Reproductive_Health_Combined_Chapters.pdf","pg":"Ch. 52, Cystoscopy (no page numbers in file); Fundamentals pp71–72","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Intervene","comp":"scope","traps":{"0":"unsafe","2":"reassure","3":"scope"},"safer":{"s":"Choose the action.","a":"\"I'm not sure what they're going to do to me.\"","f":"Informed consent.","e":"Signing anyway, reassurance, and family consent all fail informed consent.","r":"The provider must explain before the client signs."},"principle":"Consent requires understanding; the provider explains.","takeaway":"No understanding, no signature.","xv":2},{"id":"B10","subj":"Maternal-Newborn","topic":"Infant abduction prevention","cn":"Safety and Infection Prevention and Control","cn2":"","fmt":"mc","diff":"I","stem":"A woman in scrubs with no visible identification badge comes to a postpartum room and says she needs to take the baby to the nursery. What should the nurse do?","opts":["Allow it, because she is wearing hospital scrubs and knows the unit routine","Refuse to release the infant and alert hospital security right away","Let her take the baby if the mother says it is all right with her","Walk with her to the nursery while you carry the infant yourself"],"key":1,"rat":"No one without appropriate identification handles or transports infants, and parents are taught to give their infant only to staff with proper identification. Suspicious activity is reported to security immediately. Abductors often impersonate staff.","why":{"0":"Scrubs are not identification.","2":"Parental agreement does not replace identification.","3":"The infant should not be released to an unidentified person in any form."},"cues":"No ID badge.","aid":"No badge, no baby.","src":"Maternal_Health_Linear_Chapter_Sources_Updated.pdf","pg":"pp. 157–168 (Box 28.12)","sameFormExclude":["B11","V08"],"ask":"Intervene","comp":"intervention","traps":{"0":"unsafe","2":"unsafe","3":"unsafe"},"safer":{"s":"Choose the action.","a":"Scrubs, no ID badge, asking to take the baby.","f":"Infant security.","e":"Allowing it for scrubs or the mother's okay, or escorting, releases the infant.","r":"Refuse and alert security."},"principle":"Identification, not appearance, authorizes infant transport.","takeaway":"No badge, no baby.","xv":2},{"id":"B11","subj":"Maternal-Newborn","topic":"Infant identification","cn":"Safety and Infection Prevention and Control","cn2":"","fmt":"mc","diff":"F","stem":"The father comes to the nursery to take his newborn back to the mother's room. What must the nurse do before releasing the infant?","opts":["Ask him to state the baby's full name and date of birth","Match the bracelet numbers on the father and the infant","Check that he knows the mother's name and room number","Ask him to show a photo of himself with the mother"],"key":1,"rat":"Matching ID bracelets are placed on the mother, the infant, and the father or another person designated by the mother. When a parent comes to take an infant, the nurse always matches the infant and adult bracelet numbers.","why":{"0":"Names can be learned by anyone.","2":"Room numbers can be learned by anyone.","3":"Photos do not verify identity."},"cues":"","aid":"Bracelet to bracelet: numbers must match.","src":"Maternal_Health_Linear_Chapter_Sources_Updated.pdf","pg":"pp. 91–131 (identification); pp. 157–168 (Box 28.12)","sameFormExclude":["B10","V08"],"ask":"Intervene","comp":"intervention","traps":{"0":"unsafe","2":"unsafe","3":"unsafe"},"safer":{"s":"Choose the required check.","a":"Father picking up the newborn.","f":"Infant identification.","e":"Names, room numbers, and photos can be learned or faked.","r":"Matching bracelet numbers verifies identity."},"principle":"Verify with the system designed for it.","takeaway":"Bracelet to bracelet.","xv":2},{"id":"B12","subj":"Fundamentals","topic":"Sharps disposal","cn":"Safety and Infection Prevention and Control","cn2":"","fmt":"mc","diff":"F","stem":"After giving an IM injection, what should the nurse do with the used needle and syringe?","opts":["Recap the needle carefully and place it on the medication cart.","Dispose of it at once in a puncture-resistant sharps container.","Place it in the client's trash can lined with a heavy plastic bag.","Bend the needle so that no one else can reuse it."],"key":1,"rat":"All sharps are disposed of immediately after use in a closed, puncture-resistant, leakproof, labeled or color-coded container, and infectious materials are handled as hazards.","why":{"0":"Leaving sharps on a cart risks needlestick injury.","2":"Regular trash is not puncture-resistant.","3":"Manipulating a used needle risks injury."},"cues":"","aid":"Sharps go straight to the sharps box, no detours.","src":"Fundamentals HESI review 3.pdf","pg":"p7","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Intervene","comp":"intervention","traps":{"0":"unsafe","2":"unsafe","3":"unsafe"},"safer":{"s":"Choose sharps disposal.","a":"Used needle after IM injection.","f":"Safety: sharps handling.","e":"Recapping, trash cans, and bending risk injury.","r":"Immediate disposal in a sharps container."},"principle":"Remove the hazard immediately.","takeaway":"Sharps go straight to the sharps box.","xv":2},{"id":"B13","subj":"Fundamentals","topic":"Bed-to-chair transfer","cn":"Safety and Infection Prevention and Control","cn2":"Basic Care and Comfort","fmt":"mc","diff":"F","stem":"The nurse observes a UAP preparing to move a weak client from the bed to a chair. Which action shows safe technique?","opts":["Raising the bed to its highest setting to save the UAP's back","Lowering the bed to its lowest setting and using a gait belt","Pulling the client up by the arms","Leaving the wheelchair brakes unlocked so it can roll closer"],"key":1,"rat":"For bed-to-chair transfers the bed is set at its lowest setting, a gait belt is used for added support, brakes are engaged, and the client assists as much as possible.","why":{"0":"A high bed increases fall risk.","2":"Pulling by the arms risks injury.","3":"Brakes are engaged during a transfer."},"cues":"","aid":"Low bed, locked brakes, gait belt on.","src":"Fundamentals HESI review 3.pdf","pg":"pp55, 77–80","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Scope","comp":"scope","traps":{"0":"unsafe","2":"unsafe","3":"unsafe"},"safer":{"s":"Choose safe transfer technique.","a":"Weak client, bed to chair.","f":"Safety: transfers.","e":"High bed, pulling arms, unlocked brakes cause falls.","r":"Low bed with a gait belt."},"principle":"Supervising a transfer means checking height, brakes, and support.","takeaway":"Low bed, locked brakes, gait belt on.","xv":2},{"id":"B14","subj":"Pediatrics","topic":"Restraint (safety reminder device) care","cn":"Safety and Infection Prevention and Control","cn2":"","fmt":"mc","diff":"I","stem":"A toddler has elbow restraints to protect an IV site. Which action is correct?","opts":["Remove the restraints only once per shift for skin care and bathing.","Check skin and circulation and provide range of motion every 2 hours.","Keep the restraints on even after the IV is removed, to be safe.","Tie the restraints to the side rails so they stay snug."],"key":1,"rat":"Safety reminder devices are used only when necessary with frequent reassessment; skin and circulation are monitored and range of motion is provided every 2 hours.","why":{"0":"Once per shift is too infrequent.","2":"Restraints are used only when necessary.","3":"No source supports this; the focus is skin, circulation, and ROM checks."},"cues":"","aid":"Restraints run on a 2-hour clock: skin, circulation, motion.","src":"Pediatric_Nursing_Complete_Linear_Study_Packet.pdf","pg":"Common Procedures: Restraints (no page numbers in file)","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Intervene","comp":"intervention","traps":{"0":"unsafe","2":"unsafe","3":"unsafe"},"safer":{"s":"Choose correct restraint care.","a":"Toddler with elbow restraints.","f":"Safety: restraint monitoring.","e":"Once a shift, keeping them on after the IV, tying to rails are wrong.","r":"Skin, circulation, and ROM every 2 hours."},"principle":"Restraints require frequent checks and removal when no longer needed.","takeaway":"Restraints run on a 2-hour clock.","xv":2},{"id":"B15","subj":"Maternal-Newborn","topic":"Standard precautions at birth","cn":"Safety and Infection Prevention and Control","cn2":"","fmt":"mc","diff":"F","stem":"Which action follows standard precautions during and right after a vaginal birth?","opts":["Handle the newborn without gloves at first to promote skin contact and bonding.","Wear gloves and a gown when handling the newborn right after birth.","Wear gloves only if blood is visible on the newborn's skin.","Skip protective eyewear because the birth is low risk and routine."],"key":1,"rat":"During birth nurses are exposed to maternal and newborn blood and body fluids. Standard precautions include gloves and a gown when handling the newborn immediately after birth, and a cover gown and mask with shield or protective eyewear when assisting.","why":{"0":"The newborn is covered in maternal fluids right after birth.","2":"Precautions apply regardless of visible blood.","3":"Eye protection is worn when assisting with a birth."},"cues":"","aid":"Birth is a splash zone: gown, gloves, eyes.","src":"Maternal_Health_Linear_Chapter_Sources_Updated.pdf","pg":"pp. 91–131 (standard precautions for childbirth)","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Intervene","comp":"intervention","traps":{"0":"unsafe","2":"unsafe","3":"unsafe"},"safer":{"s":"Choose the standard-precaution action.","a":"Vaginal birth.","f":"Infection control: standard precautions.","e":"No gloves, gloves only with visible blood, no eyewear are wrong.","r":"Gloves and gown when handling the newborn right after birth."},"principle":"Standard precautions apply to all contact with body fluids.","takeaway":"Birth is a splash zone: gown, gloves, eyes.","xv":2},{"id":"B16","subj":"Fundamentals","topic":"Right documentation","cn":"Safety and Infection Prevention and Control","cn2":"Pharmacological Therapies","fmt":"mc","diff":"F","stem":"A nurse gives a scheduled medication but forgets to chart it. Another nurse gives a second dose an hour later. Which right of medication administration was missed first?","opts":["Right route of administration","Right time of administration","Right documentation","Right client identification"],"key":2,"rat":"The instructor's six rights are right client, time, dose, medication, route, and documentation. Failing to document the first dose led to the duplicate dose.","why":{"0":"The route was not the problem.","1":"The first dose was on time; the second was a consequence of missing documentation.","3":"The correct client received both doses."},"cues":"","aid":"Not charted = not given, in everyone else's eyes.","src":"Fundamentals HESI review 3.pdf","pg":"p33","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Interpret","comp":"med","traps":{"0":"irrelevant","1":"otherdx","3":"irrelevant"},"safer":{"s":"Identify the right that was missed first.","a":"First dose not charted, then given again.","f":"Medication rights.","e":"Route and client were correct; time was a consequence.","r":"Documentation was missed first."},"principle":"Find the first failure in the chain.","takeaway":"Not charted = not given, in everyone else's eyes.","xv":2},{"id":"B17","subj":"Maternal-Newborn","topic":"Postpartum psychosocial red flags","cn":"Psychosocial Integrity","cn2":"","fmt":"mc","diff":"I","stem":"Which postpartum finding should the nurse report for further psychosocial evaluation?","opts":["Mood swings and tearfulness on day 3","On day 1, the mother wants to rest and lets others care for the baby","The mother refuses to hold the baby and has not named him","The mother wants to talk about her labor several times"],"key":2,"rat":"Refusal to interact with or care for the baby, including not naming or holding him, is a sign of a potential psychosocial problem needing further evaluation. Mood swings between days 2 and 7, passive taking-in behavior in the first day or two, and retelling the birth story are common.","why":{"0":"Mood swings are common early postpartum.","1":"Taking-in behavior is expected in the first 1–2 days.","3":"Retelling the birth experience is common and helpful."},"cues":"No holding, no name.","aid":"Normal: tired, teary, talkative. Not normal: detached from the baby.","src":"Maternal_Health_Linear_Chapter_Sources_Updated.pdf","pg":"pp. 157–168 (Box 28.11; taking-in phase)","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Prioritize","comp":"cue","traps":{"0":"reassure","1":"reassure","3":"reassure"},"safer":{"s":"Choose the finding to report.","a":"Refuses to hold the baby; hasn't named him.","f":"Postpartum psychosocial assessment.","e":"Mood swings, taking-in behavior, and retelling the birth are expected.","r":"Detachment from the baby needs evaluation."},"principle":"Know expected adjustment so deviations stand out.","takeaway":"Tired, teary, talkative is normal; detached is not.","xv":2},{"id":"B18","subj":"Maternal-Newborn","topic":"Taking-in and taking-hold","cn":"Psychosocial Integrity","cn2":"Health Promotion and Maintenance","fmt":"mc","diff":"I","stem":"On the first postpartum day, a mother is passive, focused on her own comfort, and asks the nurse to change the baby. When will teaching about newborn care most likely be effective?","opts":["Right now, before she gets used to letting others care for the baby","When she enters the taking-hold stage and begins assuming responsibility","Only after discharge, at the 6-week postpartum visit","Teaching should be skipped because she is showing no interest"],"key":1,"rat":"In the taking-in stage of the first 1–2 days, the mother is passive and focuses on personal needs. In the taking-hold stage she assumes greater responsibility for herself and her baby, making her more ready to learn.","why":{"0":"She is focused on her own needs right now.","2":"Teaching is paced through the hospital stay.","3":"Readiness changes quickly; teaching is timed, not skipped."},"cues":"Day 1 + passive.","aid":"Taking-in = receiving care; taking-hold = taking charge. Teach when she takes hold.","src":"Maternal_Health_Linear_Chapter_Sources_Updated.pdf","pg":"pp. 157–168 (Ch. 28)","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Teach","comp":"teach","traps":{"0":"premature","2":"later","3":"unsafe"},"safer":{"s":"Choose when teaching will work best.","a":"Day 1, passive, focused on own needs.","f":"Readiness to learn: maternal phases.","e":"Now is too early, after discharge too late, skipping is wrong.","r":"Teach when she enters taking-hold."},"principle":"Time teaching to readiness.","takeaway":"Teach when she takes hold.","xv":2},{"id":"B19","subj":"Pediatrics","topic":"Regression during hospitalization","cn":"Psychosocial Integrity","cn2":"","fmt":"mc","diff":"F","stem":"The parent of a hospitalized, toilet-trained 3-year-old is upset that the child has started wetting his pants. What is the nurse's best response?","opts":["\"He may need to be toilet trained again from the very beginning.\"","\"A hospital stay can cause temporary regression; this is common.\"","\"Wetting like this usually means he has a urinary tract infection.\"","\"Punishing accidents now will keep them from becoming a habit.\""],"key":1,"rat":"Disruption of routine during hospitalization may cause regression; the nurse provides psychosocial and emotional support and a child-friendly environment.","why":{"0":"Regression is usually temporary.","2":"Nothing suggests infection.","3":"Physical punishment and humiliation are avoided."},"cues":"","aid":"Hospital stress = developmental step back; comfort brings the step forward again.","src":"Pediatric_Nursing_Complete_Linear_Study_Packet.pdf","pg":"Hospitalization Impacts (no page numbers in file)","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Teach","comp":"teach","traps":{"0":"unsafe","2":"otherdx","3":"unsafe"},"safer":{"s":"Choose the best response to the parent.","a":"Toilet-trained 3-year-old wetting during hospitalization.","f":"Development: regression under stress.","e":"Retraining, infection, and punishment are wrong.","r":"Explaining temporary regression reassures accurately."},"principle":"Explain expected developmental responses to stress.","takeaway":"Hospital stress causes a temporary step back.","xv":2},{"id":"B20","subj":"Reproductive","topic":"Body image after mastectomy","cn":"Psychosocial Integrity","cn2":"","fmt":"mc","diff":"I","stem":"Two days after a mastectomy, a client avoids looking at her chest during dressing changes. Which nursing approach is most helpful?","opts":["Cover the incision quickly so she doesn't have to look at it yet","Offer a mirror and involve her in the dressing change at her own pace","Reassure her that the scar will be nearly invisible within a few weeks","Suggest that she start wearing a breast prosthesis right away"],"key":1,"rat":"Patients often grieve the loss of a body part. Involving the patient in dressing changes and offering a mirror encourages processing the loss, and the nurse explains that acceptance takes time. A prosthesis is worn only when authorized by the provider.","why":{"0":"Avoidance delays adaptation.","2":"False reassurance.","3":"A prosthesis requires provider authorization and does not address the grief."},"cues":"","aid":"See it, touch it, accept it — at her pace.","src":"Reproductive_Health_Combined_Chapters.pdf","pg":"Ch. 52, mastectomy nursing care (no page numbers in file)","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Intervene","comp":"intervention","traps":{"0":"unsafe","2":"reassure","3":"scope"},"safer":{"s":"Choose the most helpful approach.","a":"Avoids looking at the incision 2 days after mastectomy.","f":"Body image and grief.","e":"Covering it, false reassurance, and an unauthorized prosthesis don't help.","r":"Mirror and participation at her pace support adaptation."},"principle":"Support gradual acceptance; avoid reinforcing avoidance.","takeaway":"See it, touch it, accept it, at her pace.","xv":2},{"id":"B21","subj":"Psychiatric/Mental Health","topic":"Suspected impaired colleague","cn":"Coordinated Care","cn2":"Safety and Infection Prevention and Control","fmt":"mc","diff":"A","stem":"Over several weeks the LVN notices that a coworker's narcotic counts are often off, the coworker offers to medicate other nurses' clients for pain, and clients report poor pain relief on that nurse's shifts. What is the best action?","opts":["Confront the coworker privately and ask whether they are using drugs.","Report the specific observations objectively to the supervisor.","Say nothing until there is proof, to avoid damaging the coworker's career.","Start double-checking the coworker's narcotic documentation without telling anyone."],"key":1,"rat":"Discrepancies in narcotic counts, offers to medicate coworkers' clients, and clients reporting less pain relief with a particular nurse are listed indications of drug diversion. Every nurse has a duty to report suspect behavior; observations are reported objectively to supervisors, and the supervisor or board contacts the peer assistance program.","why":{"0":"Confronting the coworker is not the reporting path the source describes.","2":"Ignoring or covering up the problem puts clients at risk; proof is not required to report observations.","3":"Secret monitoring does not protect clients and delays reporting."},"cues":"Count discrepancies + offers to medicate others' clients + poor pain relief.","aid":"See it, write it, report it up: objective facts to the supervisor.","src":"Psychiatric_Nursing_Complete_Linear_Study_Packet.pdf","pg":"pp. 328–414 (Section 04, Ch. 36 notes; Box 36.10)","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Intervene","comp":"scope","traps":{"0":"otherdx","2":"delay","3":"delay"},"safer":{"s":"Choose the best action.","a":"Count discrepancies, offers to medicate others' clients, poor pain relief on that nurse's shifts.","f":"Professional duty: suspected impairment and diversion.","e":"Confronting, waiting for proof, and secret monitoring are wrong.","r":"Objective report to the supervisor protects clients."},"principle":"Report objective observations through the proper channel.","takeaway":"See it, write it, report it up.","compare":{"a":1,"b":0,"aWhy":"It puts the facts in front of the person responsible for acting.","bWhy":"Talking to the coworker directly feels honest and supportive.","decider":"The source directs reporting observations objectively to supervisors; confronting is not the described path.","flip":"No version of these cues makes confronting the coworker the safest first step; reporting comes first."},"xv":2},{"id":"B22","subj":"Psychiatric/Mental Health","topic":"Duty to warn","cn":"Coordinated Care","cn2":"Safety and Infection Prevention and Control","fmt":"mc","diff":"I","stem":"During a one-on-one session, a client on the psychiatric unit says, \"When I get out, I'm going to kill my landlord.\" What is the nurse's responsibility?","opts":["Keep the statement confidential to protect the therapeutic relationship.","Notify the appropriate authorities as required by the duty to warn.","Document the statement and take no further action unless it is repeated.","Tell the client that such statements are not allowed on the unit."],"key":1,"rat":"The nurse has a duty to warn authorities of danger. When a patient expresses an intent to kill someone upon release, the nurse must notify the appropriate authorities; families of victims have won lawsuits against health care workers who did not warn.","why":{"0":"Confidentiality has limits when someone is in danger.","2":"A single statement of intent to kill triggers the duty to warn.","3":"Scolding does not address the threat."},"cues":"Specific intent to kill a named person on release.","aid":"A threat breaks the seal: confidentiality stops where danger starts.","src":"Psychiatric_Nursing_Complete_Linear_Study_Packet.pdf","pg":"pp. 66–82 (limits of confidentiality)","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Intervene","comp":"scope","traps":{"0":"unsafe","2":"delay","3":"irrelevant"},"safer":{"s":"Choose the nurse's responsibility.","a":"Stated intent to kill a named person after release.","f":"Legal: duty to warn.","e":"Confidentiality, waiting for repetition, and scolding fail the duty.","r":"Notify the appropriate authorities."},"principle":"Confidentiality ends where danger to others begins.","takeaway":"A threat breaks the seal.","xv":2},{"id":"B23","subj":"Psychiatric/Mental Health","topic":"Team confidentiality","cn":"Coordinated Care","cn2":"Psychosocial Integrity","fmt":"mc","diff":"I","stem":"A client on the psychiatric unit says, \"I'll tell you something important, but only if you promise not to tell the other staff.\" What is the nurse's best response?","opts":["\"I promise I won't tell anyone. You can trust me with this.\"","\"I can't promise that; what you share may go to the team so we can help.\"","\"Then it's better if you don't tell me anything at all.\"","\"Only tell me if it's about your medications or your treatment.\""],"key":1,"rat":"The patient must know that health care team members share information with each other. No member of the team keeps secrets from the others, which curtails manipulating the staff and pitting one staff member against another.","why":{"0":"Promising secrecy is not possible and invites manipulation.","2":"Shuts down communication.","3":"Limits communication arbitrarily."},"cues":"\"Promise not to tell.\"","aid":"No secrets on the team: one team, one story.","src":"Psychiatric_Nursing_Complete_Linear_Study_Packet.pdf","pg":"pp. 66–82 (limits of confidentiality)","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Intervene","comp":"scope","traps":{"0":"unsafe","2":"unsafe","3":"irrelevant"},"safer":{"s":"Choose the best response.","a":"\"Promise not to tell the other staff.\"","f":"Therapeutic communication: team confidentiality.","e":"Promising secrecy, shutting down, and limiting topics are wrong.","r":"Honestly explain that information is shared with the team."},"principle":"Be honest about limits of confidentiality.","takeaway":"One team, one story.","xv":2},{"id":"B24","subj":"Psychiatric/Mental Health","topic":"Objective behavioral documentation","cn":"Coordinated Care","cn2":"Psychosocial Integrity","fmt":"mc","diff":"I","stem":"Which nurse's note best follows guidelines for documenting a client's behavior?","opts":["\"Client appears depressed and withdrawn this morning.\"","\"Client seems to be in a bad mood today and is not talkative.\"","\"No eye contact. States, 'I don't see the point of getting up anymore.'\"","\"Client is being difficult and uncooperative with morning care.\""],"key":2,"rat":"Notes should be accurate and descriptive. Avoid terms such as \"appears depressed\"; specify behaviors such as \"no eye contact noted during conversation.\" Documenting the patient's actual conversation is valuable.","why":{"0":"The source names this as a term to avoid.","1":"Vague and interpretive.","3":"Judgmental and nonspecific."},"cues":"","aid":"Chart what a camera and a microphone would catch.","src":"Psychiatric_Nursing_Complete_Linear_Study_Packet.pdf","pg":"pp. 66–82 (documentation methods)","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Intervene","comp":"intervention","traps":{"0":"irrelevant","1":"irrelevant","3":"irrelevant"},"safer":{"s":"Choose the best behavioral note.","a":"Four documentation styles.","f":"Documentation: objective and descriptive.","e":"\"Appears depressed,\" \"bad mood,\" and \"difficult\" are interpretive.","r":"Observed behavior plus the client's words."},"principle":"Document what you see and hear, not conclusions.","takeaway":"Chart what a camera and microphone would catch.","xv":2},{"id":"B25","subj":"Pediatrics","topic":"Discharge referral for continuity of care","cn":"Coordinated Care","cn2":"Health Promotion and Maintenance","fmt":"mc","diff":"F","stem":"A school-age child is being discharged after a subtotal thyroidectomy for hyperthyroidism. Which action best supports continuity of care?","opts":["Tell the family to call the unit with any questions after they get home.","Make a referral to a public health, school, or home health nurse.","Schedule the child for daily lab draws at the clinic for a month.","Advise the family to keep the child home from school indefinitely."],"key":1,"rat":"Upon discharge, a referral is made to a public health nurse, school nurse, or home health care nurse to facilitate continuity of care and increase compliance with the medical regimen.","why":{"0":"Passive and does not ensure follow-up.","2":"Not supported by the source.","3":"Not supported by the source."},"cues":"Discharge + continuity.","aid":"Discharge isn't goodbye, it's a hand-off: refer to the next nurse.","src":"PEDS_Complete_Linear_Word_Sources_2026.pdf","pg":"pp. 211–258 (Hyperthyroidism)","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Intervene","comp":"scope","traps":{"0":"irrelevant","2":"unsafe","3":"unsafe"},"safer":{"s":"Choose the action supporting continuity.","a":"Discharge after thyroidectomy.","f":"Coordinated care: referral.","e":"Passive calls, daily labs, and keeping the child home are unsupported.","r":"Referral to a public health, school, or home health nurse."},"principle":"Continuity means a hand-off to the next caregiver.","takeaway":"Discharge isn't goodbye; it's a hand-off.","xv":2},{"id":"B26","subj":"Maternal-Newborn","topic":"Postpartum length-of-stay law","cn":"Coordinated Care","cn2":"","fmt":"mc","diff":"F","stem":"A new mother asks how long her insurance must allow her to stay after her cesarean birth. Under the Newborns' and Mothers' Health Protection Act of 1996, what is the minimum?","opts":["24 hours","48 hours","72 hours","96 hours"],"key":3,"rat":"The Act requires health plans to allow a minimum of 48 hours after a normal vaginal birth and a minimum of 96 hours after a cesarean birth. The attending provider can decide on early discharge in consultation with the mother.","why":{"0":"Too short for either type of birth.","1":"This is the minimum after a vaginal birth.","2":"Not a figure in the Act as presented."},"cues":"Cesarean.","aid":"Vaginal = 2 days (48 h); cesarean = double it (96 h).","src":"Maternal_Health_Linear_Chapter_Sources_Updated.pdf","pg":"pp. 157–168 (laws that impact discharge)","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Teach","comp":"teach","traps":{"0":"otherdx","1":"otherdx","2":"otherdx"},"safer":{"s":"Identify the minimum stay after cesarean.","a":"Newborns' and Mothers' Health Protection Act.","f":"Legal: discharge law.","e":"24, 48, and 72 hours don't match cesarean.","r":"96 hours after cesarean."},"principle":"Separate similar numbers by condition.","takeaway":"Vaginal 48 hours; cesarean double it.","xv":2},{"id":"B27","subj":"Psychiatric/Mental Health","topic":"LVN role in the nursing process","cn":"Coordinated Care","cn2":"","fmt":"mc","diff":"I","stem":"On the mental health unit, which activity reflects the LVN's role in the nursing process?","opts":["Independently writing the client's nursing diagnoses for the care plan","Reporting observed behaviors so the RN can form problem statements","Changing the plan of care without consulting the rest of the team","Prescribing new group activities based on the client's interests"],"key":1,"rat":"The LPN/LVN's observations of patient behavior and therapeutic communication help the RN collect data to form nursing diagnoses. The LPN/LVN contributes to problem statements with the RN, and they implement the plan of care together.","why":{"0":"The RN forms the nursing diagnoses with the LVN's input.","2":"The care plan is updated as a team.","3":"Not within LVN scope."},"cues":"","aid":"LVN = the team's eyes and ears; the RN builds the diagnosis from what the LVN reports.","src":"Psychiatric_Nursing_Complete_Linear_Study_Packet.pdf","pg":"pp. 66–82 (nursing process)","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Scope","comp":"scope","traps":{"0":"scope","2":"scope","3":"scope"},"safer":{"s":"Choose the LVN's nursing process role.","a":"Mental health unit.","f":"Scope: LVN contributions.","e":"Independent diagnoses, solo plan changes, and prescribing are outside scope.","r":"Reporting observations for the RN's problem statements."},"principle":"The LVN collects and reports data; the RN analyzes and plans.","takeaway":"LVN = the team's eyes and ears.","xv":2},{"id":"B28","subj":"Neuro & Sensory","topic":"Communicating with a blind client","cn":"Psychosocial Integrity","cn2":"Basic Care and Comfort","fmt":"sata","diff":"I","stem":"The nurse is caring for a client who is blind. Which actions are appropriate? Select all that apply.","opts":["Announce your presence when entering the room.","Speak louder than usual so the client can follow.","Introduce yourself with each contact.","Explain activity occurring in the room.","Avoid phrases such as \"See what I mean?\"","Announce when you are leaving the room."],"key":[0,2,3,5],"rat":"Tips for communicating with blind people: announce your presence, talk in a normal tone of voice, introduce yourself with each contact unless well known, explain activity in the room, do not avoid common phrases like \"See what I mean?\", and announce when leaving.","why":{"1":"Use a normal tone; blindness is not hearing loss.","4":"Common phrases need not be avoided."},"cues":"","aid":"Blind client: Arrive out loud, Name yourself, Narrate, Leave out loud. Normal voice.","src":"Eyes And Ears Disorders - Patrick Liquete.pdf","pg":"p17","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Intervene","comp":"intervention","traps":{"1":"otherdx","4":"irrelevant"},"safer":{"s":"Select appropriate actions.","a":"Client who is blind.","f":"Communication with sensory loss.","e":"Speaking louder treats hearing loss; avoiding phrases is unnecessary.","r":"Announce, introduce, narrate, announce leaving."},"principle":"Adapt to the actual sense that's affected.","takeaway":"Arrive out loud, name yourself, narrate, leave out loud.","xv":2},{"id":"B29","subj":"Pediatrics","topic":"Honest communication with children","cn":"Psychosocial Integrity","cn2":"","fmt":"mc","diff":"I","stem":"A 6-year-old is about to have blood drawn and asks, \"Will it hurt?\" Which response is best?","opts":["\"No, it won't hurt at all, so there's nothing to worry about.\"","\"Some kids say it feels like a cat scratch. Will you tell me how it felt?\"","\"Don't move at all, or it will hurt a lot more.\"","\"Big kids like you don't cry, so try to be brave for me.\""],"key":1,"rat":"Be honest with children without creating overly scary impressions; the source models \"Some say it feels like a cat or dog scratching. Afterward, will you tell me what it felt like?\" Directions are stated positively (\"You need to stay very still\" rather than \"Don't move\").","why":{"0":"Dishonest; damages trust.","2":"Negative phrasing and frightening.","3":"Shaming; does not prepare the child."},"cues":"","aid":"Honest but gentle: describe the feeling, then hand the child the microphone.","src":"PEDS_Complete_Linear_Word_Sources_2026.pdf","pg":"pp. 56–89 (Strategies for nurse-child communication)","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Intervene","comp":"intervention","traps":{"0":"reassure","2":"unsafe","3":"unsafe"},"safer":{"s":"Choose the best response.","a":"6-year-old asks if a blood draw will hurt.","f":"Therapeutic communication with children.","e":"Lying, threatening, and shaming damage trust.","r":"Honest, gentle description and an invitation to share."},"principle":"Be honest without being frightening.","takeaway":"Describe the feeling, then hand the child the microphone.","xv":2},{"id":"B30","subj":"Psychiatric/Mental Health","topic":"Suicide warning signs","cn":"Psychosocial Integrity","cn2":"Safety and Infection Prevention and Control","fmt":"sata","diff":"I","stem":"A family member asks which behaviors could be warning signs of suicide. Which should the nurse include? Select all that apply.","opts":["Giving away prized possessions","Withdrawing from family and friends","Talking about death or the hereafter","Making new plans for an upcoming vacation","Unusual neglect of appearance","Expressing regret that a previous attempt failed"],"key":[0,1,2,4,5],"rat":"Warning signs include withdrawing from family or friends; talking about death, the hereafter, or suicide; giving away prized possessions; drug or alcohol abuse; personality changes; unusual neglect of appearance; feelings of hopelessness; and previously failed attempts with verbalized regrets of failure.","why":{"3":"Future-oriented planning is not a listed warning sign."},"cues":"","aid":"Warning signs 'GIVE': Giving things away, Isolating, Voicing death talk, Ending self-care. Plus regret over a failed attempt.","src":"Psychiatric_Nursing_Complete_Linear_Study_Packet.pdf","pg":"Teaching slide 19 (slide sections pp. 25–164)","sameFormExclude":["P02","XE15"],"ask":"Teach","comp":"cue","traps":{"3":"irrelevant"},"safer":{"s":"Select suicide warning signs.","a":"Family education.","f":"Cue recognition: suicide risk.","e":"Future vacation plans aren't a listed sign.","r":"Giving things away, withdrawal, death talk, neglect, regret over a failed attempt."},"principle":"Judge each option on its own.","takeaway":"Giving away, isolating, voicing death, ending self-care.","xv":2},{"id":"B31","subj":"Psychiatric/Mental Health","topic":"Denial as a response to illness","cn":"Psychosocial Integrity","cn2":"","fmt":"mc","diff":"I","stem":"The day after learning a biopsy shows cancer, a client says, \"The lab must have mixed up my results,\" and talks about a trip next month. Which understanding should guide the nurse's response?","opts":["Denial is always harmful and must be confronted immediately and directly.","Short-term denial can help the client cope; long-term denial becomes maladaptive.","The client is showing psychosis and needs an immediate psychiatric referral.","The client has accepted the diagnosis and needs no further emotional support."],"key":1,"rat":"Box 34.5 defines denial as a refusal to admit being ill. Short-term denial is often useful in mobilizing internal resources, but long-term denial usually results in maladaptive behavior patterns.","why":{"0":"Short-term denial can be useful.","2":"Denial is a common response to illness, not psychosis.","3":"Insisting the results are wrong is not acceptance."},"cues":"Day after diagnosis.","aid":"Denial is a shock absorber: helpful on the first bump, harmful if you never take your foot off it.","src":"Psychiatric_Nursing_Complete_Linear_Study_Packet.pdf","pg":"pp. 58–65 (Box 34.5)","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Interpret","comp":"analysis","traps":{"0":"unsafe","2":"otherdx","3":"otherdx"},"safer":{"s":"Choose the guiding understanding.","a":"Day after cancer diagnosis; insists lab mixed up results.","f":"Behaviors common with illness.","e":"Always harmful, psychosis, and acceptance are wrong.","r":"Short-term denial can help; long-term denial is maladaptive."},"principle":"Interpret behavior in its time frame.","takeaway":"Denial is a shock absorber for the first bump.","xv":2},{"id":"B32","subj":"Psychiatric/Mental Health","topic":"Hallucinations with rising agitation","cn":"Psychosocial Integrity","cn2":"Safety and Infection Prevention and Control","fmt":"mc","diff":"A","stem":"A client with schizophrenia has been calmly watching TV. Now he is pacing, clenching his fists, and talking angrily to someone no one else can see. Which action should the nurse take first?","opts":["Ask the client to describe in detail what the voices are saying to him.","Provide for safety: reduce stimulation, watch for aggression, and get help.","Invite the client to join a group activity in the dayroom to distract him.","Offer the client several activity choices to restore his sense of control."],"key":1,"rat":"Hallucinations and delusions have the potential to provoke aggressive behavior; the nurse watches for signs of growing agitation and provides safety. Interventions also include decreasing sensory stimuli and avoiding having the client make choices to reduce anxiety.","why":{"0":"Exploring content in detail can wait; safety comes first when agitation is rising.","2":"Socialization is by invitation, and a group adds stimulation during agitation.","3":"The source advises avoiding having the client make choices to reduce anxiety."},"cues":"Pacing + clenched fists + responding to voices.","aid":"Voices + rising agitation = safety first, stimulation down.","src":"Psychiatric_Nursing_Complete_Linear_Study_Packet.pdf","pg":"pp. 83–114 (Schizophrenia nursing interventions; Table 35.1)","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Prioritize","comp":"priority","traps":{"0":"later","2":"unsafe","3":"otherdx"},"safer":{"s":"Choose the first action.","a":"Pacing, clenched fists, angry talk to unseen person.","f":"Psychiatric safety: escalating agitation.","e":"Exploring voices can wait; group adds stimulation; choices raise anxiety.","r":"Safety and reduced stimulation come first."},"principle":"Rising agitation shifts the priority to safety.","takeaway":"Voices plus agitation: safety first, stimulation down.","compare":{"a":1,"b":0,"aWhy":"It protects everyone as agitation rises.","bWhy":"Knowing what the voices say could reveal command content.","decider":"Hallucinations can provoke aggression; with agitation rising, safety comes before exploring content.","flip":"If the client were calm, gently asking about the voices would be appropriate."},"xv":2},{"id":"B33","subj":"Maternal-Newborn","topic":"Pathologic vs physiologic jaundice","cn":"Health Promotion and Maintenance","cn2":"Reduction of Risk Potential","fmt":"mc","diff":"A","stem":"The nurse is assessing four newborns. Which finding should be reported to the provider first?","opts":["Bluish hands and feet in a 6-hour-old who is slightly cool","A 2-day-old whose dependent side is pinker than the upper side when lying on her side","Yellow skin on the face of an 18-hour-old","A bluish-black area over the lower back of a 1-day-old"],"key":2,"rat":"Physiologic jaundice is a normal occurrence that appears about 48 hours after birth. Jaundice sooner than 48 hours, and especially at birth or within the first 24 hours, is pathologic, may result from maternal–fetal blood incompatibility, and requires further assessment. Acrocyanosis, harlequin sign, and Mongolian spots are normal variations.","why":{"0":"Acrocyanosis from poor peripheral circulation is common, especially when the infant is cold.","1":"Harlequin sign is not harmful and may last up to 20 minutes.","3":"Mongolian spots are areas of increased pigmentation and are normal."},"cues":"Jaundice before 24 hours.","aid":"Jaundice timing: 'Day 1 = Danger, Day 2–3 = Due.'","src":"Maternal_Health_Linear_Chapter_Sources_Updated.pdf","pg":"pp. 169–193 (Ch. 28 Healthy Newborn, skin alterations); Ch. 29 hemolytic disease (pp. 194–330)","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Prioritize","comp":"cue","traps":{"0":"reassure","1":"reassure","3":"reassure"},"safer":{"s":"Choose the newborn finding to report first.","a":"Acrocyanosis, harlequin sign, jaundice at 18 hours, Mongolian spot.","f":"Expected newborn variations versus pathology.","e":"Acrocyanosis, harlequin sign, and Mongolian spots are normal.","r":"Jaundice before 24 hours is pathologic."},"principle":"Timing turns a common finding into a warning sign.","takeaway":"Jaundice day 1 = danger; day 2–3 = due.","xv":2},{"id":"B34","subj":"Psychiatric/Mental Health","topic":"Safety for a client with delirium","cn":"Safety and Infection Prevention and Control","cn2":"Psychosocial Integrity","fmt":"sata","diff":"I","stem":"An older adult with delirium becomes more confused at night and has tried to get out of bed alone. Which interventions should the nurse include? Select all that apply.","opts":["Place the bed in its lowest position.","Keep the call light and personal articles within reach.","Provide sufficient night lighting.","Use a bed alarm.","Keep the room completely dark to encourage sleep.","Give several instructions at once so the client knows the whole plan."],"key":[0,1,2,3],"rat":"Safety for delirium: bed in lowest position, side rails useful to aid turning, hallway rails, chair and bed alarms, call light and personal articles within reach, and sufficient night lighting. Instructions are given one step at a time in simple terms. Symptoms of delirium worsen at night.","why":{"4":"Sufficient night lighting is recommended.","5":"Give instructions one step at a time."},"cues":"Worse at night + getting up alone.","aid":"Delirium safety 'LOW-LIT-REACH': low bed, lit room, everything in reach, alarm on.","src":"Psychiatric_Nursing_Complete_Linear_Study_Packet.pdf","pg":"pp. 83–114 (Delirium, Table 35.1)","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Intervene","comp":"intervention","traps":{"4":"unsafe","5":"unsafe"},"safer":{"s":"Select delirium safety interventions.","a":"Night confusion, trying to get up alone.","f":"Safety: fall prevention in delirium.","e":"Total darkness and multiple instructions worsen confusion.","r":"Low bed, items in reach, night light, bed alarm."},"principle":"Reduce fall risk and confusion together.","takeaway":"Low, lit, in reach, alarm on.","xv":2},{"id":"B35","subj":"Fluids & Electrolytes","topic":"Potassium safety","cn":"Pharmacological Therapies","cn2":"Reduction of Risk Potential","fmt":"mc","diff":"A","stem":"A client taking furosemide has hypokalemia, palpitations, and an irregular pulse. Which order should the nurse question?","opts":["Place the client on cardiac (EKG) monitoring.","Potassium chloride IV push now.","Recheck serum potassium after replacement.","Potassium chloride added to an IV solution to infuse slowly."],"key":1,"rat":"The instructor's F&E lecture: with potassium problems, think EKG and put the client on a monitor because the heart is the priority. Never give potassium IV push; it can cause cardiac arrest. Potassium is always given in a solution as a slow drip, and labs are rechecked as treatment proceeds. Loop diuretics such as furosemide are a cause of hypokalemia.","why":{"0":"Appropriate: potassium imbalance threatens cardiac rhythm.","2":"Appropriate monitoring of replacement.","3":"Appropriate: diluted, slow infusion is the safe IV route."},"cues":"\"IV push\" + potassium.","aid":"Potassium never gets pushed — it gets a slow drip. 'K+ never takes the express lane.'","src":"Musculoskeletal_Complete_Project_Combined (2).pdf","pg":"pp. 500–545 (Fluid & Electrolyte lecture transcript, hypokalemia)","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Clarify","comp":"med","traps":{"0":"irrelevant","2":"irrelevant","3":"irrelevant"},"safer":{"s":"Find the order to question.","a":"Hypokalemia with palpitations on furosemide.","f":"Medication safety: potassium route.","e":"Monitoring, rechecking, and slow diluted infusion are appropriate.","r":"Potassium IV push is never given."},"principle":"Some routes are never safe for some drugs.","takeaway":"Potassium never gets pushed.","med":{"why":"Hypokalemia from a loop diuretic.","check":"Cardiac rhythm, serum potassium.","give":"Oral or diluted slow IV infusion; never IV push.","watch":"Dysrhythmias; repeat potassium levels.","teach":"Report palpitations; follow diet and drug plan."},"xv":2},{"id":"B36","subj":"Musculoskeletal","topic":"Fall risk in older adults","cn":"Safety and Infection Prevention and Control","cn2":"Health Promotion and Maintenance","fmt":"mc","diff":"A","stem":"Four older adult clients are on a medical unit. Which client is at highest risk for a fall?","opts":["A 70-year-old who walks a mile daily, wears well-fitted shoes, and takes a daily vitamin","A 78-year-old with osteoporosis, poor vision, and dizziness on standing after a new BP pill","A 72-year-old with arthritis in both hands who uses a walker correctly when walking","A 75-year-old taking calcium and vitamin D who has no gait or balance problems"],"key":1,"rat":"Factors that increase fall risk include gait and balance problems, decreased vision and hearing, diminished reflexes, orthostatic hypotension, and medication use. Many falls happen getting into or out of a chair or bed, and osteoporosis with low bone density increases the risk of hip fracture from a fall.","why":{"0":"Active with proper footwear; few risk factors.","2":"Correct use of assistive devices reduces injury.","3":"Calcium and vitamin D reduce fracture risk; no gait problems."},"cues":"Orthostatic dizziness + poor vision + new BP medicine + osteoporosis.","aid":"Fall risk stacks up: count the risk factors — the tallest stack falls first.","src":"Musculoskeletal_Complete_Project_Combined (2).pdf","pg":"pp. 283–351 (Ch. 44, Lifespan: Older Adults; Health Promotion: Hip Fracture)","sameFormExclude":[],"ask":"Prioritize","comp":"cue","traps":{"0":"lower","2":"lower","3":"lower"},"safer":{"s":"Choose the highest fall risk.","a":"Orthostatic dizziness, poor vision, new antihypertensive, osteoporosis.","f":"Risk stacking.","e":"Active walker, correct walker use, and calcium without gait problems have fewer risks.","r":"The client with the most risk factors."},"principle":"Count risk factors; the tallest stack wins.","takeaway":"Fall risk stacks up.","xv":2},{"id":"XE01","ver":2,"tier":"expert","cog":"judgment","subj":"Pain & Opioids","topic":"Meperidine metabolite toxicity","cn":"Pharmacological Therapies","cn2":"Safety and Infection Prevention and Control","fmt":"mc","diff":"A","ask":"Intervene","comp":"med","stem":"A client with chronic kidney disease has received meperidine IM every 4 hours for 3 days after abdominal surgery. The client is now jittery, has new hand tremors, and seems keyed up. Pain is 4/10, respirations are 16/min, and SpO₂ is 96%. Which action should the nurse take?","opts":["Give naloxone as ordered because the client is having an opioid reaction","Hold the next dose and report the tremors and agitation to the provider","Give the next dose because pain is controlled and breathing is normal","Dim the lights and reassess the client's anxiety in 1 hour"],"key":1,"rat":"Meperidine's active metabolite, normeperidine, accumulates with use beyond 1–2 days and in renal impairment. Toxic levels cause excitation, tremors, and seizures. This client has both risk factors and early signs, so the drug is held and the provider is notified. Naloxone reverses opiate agonist effects such as respiratory depression, which this client does not have.","why":{"0":"Naloxone reverses opiate agonist effects like respiratory depression; this client's breathing is normal and the picture is excitation from metabolite buildup.","2":"Normal respirations do not rule out normeperidine toxicity, which presents with excitation and can progress to seizures.","3":"Treating this as anxiety misses a drug toxicity that can progress to seizures."},"traps":{"0":"otherdx","2":"isolated","3":"otherdx"},"cues":"Renal impairment, meperidine for 3 days, new tremors and excitation.","aid":"Meperidine's metabolite is a slow-building storm: kidneys can't clear it, nerves get jumpy, seizures follow.","principle":"An adverse effect can look nothing like the drug's usual toxicity; match the findings to the specific drug and patient risk.","takeaway":"Not every opioid problem is respiratory depression. Meperidine plus kidney disease plus tremors means metabolite toxicity.","safer":{"s":"Choose the action for new neuro findings on meperidine.","a":"CKD, 3 days of meperidine, tremors, jitteriness; RR and SpO₂ normal.","f":"Medication safety: drug-specific toxicity.","e":"Naloxone treats a different problem, giving the dose ignores toxicity, anxiety care delays.","r":"Holding and reporting beats giving the dose because tremors signal toxicity that can progress to seizures."},"compare":{"a":1,"b":2,"aWhy":"It stops further accumulation and brings in the provider.","bWhy":"Pain is controlled and breathing is normal, the usual checks before an opioid.","decider":"Tremors and excitation in a client with renal impairment after 3 days of meperidine point to normeperidine toxicity.","flip":"Without tremors or excitation, the provider would still need to review continuing meperidine past 1–2 days."},"med":{"why":"Postoperative pain.","check":"Kidney function, duration of therapy, neuro status, respirations.","give":"Only short-term; use beyond 1–2 days is not recommended.","watch":"Excitation, tremors, seizures (normeperidine), respiratory depression.","teach":"Report jitteriness or tremors right away."},"src":"Pain_Management_and_Urinary_System_Combined.pdf","pg":"p3","sameFormExclude":[]},{"id":"XE02","ver":2,"tier":"expert","cog":"analysis","subj":"Pediatrics","topic":"Opioid restriction in children","cn":"Pharmacological Therapies","cn2":"Safety and Infection Prevention and Control","fmt":"mc","diff":"I","ask":"Clarify","comp":"med","stem":"A 9-year-old is being discharged after a tonsillectomy for obstructive sleep apnea. Which discharge prescription should the nurse clarify with the provider before reinforcing teaching?","opts":["Acetaminophen liquid by mouth every 6 hours as needed for pain","Codeine elixir by mouth every 4 hours as needed for pain","Ice collar to the neck as needed for comfort","Encourage apple juice and water; avoid crunchy or seeded foods"],"key":1,"rat":"The medication safety alert states that codeine and tramadol should not be used to treat pain in children younger than 12, and should also be avoided at ages 12–18 in children with obesity, obstructive sleep apnea, or severe lung disease. Tonsillectomy discharge teaching includes mild analgesics or an ice collar for pain, no aspirin, encouraged fluids such as apple juice, and avoiding crunchy or seeded foods.","why":{"0":"A mild analgesic for pain fits the tonsillectomy discharge teaching.","2":"An ice collar is a listed comfort measure.","3":"Fluids and avoiding crunchy or seeded foods are listed discharge instructions."},"traps":{"0":"irrelevant","2":"irrelevant","3":"irrelevant"},"cues":"Age 9, codeine prescribed, history of obstructive sleep apnea.","aid":"Codeine and tramadol: not under 12, and not for teens with apnea, obesity, or bad lungs.","principle":"Check age-specific contraindications before carrying out a prescription.","takeaway":"Clarify questions reward knowing the one order that is unsafe for this age or condition, even if it is routine for adults.","safer":{"s":"Find the prescription to clarify.","a":"Age 9, sleep apnea, codeine ordered.","f":"Medication safety: age and condition contraindications.","e":"Acetaminophen, ice collar, and diet instructions all match tonsillectomy teaching.","r":"Codeine is the only order contraindicated by age, so it must be clarified before teaching."},"med":{"why":"Pain after tonsillectomy.","check":"Age and conditions such as sleep apnea before any codeine or tramadol.","give":"Use mild analgesics; no aspirin.","watch":"Breathing problems with opioids in children.","teach":"Use the prescribed mild analgesic or ice collar; report bleeding."},"src":"Pain_Management_and_Urinary_System_Combined.pdf","pg":"p3 (Medication Safety Alert); PEDS Word pp. 154–210 (tonsillectomy discharge)","sameFormExclude":["V07"]},{"id":"XE03","ver":2,"tier":"expert","cog":"judgment","subj":"Psychiatric","topic":"Clozapine with fever and sore throat","cn":"Pharmacological Therapies","cn2":"Safety and Infection Prevention and Control","fmt":"mc","diff":"A","ask":"Intervene","comp":"cue","stem":"A client who has taken clozapine for several months reports a sore throat and chills. Temperature is 101.0°F (38.3°C). The client says, \"It's just a cold.\" Which action should the nurse take?","opts":["Give acetaminophen and offer warm salt-water gargles","Agree that it sounds like a cold and recheck tomorrow","Report the findings promptly and anticipate a blood count","Stop the clozapine and document the reason"],"key":2,"rat":"Clozapine carries a 1–2% incidence of agranulocytosis and requires weekly serum monitoring. Agranulocytosis presents with fever, chills, and sores in the mouth or throat, is diagnosed by a CBC with differential, and makes infections life-threatening, so signs of infection are reported promptly to start therapy. Stopping a medication is a provider decision.","why":{"0":"Symptom relief without reporting delays recognition of a possibly life-threatening infection.","1":"Fever and sore throat on clozapine are warning signs of agranulocytosis, not something to wait on.","3":"Discontinuing a prescribed drug is the provider's decision; the nurse reports and holds as directed."},"traps":{"0":"delay","1":"reassure","3":"scope"},"cues":"Clozapine, fever, chills, sore throat.","aid":"Clozapine plus fever or sore throat: check the white count before anything else.","principle":"Interpret common symptoms through the lens of the patient's high-risk medication.","takeaway":"A 'cold' in a client on clozapine can be agranulocytosis. Report it and expect a CBC.","safer":{"s":"Choose the right action for new symptoms.","a":"Clozapine, temperature 101.0°F, chills, sore throat.","f":"Medication safety: serious adverse effect (agranulocytosis).","e":"Comfort measures and waiting delay care; stopping the drug is outside nursing scope.","r":"Reporting with an expected CBC beats acetaminophen because the fever may be the only sign of agranulocytosis."},"compare":{"a":2,"b":3,"aWhy":"It gets the provider and a blood count involved quickly.","bWhy":"If clozapine is causing agranulocytosis, stopping it sounds protective.","decider":"Stopping a medication is a provider decision; the nurse's role is to report promptly with data.","flip":"Once the provider orders the clozapine held, holding it becomes the correct nursing action."},"med":{"why":"Treatment-resistant schizophrenia.","check":"Weekly serum monitoring (blood counts).","give":"As prescribed after confirming monitoring is current.","watch":"Agranulocytosis (fever, sore throat, chills), seizures, orthostatic hypotension.","teach":"Report fever, sore throat, or mouth sores right away; keep blood-test appointments; rise slowly."},"src":"Psychiatric_Nursing_Complete_Linear_Study_Packet.pdf","pg":"pp. 66–82 (atypical antipsychotics: clozapine); Blood_and_Lymph p37 (agranulocytosis)","sameFormExclude":[]},{"id":"XE04","ver":2,"tier":"expert","cog":"judgment","subj":"Pediatrics","topic":"Rebound after aerosolized epinephrine","cn":"Physiological Adaptation","cn2":"Reduction of Risk Potential","fmt":"mc","diff":"A","ask":"Evaluate","comp":"evaluate","stem":"A 2-year-old with laryngotracheobronchitis received aerosolized epinephrine 1 hour ago. Inspiratory stridor has resolved, and the child is playing in the crib. The parent asks, \"He looks fine now. Can we go home?\" Which response is best?","opts":["\"His breathing looks much better now, so he should be ready to go home.\"","\"He can go home once he drinks a full bottle to show he is ready.\"","\"The medicine wears off in a few hours and swelling can return; he needs watching.\"","\"We will give him a sedative so he sleeps tonight, and then he can go home.\""],"key":2,"rat":"Aerosolized epinephrine decreases airway edema, but its effects are short-lived; airway edema can return a few hours after a dose and repeated doses may be needed, so children receiving it are monitored very closely. Sedatives are contraindicated because they mask restlessness, a sign of hypoxia.","why":{"0":"Improvement right after epinephrine can be temporary; edema may return within hours.","1":"Drinking does not show that the airway will stay open once the drug wears off.","3":"Sedatives mask restlessness, a key sign of worsening hypoxia."},"traps":{"0":"reassure","1":"irrelevant","3":"unsafe"},"cues":"Improvement 1 hour after a short-acting airway drug.","aid":"Epinephrine is a loan, not a gift: the swelling can come back to collect.","principle":"Evaluate outcomes against the drug's duration of action, not a single good moment.","takeaway":"When a fast, short-acting drug fixes a problem, the question is what happens when it wears off.","safer":{"s":"Evaluate whether improvement means discharge.","a":"Stridor resolved 1 hour after aerosolized epinephrine; child playing.","f":"Evaluate outcomes; airway safety in a child.","e":"Discharge, the drinking test, and sedation ignore rebound or mask hypoxia.","r":"Close monitoring beats discharge because the epinephrine effect is temporary."},"compare":{"a":2,"b":0,"aWhy":"It accounts for the drug's short duration and the risk of returning edema.","bWhy":"Stridor is gone and the child is playing, which looks like a good outcome.","decider":"Epinephrine's effect is short-lived, and edema can return a few hours after the dose.","flip":"If the child stayed free of stridor well after the medication wore off, the outcome would be more reliable."},"src":"PEDS_Complete_Linear_Word_Sources_2026.pdf","pg":"pp. 154–210 (Croup: LTB management)","sameFormExclude":[]},{"id":"XE05","ver":2,"tier":"expert","cog":"application","subj":"Endocrine","topic":"Exercise with high glucose","cn":"Health Promotion and Maintenance","cn2":"Physiological Adaptation","fmt":"mc","diff":"I","ask":"Teach","comp":"teach","stem":"A client with type 1 diabetes plans to go jogging after lunch. The pre-exercise fingerstick glucose is 268 mg/dL. Which response by the nurse is best?","opts":["\"Eat a carbohydrate snack first, then go ahead with your jog as planned.\"","\"Exercise will bring your glucose down, so jogging now is a good idea.\"","\"Skip your next insulin dose so you won't get low while you're jogging.\"","\"Check your urine for ketones and call your provider before you exercise.\""],"key":3,"rat":"Regular exercise is encouraged because it improves insulin sensitivity, with changes before, during, and after exercise. The diabetes slides give two thresholds: glucose below 100 mg/dL calls for a carbohydrate snack before exercise, and glucose above 250 mg/dL means possible ketones and a check with the provider.","why":{"0":"A pre-exercise snack is for glucose below 100 mg/dL, not 268.","1":"Exercise does improve insulin sensitivity, but above 250 mg/dL ketones must be checked first.","2":"Skipping insulin raises glucose further and is not part of the exercise guidance."},"traps":{"0":"otherdx","1":"irrelevant","2":"unsafe"},"cues":"Glucose above 250 mg/dL before exercise.","aid":"Before a workout: under 100, snack; over 250, stop and check.","principle":"Apply the threshold that matches the current value, not the general rule.","takeaway":"Exercise is good for diabetes in general, but the glucose number right now decides whether to exercise.","safer":{"s":"Choose the best teaching response.","a":"Type 1 diabetes, glucose 268 mg/dL, planning to jog.","f":"Teaching: diabetes exercise thresholds.","e":"The snack fits a low value, the true statement ignores the threshold, skipping insulin is unsafe.","r":"Checking ketones and calling beats exercising now because the value is above 250."},"compare":{"a":3,"b":1,"aWhy":"It applies the over-250 rule from the slides.","bWhy":"Exercise really does improve insulin sensitivity.","decider":"Above 250 mg/dL, ketones may be present and the provider must be consulted first.","flip":"At a glucose between 100 and 250, encouraging the planned exercise would be appropriate."},"src":"Med_Surg_All_Sources_Linear_Master_2026.pdf","pg":"pp. 349–415 (Endocrine/DM slide 42)","sameFormExclude":[]},{"id":"XE06","ver":2,"tier":"expert","cog":"application","subj":"Endocrine","topic":"Sick-day management","cn":"Health Promotion and Maintenance","cn2":"Pharmacological Therapies","fmt":"sata","diff":"I","ask":"Evaluate","comp":"teach","stem":"A client with diabetes has the flu. Which statements show that the client understands sick-day management? Select all that apply.","opts":["\"I'll keep taking my diabetes medicine even though I'm eating less.\"","\"I'll check my blood sugar every 1 to 2 hours while I'm awake.\"","\"I'll call my provider if my sugar is over 250.\"","\"I'll cut back on fluids so I don't have to get up to urinate.\"","\"If I can't eat meals, I'll sip regular soft drinks or have gelatin.\"","\"I won't bother checking for ketones until I feel better.\""],"key":[0,1,2,4],"rat":"During minor illness, the client continues drug therapy, uses soft drinks, gelatin, or sports drinks, checks glucose every 1–2 hours while awake, calls the provider if glucose is over 250 mg/dL, checks urine for ketones and watches for fever and vomiting, and increases fluid intake.","why":{"3":"Fluid intake is increased during illness.","5":"Urine ketones are checked during illness."},"traps":{"3":"unsafe","5":"unsafe"},"cues":"Minor illness; each statement judged on its own.","aid":"Sick days: keep the meds, sip the sugar, check every 1–2 hours, call over 250, drink up.","principle":"In select-all items, judge each option as its own true-or-false decision.","takeaway":"Illness raises glucose, so the client keeps taking medicine and monitors more, not less.","safer":{"s":"Select every statement that shows correct understanding.","a":"Flu, eating less, diabetes.","f":"Teaching evaluation: sick-day rules.","e":"Reducing fluids and stopping ketone checks contradict the sick-day guidance.","r":"Each remaining statement matches a listed sick-day rule."},"src":"Med_Surg_All_Sources_Linear_Master_2026.pdf","pg":"pp. 349–415 (Endocrine/DM slide 43)","sameFormExclude":[]},{"id":"XE07","ver":2,"tier":"expert","cog":"analysis","subj":"Endocrine","topic":"Oral antidiabetic drugs before surgery","cn":"Pharmacological Therapies","cn2":"Reduction of Risk Potential","fmt":"mc","diff":"I","ask":"Prioritize","comp":"med","stem":"A client with type 2 diabetes is scheduled for surgery tomorrow morning. Which finding should the nurse report to the provider before the procedure?","opts":["Fingerstick glucose this evening is 142 mg/dL after a light dinner","The client says insulin may be used for a short time after surgery","The client took the usual oral diabetes tablet this morning","The client is worried about being NPO after midnight and feeling hungry"],"key":2,"rat":"For surgery, IV fluids and insulin are given before, during, and after the procedure while the client is NPO. In type 2 diabetes, oral antidiabetic medications are stopped 48 hours before surgery, and insulin is used temporarily. Stress can raise glucose, so a mildly elevated value is not unexpected.","why":{"0":"Stress can increase glucose; this value does not change tomorrow's plan the way a continued oral agent does.","1":"This statement is accurate; insulin is used temporarily around surgery.","3":"Anxiety deserves support, but it does not affect surgical safety the way the medication does."},"traps":{"0":"isolated","1":"irrelevant","3":"later"},"cues":"Oral agent taken today; surgery tomorrow.","aid":"Pills off 2 days before surgery; insulin covers the gap.","principle":"Report the finding that conflicts with the preoperative plan.","takeaway":"In surgery questions, look for the medication that should have been stopped and wasn't.","safer":{"s":"Identify the finding to report before surgery.","a":"Type 2 diabetes, oral agent taken this morning with a dose planned tonight, glucose 142, accurate insulin statement, anxiety.","f":"Perioperative medication safety.","e":"Glucose 142 is explainable by stress, the insulin statement is correct, and anxiety is lower priority.","r":"The oral agent beats the glucose value because the protocol stops it 48 hours before surgery."},"compare":{"a":2,"b":0,"aWhy":"The oral agent should have been stopped 48 hours before surgery.","bWhy":"An elevated glucose before surgery seems worth reporting.","decider":"Stress can raise glucose and insulin will cover it; the continued oral agent conflicts with the protocol.","flip":"A glucose above 250 mg/dL would also need prompt reporting."},"src":"Med_Surg_All_Sources_Linear_Master_2026.pdf","pg":"pp. 349–415 (Endocrine/DM slides 43–44)","sameFormExclude":[]},{"id":"XE08","ver":2,"tier":"expert","cog":"analysis","subj":"Adult GI","topic":"Hepatotoxic drugs in hepatic encephalopathy","cn":"Pharmacological Therapies","cn2":"Physiological Adaptation","fmt":"mc","diff":"A","ask":"Clarify","comp":"med","sameFormExclude":["G03","XE13"],"stem":"A client with cirrhosis has new disorientation and asterixis. Which prescription should the nurse clarify with the provider?","opts":["Lactulose 30 mL by mouth three times daily","Acetaminophen 650 mg by mouth every 6 hours PRN","Daily weight and abdominal girth measurement","Vitamin K as ordered for prolonged clotting times"],"key":1,"rat":"Disorientation and asterixis signal hepatic encephalopathy from ammonia the scarred liver cannot clear. Management includes lactulose and avoiding hepatotoxic medications such as acetaminophen (Tylenol) and sedatives. Daily weight and girth, and vitamin K for abnormal coagulation results, are listed nursing considerations.","why":{"0":"Lactulose lowers ammonia and is expected for hepatic encephalopathy.","2":"Daily weight and girth are standard monitoring in cirrhosis.","3":"Vitamin K is given when prescribed for abnormal coagulation results."},"traps":{"0":"irrelevant","2":"irrelevant","3":"irrelevant"},"cues":"Cirrhosis with encephalopathy; acetaminophen is hepatotoxic.","aid":"A sick liver can't handle Tylenol or sedatives: protect what's left.","principle":"Screen every order against the organ that is failing.","takeaway":"Over-the-counter does not mean safe for every patient. Acetaminophen is hepatotoxic in liver disease.","safer":{"s":"Find the prescription to clarify.","a":"Cirrhosis, new disorientation, asterixis.","f":"Medication safety: hepatotoxic drug in liver failure.","e":"Lactulose, weights and girth, and vitamin K all fit the plan.","r":"Acetaminophen is the only order the source says to avoid."},"med":{"why":"Headache.","check":"Liver function; avoid hepatotoxic drugs such as acetaminophen and sedatives.","give":"Clarify before giving.","watch":"Worsening confusion, asterixis, bleeding.","teach":"Avoid alcohol, acetaminophen, and sedatives unless the provider approves."},"src":"GI_Linear_Chapter_Sourcebook.pdf","pg":"pp122, 136–137"},{"id":"XE09","ver":2,"tier":"expert","cog":"application","subj":"Adult GI","topic":"Paracentesis sequence","cn":"Reduction of Risk Potential","cn2":"","fmt":"order","diff":"I","ask":"Sequence","comp":"intervention","stem":"A client with ascites is scheduled for a paracentesis at the bedside. Place the nurse's actions in the order they should be performed.","opts":["Have the client empty the bladder","Confirm the signed consent form","After the procedure, monitor vital signs and check the puncture site for bleeding","Position the client sitting on the side of the bed or in high Fowler's position"],"key":[1,0,3,2],"rat":"Paracentesis requires a consent form. The client urinates immediately before to prevent bladder puncture and then sits on the side of the bed or in high Fowler's position. Afterward, the nurse monitors vital signs, checks the puncture site for bleeding or drainage, measures and documents the fluid, weighs the client, and watches for signs of hypovolemia and shock.","why":{},"traps":{},"cues":"Consent first; void immediately before; positioning right before the procedure; monitoring after.","aid":"Paracentesis: Sign, Pee, Sit, then Watch.","principle":"Put safety-critical preparation first, then follow the procedure's natural order.","takeaway":"Voiding comes right before the tap so the bladder is empty when the needle goes in.","safer":{"s":"Order the steps.","a":"Bedside paracentesis.","f":"Procedure sequence and safety.","e":"No step is wrong here; the task is order.","r":"Consent, then empty bladder, then position, then post-procedure monitoring."},"src":"GI_Linear_Chapter_Sourcebook.pdf","pg":"pp121, 135–136","sameFormExclude":["XE24"]},{"id":"XE10","ver":2,"tier":"expert","cog":"analysis","subj":"Maternal-Newborn","topic":"Oxytocin titration order","cn":"Pharmacological Therapies","cn2":"Reduction of Risk Potential","fmt":"mc","diff":"A","ask":"Clarify","comp":"med","stem":"A client at term is admitted for induction of labor. Which oxytocin prescription should the nurse clarify before implementing it?","opts":["Begin the infusion at 1 milliunit/min using an infusion pump","Increase by 2 milliunits/min every 5 minutes until contractions are every 2 minutes","Add the oxytocin to the IV solution after the line is patent and running","Monitor contraction frequency, duration, intensity, and fetal heart rate continuously"],"key":1,"rat":"For induction, oxytocin starts at 0.5–1 milliunit/min by infusion pump and may increase by 1–2 milliunits/min every 15–60 minutes; rates should not be altered more often than every 15–30 minutes. Oxytocin is added only after the IV is patent and running, and contractions and fetal heart rate are monitored throughout.","why":{"0":"A starting rate of 1 milliunit/min by pump matches the dosing guidance.","2":"Adding oxytocin only to a patent, running line is the stated procedure.","3":"Continuous monitoring of contractions and fetal heart rate is required."},"traps":{"0":"irrelevant","2":"irrelevant","3":"irrelevant"},"cues":"Every 5 minutes versus the 15–30 minute minimum interval.","aid":"Oxytocin climbs slowly: small steps, at least 15 minutes apart.","principle":"Compare each order to the safe range for dose, rate, and frequency.","takeaway":"Clarify items often hinge on timing. A correct dose increase given too often is still unsafe.","safer":{"s":"Find the order to clarify.","a":"Increase interval of 5 minutes.","f":"Obstetric medication safety: titration limits.","e":"Starting rate, line patency, and monitoring are correct.","r":"The 5-minute titration is the only order outside the stated interval."},"med":{"why":"Induction of labor at term.","check":"Baseline vital signs, I&O, patent IV, infusion pump.","give":"Start 0.5–1 milliunit/min; increase 1–2 milliunits/min no more often than every 15–30 minutes.","watch":"Contractions longer than 90 seconds, fetal distress.","teach":"Explain monitoring; report pain changes."},"src":"Maternal_Health_Linear_Chapter_Sources_Updated.pdf","pg":"pp. 331–381 (Ch. 39 oxytocin dosage and administration)","sameFormExclude":[]},{"id":"XE11","ver":2,"tier":"expert","cog":"analysis","subj":"Maternal-Newborn","topic":"Home phototherapy reports","cn":"Health Promotion and Maintenance","cn2":"Reduction of Risk Potential","fmt":"matrix","diff":"A","ngn":true,"cj":"Analyze cues","ask":"Interpret","comp":"analysis","stem":"A parent calls about a newborn receiving home phototherapy with a biliblanket. For each report, indicate whether it is an expected effect or requires a call to the provider.","rows":["Stools are looser and a different color than before","A mild rash where the loose stools touch the skin","Only 4 wet diapers in the past 24 hours","Axillary temperature 98.8°F (37.1°C)","Vomited the last two feedings","Much harder to wake for feedings than yesterday"],"cols":["Expected effect","Call the provider"],"key":[0,0,1,0,1,1],"rat":"Box 29.9 lists changes in stool frequency and color, and a rash from more frequent loose stools, as effects of phototherapy. Parents notify the provider for a notable change in activity level, a temperature not maintained between 97°F and 100°F, poor feeding, fewer than six voids per day or no void in 6 hours, or vomiting two or more feedings.","why":{"2":"Fewer than six voids a day is a reason to call.","4":"Vomiting two or more feedings is a reason to call.","5":"A notable change in activity level is a reason to call."},"traps":{},"cues":"Voids under six, vomiting two feedings, activity change.","aid":"Biliblanket calls: Dry (under 6 voids), Drowsy (activity change), Dumping feeds (vomiting 2+), Degrees off (outside 97–100°F).","principle":"Separate expected treatment effects from signs of dehydration or illness.","takeaway":"Phototherapy changes stools on purpose. Fewer wet diapers or vomiting means the baby may be getting sick or dry.","safer":{"s":"Classify each report.","a":"Stool and rash changes; voids, vomiting, temperature, activity.","f":"Expected effect versus warning sign in newborn care.","e":"Stool and rash changes are expected; the temperature is in range.","r":"Low voids, vomiting, and lethargy each meet a call criterion."},"src":"Maternal_Health_Linear_Chapter_Sources_Updated.pdf","pg":"pp. 194–330 (Ch. 29, Box 29.9)","sameFormExclude":[]},{"id":"XE12","ver":2,"tier":"expert","cog":"analysis","subj":"Renal/Urinary","topic":"AKI phase from a trend","cn":"Physiological Adaptation","cn2":"Reduction of Risk Potential","fmt":"mc","diff":"A","ngn":true,"cj":"Analyze cues","ask":"Interpret","comp":"analysis","sameFormExclude":["R03","XE25"],"trend":{"label":"Hospital day","times":["Day 1","Day 2","Day 3","Day 4","Day 5"],"series":[{"name":"Urine output","unit":"mL/24 h","vals":[320,300,650,1400,1900]},{"name":"BUN","unit":"mg/dL","vals":[68,74,70,58,46]},{"name":"Creatinine","unit":"mg/dL","vals":[4.1,4.4,4.2,3.6,2.9]}]},"stem":"A client is recovering from acute kidney injury. Review the trend. Which interpretation and nursing focus are most accurate on day 5?","opts":["The client remains in the oliguric phase; watch for rising potassium","The client has entered the diuretic phase; monitor closely for dehydration","Kidney function has fully recovered; fluid monitoring can be reduced","The rising output signals fluid overload; restrict fluids further"],"key":1,"rat":"In the oliguric phase, BUN and creatinine rise and output falls below 400 mL/24 hr. In the diuretic phase, blood chemistry begins to return to normal and output increases to 1–2 L in 24 hours; dehydration may occur. Recovery, with GFR rising toward normal, can take 3 months to a year, and nursing care keeps close watch on fluid status and output.","why":{"0":"Output was under 400 mL on days 1–2, but it now exceeds 1 L with falling BUN and creatinine.","2":"Recovery takes 3 months to a year; the client is mid-course with dehydration risk.","3":"High output in the diuretic phase raises the risk of dehydration, not overload."},"traps":{"0":"otherdx","2":"reassure","3":"isolated"},"cues":"Output rising past 1 L/24 h while BUN and creatinine fall.","aid":"AKI is a dam: first it holds water (oliguric), then it opens the gates (diuretic), so watch for drying out.","principle":"Read the direction of several values together before naming the phase.","takeaway":"In trends, the movie matters more than any single frame. Several values moving together tell you the phase.","safer":{"s":"Interpret the trend and choose the focus.","a":"Output 320 to 1900 mL/24 h; BUN 74 to 46; creatinine 4.4 to 2.9.","f":"Trend interpretation: phases of AKI.","e":"Oliguric fits days 1–2 only; full recovery is too early; overload contradicts the phase.","r":"Diuretic phase with dehydration risk fits every value on day 5."},"compare":{"a":1,"b":2,"aWhy":"Rising output with improving labs matches the diuretic phase.","bWhy":"Every value is moving in the right direction.","decider":"Recovery takes months, and the diuretic phase carries its own dehydration risk.","flip":"Months later, with stable normal output and labs near baseline, recovery would fit."},"src":"Urinary_System_Chapters_1-6.pdf","pg":"pp84–85"},{"id":"XE13","ver":2,"tier":"expert","cog":"analysis","subj":"Adult GI","topic":"Cues of worsening hepatic encephalopathy","cn":"Physiological Adaptation","cn2":"Reduction of Risk Potential","fmt":"highlight","diff":"A","ngn":true,"cj":"Recognize cues","ask":"Interpret","comp":"cue","sameFormExclude":["G03","XE08"],"stem":"A client with cirrhosis is being monitored for hepatic encephalopathy. Click to highlight each finding in the nurse's note that suggests the encephalopathy is worsening.","segs":[{"t":"0800 Nurse's note: ","s":false},{"t":"Oriented to person only; was oriented ×3 yesterday.","s":true},{"t":" ","s":false},{"t":"Flapping tremor of both hands when the arms are extended.","s":true},{"t":" ","s":false},{"t":"Abdominal girth up 2 cm since yesterday.","s":true},{"t":" ","s":false},{"t":"Twitching of the arms and legs while resting.","s":true},{"t":" ","s":false},{"t":"Fetor hepaticus noted on the breath.","s":true},{"t":" ","s":false},{"t":"2+ edema of both ankles.","s":true},{"t":" ","s":false},{"t":"Ate half of breakfast.","s":true}],"key":[1,3,7,9],"rat":"Hepatic encephalopathy is brain damage from ammonia intoxication. Its signs progress from inappropriate behavior and disorientation to asterixis (a flapping tremor) and twitching of the extremities, then stupor and coma; the nurse is also alert for fetor hepaticus. Increasing girth and ankle edema reflect fluid retention, a different complication of cirrhosis.","why":{"5":"Rising girth reflects ascites from fluid retention, a different complication.","11":"Ankle edema reflects fluid retention, not encephalopathy.","13":"Intake is worth noting but is not a sign of encephalopathy."},"traps":{"5":"otherdx","11":"otherdx","13":"irrelevant"},"cues":"Orientation change, asterixis, twitching, fetor hepaticus.","aid":"Ammonia climbs the ladder: confused, then flapping, then twitching, then stupor.","principle":"Highlight only cues that support the specific judgment asked about, not every abnormal finding.","takeaway":"Two problems can share one patient. Ascites and edema are real, but they belong to fluid retention, not encephalopathy.","safer":{"s":"Highlight cues of worsening encephalopathy only.","a":"Disorientation, asterixis, twitching, fetor hepaticus, girth, edema, intake.","f":"Cue recognition: neurologic signs of ammonia intoxication.","e":"Girth and edema belong to fluid retention; intake is unrelated.","r":"The four neurologic signs directly support the judgment."},"src":"GI_Linear_Chapter_Sourcebook.pdf","pg":"pp122, 136–137"},{"id":"XE14","ver":2,"tier":"expert","cog":"application","subj":"Renal/Urinary","topic":"Supervising care of a client with an AV fistula","cn":"Coordinated Care","cn2":"Reduction of Risk Potential","fmt":"sata","diff":"I","ask":"Scope","comp":"scope","stem":"The LVN is supervising a UAP caring for a client with a left-arm arteriovenous fistula for hemodialysis. Which actions by the UAP require the LVN to intervene? Select all that apply.","opts":["Takes the blood pressure on the left arm","Bathes the client with tepid water","Places a sign above the bed identifying the access arm","Tells the lab the left arm has larger veins for the blood draw","Reports that the client says the buzzing at the fistula feels weaker today"],"key":[0,3],"rat":"After dialysis and between treatments, signs are posted identifying the access site, blood pressure and venipuncture are not done on the access arm, the fistula is checked for a bruit and thrill, changes are reported, and the client is bathed with tepid water to remove urea deposits.","why":{"1":"Tepid-water bathing removes urea deposits and is appropriate.","2":"Posting signs about the access site is a listed intervention.","4":"Reporting a change in the thrill is exactly what the UAP should do."},"traps":{"1":"irrelevant","2":"irrelevant","4":"irrelevant"},"cues":"Left-arm fistula: no BP and no venipuncture on that arm.","aid":"Fistula arm = 'no-stick, no-squeeze' zone.","principle":"Supervision means stopping unsafe actions and supporting correct reporting.","takeaway":"A UAP who reports a change is doing the job right. Intervene on actions, not on good reports.","safer":{"s":"Select the actions that need intervention.","a":"Left-arm fistula; BP, bathing, signage, lab draw, report of weaker thrill.","f":"Supervision and access-site protection.","e":"Bathing, signage, and reporting are correct.","r":"BP and venipuncture on the access arm both threaten the fistula."},"src":"Urinary_System_Chapters_1-6.pdf","pg":"p91","sameFormExclude":[]},{"id":"XE15","ver":2,"tier":"expert","cog":"judgment","subj":"Psychiatric","topic":"Prioritizing on a mental health unit","cn":"Psychosocial Integrity","cn2":"Safety and Infection Prevention and Control","fmt":"mc","diff":"A","ask":"Prioritize","comp":"priority","sameFormExclude":["P02","B30"],"stem":"After report on a mental health unit, which client should the nurse see first?","opts":["A client in a manic phase who is shouting and arguing about the TV rules","A client with schizophrenia who hears voices but is calmly watching TV","A client with depression who gave away a prized guitar and talks about the hereafter","A client with an anxiety disorder asking for a PRN dose before group therapy"],"key":2,"rat":"Warning signs of suicide include giving away prized possessions, talking about death or the hereafter, and withdrawing from others. Safety outranks routine therapeutic care. Manic clients tend to be argumentative and need calm, firm limits; hallucinations call for watching for growing agitation.","why":{"0":"Arguing is common in mania and needs firm limits, but it is not the most immediate danger.","1":"The client is calm; the nurse watches for growing agitation.","3":"A PRN request is a lower-acuity need."},"traps":{"0":"lower","1":"lower","3":"lower"},"cues":"Gave away a prized possession and talking about the hereafter.","aid":"Giving things away + talking about the end = check for a plan now.","principle":"Risk of self-harm outranks disruptive but nonlethal behavior.","takeaway":"The loudest client is not always the priority. Quiet suicide warning signs come first.","safer":{"s":"Choose the client to see first.","a":"Gave away a guitar and talking about the hereafter; loud arguing; calm hallucinations; PRN request.","f":"Psychiatric safety: suicide risk.","e":"Arguing, calm hallucinations, and a PRN request are lower risk right now.","r":"Suicide warning signs beat the arguing client because the risk is lethal."},"compare":{"a":2,"b":0,"aWhy":"Two warning signs of suicide are present.","bWhy":"Loud arguing is disruptive and could escalate.","decider":"Suicide risk is a direct threat to life; arguing in mania calls for limits but is less immediately dangerous.","flip":"If the manic client were threatening someone or becoming physically aggressive, that client could come first."},"src":"Psychiatric_Nursing_Complete_Linear_Study_Packet.pdf","pg":"Teaching slide 19 (slide sections pp. 25–164); pp. 177–193 (mania); pp. 83–114 (schizophrenia)"},{"id":"V01","ver":2,"cog":"analysis","subj":"Fundamentals","topic":"Mechanical soft diet tray check","cn":"Basic Care and Comfort","cn2":"Safety and Infection Prevention and Control","fmt":"sata","diff":"I","ask":"Intervene","comp":"intervention","stem":"A client on a mechanical soft diet receives a lunch tray. Which items should the nurse remove before the client eats? Select all that apply.","opts":["Toasted bagel","Ground chicken in gravy","Popcorn","Mashed potatoes","Peanut butter","Raw broccoli salad","Pickles","Scrambled eggs"],"key":[0,2,4,5,6],"rat":"A mechanical soft diet uses soft foods that need less chewing, such as ground or chopped meat and soft fruits and vegetables. The instructor's diet slide lists foods to avoid: bread products such as bagels, popcorn, peanut butter, raw vegetables and salads, and olives and pickles. Mashed potatoes and scrambled eggs need no chewing.","why":{"1":"Ground or chopped meat is the model mechanical soft protein.","3":"Mashed potatoes need little or no chewing.","7":"Scrambled eggs are soft and easy to chew."},"traps":{},"cues":"Each item judged against 'needs less chewing.'","aid":"Mechanical soft: if it crunches, chews, sticks, or snaps, it goes back.","principle":"Treat every select-all option as its own true-or-false decision.","takeaway":"Soft and ground stays; crunchy, sticky, raw, and bready go.","safer":{"s":"Select the items to remove.","a":"Bagel, popcorn, peanut butter, raw salad, pickles versus ground meat, mashed potatoes, eggs.","f":"Therapeutic diet safety.","e":"Ground chicken, mashed potatoes, and eggs fit the diet.","r":"The five crunchy, sticky, raw, or bready items come off the tray."},"src":"Fundamentals HESI review 3.pdf","pg":"pp53–54","sameFormExclude":[]},{"id":"V02","ver":2,"cog":"application","subj":"Pain & Opioids","topic":"Opioid urinary hesitancy","cn":"Basic Care and Comfort","cn2":"Pharmacological Therapies","fmt":"mc","diff":"I","ask":"Intervene","comp":"intervention","stem":"A client receiving morphine after surgery says, \"I feel like I need to go, but nothing will come out.\" The last void was 6 hours ago. Respirations are 16/min. What should the nurse do first?","opts":["Insert a straight catheter to drain the bladder","Assess the lower abdomen for bladder distention","Restrict fluids until the client is able to void","Give the next morphine dose to relax the bladder"],"key":1,"rat":"Opiate agonists can cause spasms of the ureters and bladder, leading to urinary retention and difficulty starting the stream. If urinary hesitancy develops, the nurse assesses for bladder distention, reports it to the provider, and tries to stimulate urination by running water or placing the client's hands in water.","why":{"0":"Catheterization is a routine PN procedure, but it requires an order and comes after assessment.","2":"Restricting fluids does not relieve retention.","3":"More opioid can worsen retention."},"traps":{"0":"premature","2":"irrelevant","3":"unsafe"},"cues":"Opioid, urge without flow, 6 hours since voiding.","aid":"Opioid can't-go: check the bladder, run the water, report.","principle":"Stable client with a new problem: assess before you intervene.","takeaway":"Assess for distention first; invasive steps come after assessment and orders.","safer":{"s":"Choose the first action.","a":"Hesitancy on morphine, 6 hours since voiding, breathing normal.","f":"Nursing process: stable client, assess first.","e":"Catheterizing is premature, restricting fluids is irrelevant, more morphine worsens it.","r":"Assessing for distention comes before catheterization."},"compare":{"a":1,"b":0,"aWhy":"It confirms retention and gives the provider useful data.","bWhy":"Catheterization relieves retention and is within PN scope.","decider":"The client is stable, so the next step is assessment; catheterization needs an order after findings are reported.","flip":"With confirmed distention and a catheterization order, inserting the catheter becomes the next step."},"src":"Pain_Management_and_Urinary_System_Combined.pdf","pg":"p5","sameFormExclude":[]},{"id":"V03","ver":2,"cog":"application","subj":"Musculoskeletal","topic":"Care of a wet plaster cast","cn":"Basic Care and Comfort","cn2":"Reduction of Risk Potential","fmt":"sata","diff":"I","ask":"Intervene","comp":"intervention","stem":"A plaster cast was applied to a client's forearm 2 hours ago. Which actions are appropriate? Select all that apply.","opts":["Handle the cast with the palms of the hands","Turn the extremity every 1 to 2 hours unless contraindicated","Use a hair dryer on the hot setting to speed drying","Lift the cast with the fingertips to avoid smudging it","Petal the cast edges or apply moleskin","Monitor for hot spots, foul odor, or changes in pain"],"key":[0,1,4,5],"rat":"A wet plaster cast takes 24–72 hours to dry. It is handled with the palms, not fingertips, and the extremity is turned every 1–2 hours to circulate air. A hair dryer may be used only on a cool setting because heat makes the cast hot enough to burn the skin. Edges are petaled or covered with moleskin, and the nurse monitors for circulatory impairment and infection (fever, hot spots, foul odor, changes in pain).","why":{"2":"Heat warms the plaster and can burn the skin; only a cool setting is used.","3":"Fingertips dent a wet cast; the palms are used."},"traps":{"2":"unsafe","3":"unsafe"},"cues":"Plaster, 2 hours old: still wet.","aid":"Wet plaster: palms, turn, cool air, petal, sniff and feel for trouble.","principle":"Judge each option independently against the cast-care rules.","takeaway":"Palms, not fingertips; cool air, never hot.","safer":{"s":"Select appropriate cast care.","a":"Wet plaster cast.","f":"Basic care: cast drying and complication watch.","e":"Hot air burns; fingertips dent the cast.","r":"Palms, turning, petaling, and infection monitoring fit."},"src":"Musculoskeletal_Complete_Project_Combined (2).pdf","pg":"Slides pp. 3–249 (cast care)","sameFormExclude":[]},{"id":"V04","ver":2,"cog":"analysis","subj":"Musculoskeletal","topic":"Preventing contracture after amputation","cn":"Basic Care and Comfort","cn2":"Reduction of Risk Potential","fmt":"mc","diff":"A","ask":"Intervene","comp":"intervention","stem":"Two days after an above-knee amputation, which plan best prevents hip flexion and abduction contractures?","opts":["Teach abduction exercises to strengthen the hip and keep the limb propped on pillows","Position the client prone for periods and teach adduction and extension exercises every 4 hours","Limit turning to the back and the unaffected side to protect the incision","Keep the client sitting in a chair for most of the day to build strength"],"key":1,"rat":"After amputation, the client is turned onto the side, back, and abdomen (after 24 hours) to maintain range of motion, and adduction and extension exercises are taught and performed every 4 hours to prevent abduction and flexion contractures. The purposes of prone and extension positions are taught.","why":{"0":"Abduction exercises and propping the limb flexed work against the adduction and extension the source recommends.","2":"The client is turned onto the abdomen as well after 24 hours.","3":"Prolonged sitting keeps the hip flexed; prone and extension positions prevent flexion contracture."},"traps":{"0":"otherdx","2":"later","3":"unsafe"},"cues":"Contracture prevention = prone and extension.","aid":"Lie flat, stay straight, squeeze in: prone, extension, adduction.","principle":"Choose the intervention that directly counters the specific risk named.","takeaway":"Prone positioning and adduction-extension exercises prevent the contractures that block a prosthesis.","safer":{"s":"Choose the plan that prevents contractures.","a":"Above-knee amputation, day 2, risk of flexion and abduction contractures.","f":"Mobility and positioning.","e":"Abduction and propping, avoiding prone, and prolonged sitting promote contracture.","r":"Prone periods with adduction and extension exercises counter both risks."},"src":"Musculoskeletal_Complete_Project_Combined (2).pdf","pg":"Ch. 44 textbook sections pp. 250–351 (amputation patient problems)","sameFormExclude":[]},{"id":"V05","ver":2,"cog":"application","subj":"Fundamentals","topic":"When oropharyngeal suctioning is needed","cn":"Basic Care and Comfort","cn2":"Physiological Adaptation","fmt":"sata","diff":"I","ask":"Interpret","comp":"cue","stem":"A client who had a stroke has weak cough and swallow. Which findings indicate the need for oropharyngeal suctioning? Select all that apply.","opts":["Noisy, gurgling breathing","Secretions visible in the mouth","SpO₂ falling from 96% to 91%","Heart rate rising from 84 to 102","Blood pressure 118/74","Asking for a drink of water"],"key":[0,1,2,3],"rat":"Indications for suctioning include noisy breathing, excessive visible or audible secretions, increased or decreased respirations, increased heart rate, decreased oxygen saturation, prolonged expiratory sounds, diminished air entry, and altered chest movement. Oropharyngeal suctioning with a Yankauer catheter removes secretions from the oral cavity and pharynx; the nurse checks scope of practice and facility policy before assisting.","why":{"4":"A normal blood pressure is not a suctioning indication.","5":"A request for water raises a swallowing-safety question, not a suctioning indication."},"traps":{"4":"irrelevant","5":"otherdx"},"cues":"Gurgling, visible secretions, falling SpO₂, rising heart rate.","aid":"Suction when you can Hear it, See it, or the monitors Show it (SpO₂ down, HR up).","principle":"Cluster airway cues; ignore stable unrelated data.","takeaway":"Gurgles, visible secretions, falling saturation, and rising pulse mean clear the airway.","safer":{"s":"Select suctioning indications.","a":"Gurgling, visible secretions, SpO₂ 96 to 91, HR 84 to 102.","f":"Airway clearance.","e":"Normal BP and a water request are not indications.","r":"The four airway and oxygenation cues indicate suctioning."},"src":"Fundamentals HESI review 3.pdf","pg":"pp56–57","sameFormExclude":[]},{"id":"V06","ver":2,"cog":"analysis","subj":"Fundamentals","topic":"Matching therapeutic diets","cn":"Basic Care and Comfort","cn2":"Health Promotion and Maintenance","fmt":"matrix","diff":"I","ngn":true,"cj":"Generate solutions","ask":"Intervene","comp":"intervention","stem":"For each condition, select the diet the nurse should expect.","rows":["Pancreatitis","Phenylketonuria (PKU)","Acute or chronic kidney disease","Pernicious anemia"],"cols":["Low fat, small frequent feedings","Low protein, low phenylalanine","Protein restricted, adequate calories","High protein, high vitamin B"],"key":[0,1,2,3],"rat":"The instructor's therapeutic diet slide pairs pancreatitis with low-fat, small frequent feedings and no alcohol; PKU with low protein and low phenylalanine; renal disease (AKI, CKD) with protein restriction and calories; and pernicious anemia with high protein and high vitamin B.","why":{},"traps":{},"aid":"Pancreas hates fat, PKU hates phe, kidneys hate protein load, pernicious anemia needs B.","principle":"Match the diet to the organ or pathway that is failing.","takeaway":"Each diet protects a specific organ or replaces a specific deficit.","safer":{"s":"Match each condition to its diet.","a":"Four conditions, four diets.","f":"Therapeutic nutrition.","e":"One diet per row.","r":"Pancreatitis low fat; PKU low phenylalanine; renal protein restricted; pernicious anemia high B."},"src":"Fundamentals HESI review 3.pdf","pg":"p53","sameFormExclude":[]},{"id":"V07","ver":2,"cog":"application","subj":"Pediatrics","topic":"Tonsillectomy discharge teaching","cn":"Health Promotion and Maintenance","cn2":"Reduction of Risk Potential","fmt":"mc","diff":"I","ask":"Evaluate","comp":"teach","sameFormExclude":["XE02","K09"],"stem":"The nurse reinforces discharge teaching with the parent of a child after tonsillectomy. Which statement indicates a need for further teaching?","opts":["\"I'll offer apple juice, water, and grape juice often.\"","\"I'll have him gargle with warm salt water to keep his throat clean.\"","\"I'll call right away if I see bleeding, even a week from now.\"","\"I'll avoid giving him chips, crunchy foods, or foods with seeds.\""],"key":1,"rat":"Discharge instructions after tonsillectomy include encouraging fluids such as apple juice, water, and grape juice; avoiding irritating, highly seasoned, crunchy, or seeded foods; avoiding gargles and vigorous tooth brushing; avoiding coughing or throat clearing; using mild analgesics or an ice collar; no aspirin; and calling immediately for bleeding, which is possible 5–10 days after surgery as tissue sloughs.","why":{"0":"Correct: these fluids are recommended.","2":"Correct: bleeding can occur 5–10 days after surgery.","3":"Correct: crunchy and seeded foods are avoided."},"traps":{"0":"irrelevant","2":"irrelevant","3":"irrelevant"},"cues":"Gargling after tonsillectomy.","aid":"After tonsils: no gargle, no crunch, no aspirin; watch days 5–10.","principle":"In negative stems, look for the unsafe statement.","takeaway":"Gargling can disturb the healing tonsil beds.","safer":{"s":"Find the statement needing more teaching.","a":"Gargling plan.","f":"Teaching evaluation: post-tonsillectomy.","e":"Fluids, bleeding watch, and avoiding crunchy foods are correct.","r":"Gargles are to be avoided."},"src":"PEDS_Complete_Linear_Word_Sources_2026.pdf","pg":"pp. 154–210 (Tonsillectomy discharge instructions)"},{"id":"V08","ver":2,"cog":"application","subj":"Maternal-Newborn","topic":"Teaching parents to prevent infant abduction","cn":"Safety and Infection Prevention and Control","cn2":"Health Promotion and Maintenance","fmt":"sata","diff":"I","ask":"Teach","comp":"teach","sameFormExclude":["B10","B11"],"stem":"Which instructions should the nurse include when teaching new parents how to prevent infant abduction? Select all that apply.","opts":["Give the baby only to staff with the correct identification","If no one else is in the room, take the baby into the bathroom with you","Leave the baby in the hallway bassinet while you shower","Avoid putting a sign in your yard announcing the new baby","Let anyone in scrubs take the baby for tests","Keep the baby under your watch at all times"],"key":[0,1,3,5],"rat":"Box 28.12 teaches parents that the infant is observed at all times, that the mother can take the infant into the bathroom with her or send the infant to the nursery if no family member is present, that the infant is given only to staff with proper identification, and that parents avoid media announcements or yard signs about a new baby.","why":{"2":"Infants are never left unattended in a hallway.","4":"Scrubs are not identification."},"traps":{"2":"unsafe","4":"unsafe"},"cues":"","aid":"Eyes on baby, ID on staff, no yard signs.","principle":"Teach the behaviors that close each opportunity for abduction.","takeaway":"Only staff with correct ID take the baby, and the baby is never unattended.","safer":{"s":"Select correct parent teaching.","a":"Infant security.","f":"Safety teaching.","e":"Unattended hallway bassinets and trusting scrubs are unsafe.","r":"ID checks, bathroom with mom, no yard signs, constant watch."},"src":"Maternal_Health_Linear_Chapter_Sources_Updated.pdf","pg":"pp. 157–168 (Box 28.12)"},{"id":"V09","ver":2,"cog":"analysis","subj":"Hematology","topic":"Neutropenic precautions in the room","cn":"Safety and Infection Prevention and Control","cn2":"Health Promotion and Maintenance","fmt":"sata","diff":"I","ask":"Intervene","comp":"intervention","sameFormExclude":["H03"],"stem":"A client with agranulocytosis is on neutropenic precautions. Which actions are appropriate? Select all that apply.","opts":["Remove a vase of fresh flowers a visitor brought","Offer a high-protein, high-calorie soft diet","Ask a visitor with a cold to wait until recovered","Serve a raw vegetable salad for extra vitamins","Teach the client and visitors handwashing","Let a staff member with a mild cough wear a mask and give care"],"key":[0,1,2,4],"rat":"For potential infection from depressed white cell production, the nurse institutes neutropenic precautions, restricts visitors and personnel with bacterial or viral infections, teaches handwashing to the client and visitors, avoids fresh flowers and plants, provides a high-protein, high-vitamin, high-calorie soft diet, and avoids raw foods.","why":{"3":"Raw foods are avoided.","5":"Personnel with infections are kept from caring for the client; a mask doesn't change that."},"traps":{"3":"unsafe","5":"otherdx"},"cues":"ANC very low.","aid":"Neutropenic room: no flowers, no raw food, no sick people.","principle":"Remove every listed source of infection.","takeaway":"A mask doesn't make a sick caregiver safe for a neutropenic client.","safer":{"s":"Select appropriate actions.","a":"Agranulocytosis on neutropenic precautions.","f":"Infection prevention.","e":"Raw salad and a masked sick staff member add risk.","r":"Remove flowers, soft high-calorie diet, turn away sick visitors, teach handwashing."},"src":"Blood_and_Lymph_Complete_Linear_Source_Packet.pdf","pg":"p38"},{"id":"V10","ver":2,"cog":"analysis","subj":"Pain & Opioids","topic":"Orthostatic hypotension after a first opioid dose","cn":"Safety and Infection Prevention and Control","cn2":"Pharmacological Therapies","fmt":"mc","diff":"I","ask":"Intervene","comp":"med","stem":"Thirty minutes after a first dose of morphine, a client wants to walk to the bathroom and says, \"I feel a little lightheaded.\" Respirations are 16/min. Which action is best?","opts":["Let the client walk alone because respirations are normal","Have the client stay lying down, check the blood pressure, and assist with toileting","Give the next morphine dose early so pain won't limit walking","Encourage the client to stand up quickly to get past the dizziness"],"key":1,"rat":"Opiate agonists commonly cause lightheadedness, dizziness, and orthostatic hypotension, especially when therapy starts in a client who is not supine. Symptoms are reduced by keeping the client supine, blood pressure is monitored closely when the client reports dizziness or faintness, and safety is provided.","why":{"0":"Normal respirations don't address the fall risk from orthostatic hypotension.","2":"More opioid worsens dizziness and sedation.","3":"Standing quickly worsens orthostatic hypotension."},"traps":{"0":"isolated","2":"unsafe","3":"unsafe"},"cues":"First dose, lightheaded, wants to walk.","aid":"First dose of an opioid: down before up, check the pressure.","principle":"Reassuring breathing does not cancel a fall risk.","takeaway":"Lightheadedness after an opioid means check BP and assist; stay supine if possible.","safer":{"s":"Choose the best action.","a":"First morphine dose, lightheaded, RR 16.","f":"Medication safety: WATCH for orthostatic hypotension.","e":"Walking alone, an early dose, and standing quickly increase falls.","r":"Supine, BP check, and assistance protect the client."},"med":{"why":"Pain.","check":"Baseline BP and orientation before the first dose.","give":"With the client supine when starting therapy.","watch":"Orthostatic hypotension, dizziness, sedation.","teach":"Call for help before getting up."},"src":"Pain_Management_and_Urinary_System_Combined.pdf","pg":"p4","sameFormExclude":[]},{"id":"XE16","ver":2,"tier":"expert","cog":"judgment","subj":"Musculoskeletal","topic":"Which client first: time-critical ischemia","cn":"Coordinated Care","cn2":"Physiological Adaptation","fmt":"mc","diff":"A","ask":"Prioritize","comp":"priority","sameFormExclude":["S01"],"stem":"After report, which client should the LVN see first?","opts":["A client with cirrhosis whose abdominal girth is up 1 cm since yesterday","A client whose new leg cast was applied 5 hours ago and who has pain unrelieved by morphine that worsens with passive toe movement","A client taking lithium whose 12-hour level was drawn this morning and who has no symptoms","A client in the diuretic phase of acute kidney injury with 1.8 L of output yesterday who is asking for water"],"key":1,"rat":"Pain unrelieved by analgesics that increases with passive movement signals compartment syndrome, and irreversible muscle ischemia can occur within 6 hours; this client has about an hour left. Daily girth changes in cirrhosis, a pending lithium level without symptoms, and dehydration risk in the diuretic phase all need follow-up but are not time-critical.","why":{"0":"Girth is monitored daily for fluid retention; a 1 cm change needs tracking, not an emergency response.","2":"The level needs follow-up when it results, but the client has no toxicity signs now.","3":"Dehydration may occur in the diuretic phase; offering fluids and monitoring are appropriate after the urgent client."},"traps":{"0":"lower","2":"lower","3":"lower"},"cues":"Pain unrelieved by morphine, worse with passive movement, 5 hours after casting.","aid":"Some problems have a clock: compartment syndrome's runs out at 6 hours.","principle":"When several clients need care, the one with an irreversible time limit comes first.","takeaway":"Ask not only 'how sick' but 'how soon is damage permanent.'","safer":{"s":"Choose the client to see first.","a":"Compartment syndrome signs at 5 hours; girth +1 cm; pending lithium level; diuretic-phase AKI.","f":"Priority ladder: actual, time-critical threat.","e":"Girth, pending level, and expected diuresis are lower priority.","r":"The cast client wins because ischemia becomes irreversible within 6 hours."},"compare":{"a":1,"b":3,"aWhy":"Compartment syndrome threatens the limb within the hour.","bWhy":"High output in the diuretic phase can cause dehydration.","decider":"The cast client has an imminent irreversible deadline; the AKI client needs fluids and monitoring, which can follow shortly.","flip":"If the AKI client showed signs of dehydration such as dizziness and very low blood pressure, that client could move up."},"src":"Musculoskeletal_Complete_Project_Combined (2).pdf","pg":"pp. 283–313 (compartment syndrome); Urinary pp84–85; GI p137"},{"id":"XE17","ver":2,"tier":"expert","cog":"application","subj":"Fundamentals","topic":"Delegating within UAP scope","cn":"Coordinated Care","cn2":"","fmt":"mc","diff":"I","ask":"Scope","comp":"scope","sameFormExclude":["T01"],"stem":"Which task should the LVN assign to the UAP?","opts":["Take vital signs on a client who received naloxone 10 minutes ago","Measure and record urine output from the indwelling catheter of a stable client","Feed lunch to a client with dysphagia who coughs on thin liquids","Check the pedal pulses of a client with a new long-leg cast"],"key":1,"rat":"The instructor's scope slide gives the UAP documenting input and output, routine stable vital signs, ADLs, and feeding only when there is no aspiration risk. Neurovascular checks are in the PN column, and vital signs right after an opioid reversal are neither routine nor stable.","why":{"0":"After naloxone, vital signs are taken at frequent intervals because depression can return; this is not routine or stable.","2":"Coughing on thin liquids is aspiration risk, which excludes UAP feeding.","3":"Neurovascular checks are a PN task."},"traps":{"0":"scope","2":"scope","3":"scope"},"cues":"Stable client, measuring and recording.","aid":"UAP: measure, record, report; never judge, teach, or assess.","principle":"Delegate tasks that are routine and stable and need no nursing judgment.","takeaway":"I&O on a stable client is classic UAP work.","safer":{"s":"Choose the delegable task.","a":"Post-naloxone vitals, stable I&O, dysphagia feeding, pulse checks on a new cast.","f":"Delegation and scope.","e":"Unstable vitals, aspiration-risk feeding, and neurovascular checks need the nurse.","r":"Recording output on a stable client is routine."},"src":"Fundamentals HESI review 3.pdf","pg":"p59; Pain_Management_and_Urinary p15"},{"id":"XE18","ver":2,"tier":"expert","cog":"application","subj":"Fundamentals","topic":"What the LVN can give","cn":"Coordinated Care","cn2":"Pharmacological Therapies","fmt":"mc","diff":"I","ask":"Scope","comp":"scope","sameFormExclude":["B01"],"stem":"A client has four new prescriptions. Which one can the LVN carry out without asking the RN?","opts":["Furosemide 40 mg IV push now","Hang the first bag of IV normal saline at 100 mL/hr","Naloxone 0.4 mg IM now for respiratory depression","Transfuse 1 unit of packed red blood cells"],"key":2,"rat":"On the instructor's PN scope slide, the LPN/LVN administers injections and narcotics but not IV medications or the first IV bag, and monitors IV fluids and blood products that the RN starts. Starting blood products and IV medications are RN tasks.","why":{"0":"IV push medications are outside the PN column.","1":"The first IV bag is excluded from the PN column.","3":"Starting blood products is an RN task."},"traps":{"0":"scope","1":"scope","3":"scope"},"cues":"IM injection versus IV and blood.","aid":"LVN gives the shot; RN starts the line, the push, and the blood.","principle":"Sort orders by route and role before acting.","takeaway":"Injections and narcotics yes; IV meds, first bag, and blood no.","safer":{"s":"Find the order within LVN scope.","a":"IV push, first IV bag, IM injection, blood.","f":"Scope of practice.","e":"IV push, first bag, and blood are RN tasks.","r":"The IM injection is within PN scope."},"src":"Fundamentals HESI review 3.pdf","pg":"p59"},{"id":"XE19","ver":2,"tier":"expert","cog":"judgment","subj":"Endocrine","topic":"Unresponsive with low glucose and no IV","cn":"Physiological Adaptation","cn2":"Coordinated Care","fmt":"mc","diff":"A","ask":"Intervene","comp":"intervention","sameFormExclude":["E01"],"stem":"A client with diabetes is found unresponsive with a fingerstick glucose of 40 mg/dL. The client has no IV access. Glucagon IM is ordered PRN. What should the LVN do first?","opts":["Place orange juice in the client's mouth with a straw","Call the RN to give 50% dextrose IV","Give glucagon IM as ordered and call for help","Recheck the glucose in 15 minutes to confirm it"],"key":2,"rat":"Treatment of hypoglycemia starts with deciding whether the client is conscious. Oral carbohydrate is for alert clients; IV 50% dextrose is given only by the RN and requires IV access; IM glucagon is the route for a client who cannot take oral glucose. Injections are within the PN column.","why":{"0":"An unresponsive client cannot safely swallow; oral glucose risks aspiration.","1":"There is no IV access, so IV dextrose would be delayed.","3":"Rechecking delays treatment of a confirmed low glucose in an unresponsive client."},"traps":{"0":"unsafe","1":"delay","3":"delay"},"cues":"Unresponsive, glucose 40, no IV, glucagon ordered.","aid":"Low and awake: juice. Low and out with no line: glucagon in the muscle.","principle":"Pick the effective route that is available now and within role.","takeaway":"No IV and no swallow means IM glucagon, which the LVN can give.","safer":{"s":"Choose the first action.","a":"Unresponsive, glucose 40, no IV access, glucagon ordered.","f":"Emergency: hypoglycemia with altered consciousness.","e":"Juice risks aspiration, IV dextrose has no access, rechecking delays.","r":"IM glucagon works now and is within PN scope."},"compare":{"a":2,"b":1,"aWhy":"Glucagon IM raises glucose without IV access.","bWhy":"IV 50% dextrose is a listed treatment for severe hypoglycemia.","decider":"There is no IV access, so dextrose would be delayed; glucagon can be given immediately.","flip":"If an IV were already in place and the RN at the bedside, IV dextrose by the RN would also work."},"src":"Med_Surg_All_Sources_Linear_Master_2026.pdf","pg":"pp. 349–415 (Endocrine/DM slides 44–45); Fundamentals p59"},{"id":"XE20","ver":2,"tier":"expert","cog":"judgment","subj":"Pain & Opioids","topic":"Naloxone in an opioid-dependent client","cn":"Pharmacological Therapies","cn2":"Physiological Adaptation","fmt":"mc","diff":"A","ask":"Intervene","comp":"med","sameFormExclude":["CASE5","T04"],"stem":"A client who has taken opioids daily for months for cancer pain is difficult to arouse with respirations of 8/min. The RN will give naloxone as ordered. Which plan should the nurse anticipate?","opts":["The largest available dose at once so breathing returns quickly","Holding naloxone because dependent clients cannot receive it","Small doses repeated every 2–3 minutes until breathing improves, with close watch for withdrawal","Naloxone followed by permanently stopping all pain medication"],"key":2,"rat":"Naloxone is the drug of choice for opiate-induced respiratory depression. In postoperative opiate depression it is given 0.1–0.2 mg IV every 2–3 minutes until the desired response. It is given with caution to clients who are physically dependent on opioids because it can precipitate severe withdrawal, and severity depends on the dose. Prior opioid use is checked before giving it.","why":{"0":"A large dose in a dependent client can precipitate severe withdrawal.","1":"Naloxone is used with caution, not withheld, when breathing is depressed.","3":"Pain still needs treatment; the provider adjusts the plan."},"traps":{"0":"invasive","1":"unsafe","3":"invasive"},"cues":"Daily opioids for months, RR 8.","aid":"Dependent client: reverse the breathing, not the whole drug; small doses, watch for withdrawal.","principle":"Treat the emergency while limiting harm from the treatment.","takeaway":"In opioid dependence, titrate naloxone to breathing and expect withdrawal.","safer":{"s":"Anticipate the naloxone plan.","a":"Long-term opioid use, RR 8, difficult to arouse.","f":"Medication safety: reversal in dependence.","e":"A big dose precipitates withdrawal, withholding ignores the emergency, stopping all analgesia is too aggressive.","r":"Small titrated doses with withdrawal monitoring."},"med":{"why":"Opiate-induced respiratory depression.","check":"Prior opioid use or dependence; baseline neuro status.","give":"Titrated doses (postoperative: 0.1–0.2 mg IV every 2–3 minutes) by the RN.","watch":"Breathing, withdrawal (nausea, sweating, tachycardia), return of depression.","teach":"Explain why pain treatment will be adjusted, not stopped."},"src":"Pain_Management_and_Urinary_System_Combined.pdf","pg":"pp15–17"},{"id":"XE21","ver":2,"tier":"expert","cog":"analysis","subj":"Psychiatric","topic":"A new prescription for a client on lithium","cn":"Pharmacological Therapies","cn2":"Reduction of Risk Potential","fmt":"mc","diff":"A","ask":"Clarify","comp":"med","sameFormExclude":["CASE4"],"stem":"A client who takes lithium is seen for back pain. Which new prescription should the nurse clarify with the provider?","opts":["Acetaminophen 650 mg by mouth every 6 hours as needed","Ibuprofen 400 mg by mouth three times daily","Encourage 10 to 12 eight-ounce glasses of water daily","Lithium level drawn 12 hours after the last dose"],"key":1,"rat":"NSAIDs such as ibuprofen raise serum lithium levels and can push a client into toxicity. Adequate fluid intake (10–12 eight-ounce glasses daily) and a level drawn 12 hours after the dose are part of safe lithium therapy.","why":{"0":"No lithium interaction is listed for acetaminophen in the source.","2":"The source recommends this fluid intake.","3":"Levels are drawn 12 hours after the last dose."},"traps":{"0":"irrelevant","2":"irrelevant","3":"irrelevant"},"cues":"NSAID plus lithium.","aid":"Lithium's enemies: NSAIDs, low salt, sweating, and diarrhea.","principle":"Screen new orders against a narrow-range drug's interactions.","takeaway":"An everyday NSAID can cause lithium toxicity.","safer":{"s":"Find the order to clarify.","a":"Lithium plus new ibuprofen.","f":"Medication safety: interaction.","e":"Acetaminophen, fluids, and the 12-hour level are appropriate.","r":"Ibuprofen raises lithium levels."},"med":{"why":"Bipolar disorder.","check":"Interacting drugs (NSAIDs), sodium and fluid status, 12-hour levels.","give":"As prescribed with adequate fluids.","watch":"Tremor, GI upset, lethargy, hyperreflexia.","teach":"Avoid NSAIDs unless approved; keep salt and fluids steady."},"src":"Psychiatric_Nursing_Complete_Linear_Study_Packet.pdf","pg":"pp. 257–280 (lithium)"},{"id":"XE22","ver":2,"tier":"expert","cog":"analysis","subj":"Fluids & Electrolytes","topic":"Drugs that raise potassium","cn":"Pharmacological Therapies","cn2":"Reduction of Risk Potential","fmt":"sata","diff":"A","ask":"Interpret","comp":"med","stem":"A client with chronic kidney disease has a potassium level above the reference range. Which home medications could be contributing? Select all that apply.","opts":["Lisinopril","Losartan","Spironolactone","Ibuprofen","Potassium supplement","Furosemide","Hydrochlorothiazide"],"key":[0,1,2,3,4],"rat":"The instructor's fluid and electrolyte lecture lists decreased kidney excretion as the most common cause of hyperkalemia and names drugs that raise potassium: ACE inhibitors (the 'prils'), ARBs (the 'sartans'), potassium-sparing diuretics such as spironolactone, NSAIDs, and potassium supplements. Loop and thiazide diuretics such as furosemide and hydrochlorothiazide cause hypokalemia.","why":{"5":"Furosemide is a cause of low potassium.","6":"Hydrochlorothiazide is a cause of low potassium."},"traps":{"5":"otherdx","6":"otherdx"},"cues":"CKD plus high potassium.","aid":"High-K crew: prils, sartans, spironolactone, NSAIDs, supplements. Low-K crew: furosemide, thiazides.","principle":"Sort drugs by their effect on the electrolyte in question.","takeaway":"In kidney disease, ACE inhibitors, ARBs, spironolactone, NSAIDs, and supplements can push potassium up.","safer":{"s":"Select contributing drugs.","a":"CKD, high potassium.","f":"Medication effects on electrolytes.","e":"Furosemide and HCTZ lower potassium.","r":"The five potassium-raising drugs."},"src":"Musculoskeletal_Complete_Project_Combined (2).pdf","pg":"pp. 500–545 (Fluid & Electrolyte lecture transcript, potassium)","sameFormExclude":[]},{"id":"XE23","ver":2,"tier":"expert","cog":"knowledge","subj":"Adult GI","topic":"Positioning with an esophageal balloon tube","cn":"Reduction of Risk Potential","cn2":"","fmt":"mc","diff":"I","ask":"Intervene","comp":"intervention","sameFormExclude":["G04"],"stem":"A client with a ruptured esophageal varix has a Sengstaken-Blakemore tube in place to control bleeding. How should the nurse position the client?","opts":["Flat in bed to reduce pressure on the varices","Head of bed elevated 30–45 degrees","Trendelenburg to support blood pressure","Prone to drain secretions from the mouth"],"key":1,"rat":"The varices slides list the Sengstaken-Blakemore tube with low intermittent wall suction and the head of the bed at 30–45 degrees as part of medical management of a ruptured varix, after airway patency and bleeding control.","why":{"0":"The slide specifies head of bed 30–45 degrees.","2":"Head-down positions are not part of this management.","3":"Prone positioning is not part of this management."},"traps":{"0":"otherdx","2":"otherdx","3":"otherdx"},"cues":"Balloon tube for variceal bleeding.","aid":"Balloon in, head up: 30 to 45.","principle":"Know the standard positioning for each device.","takeaway":"Sengstaken-Blakemore tube: HOB 30–45 degrees with low intermittent suction.","safer":{"s":"Choose the position.","a":"Sengstaken-Blakemore tube in place.","f":"Device care.","e":"Flat, Trendelenburg, and prone aren't listed.","r":"HOB 30–45 degrees."},"src":"GI_Linear_Chapter_Sourcebook.pdf","pg":"p122"},{"id":"XE24","ver":2,"tier":"expert","cog":"judgment","subj":"Adult GI","topic":"Hypovolemia after paracentesis","cn":"Reduction of Risk Potential","cn2":"Physiological Adaptation","fmt":"mc","diff":"A","ask":"Evaluate","comp":"evaluate","sameFormExclude":["XE09"],"exhibit":"<table><tr><th>Time</th><th>BP</th><th>HR</th><th>Report</th></tr><tr><td>Before procedure</td><td>124/78</td><td>88</td><td>Abdomen tense</td></tr><tr><td>30 min after</td><td>112/70</td><td>98</td><td>Breathing easier</td></tr><tr><td>60 min after</td><td>94/58</td><td>116</td><td>Dizzy when sitting up</td></tr></table>","stem":"A large volume of fluid was removed during a paracentesis. Review the trend. Which conclusion and action are most appropriate?","opts":["The procedure worked because breathing is easier; resume routine checks","The trend suggests hypovolemia; keep the client in bed and report it now","Dizziness is expected after paracentesis; encourage walking to recover","The puncture site needs a new dressing before anything else"],"key":1,"rat":"After paracentesis, the nurse monitors vital signs, keeps the client on bed rest, checks the puncture site, measures the fluid, weighs the client, and watches for signs of hypovolemia and shock. A falling blood pressure with a rising heart rate and dizziness signals hypovolemia.","why":{"0":"Easier breathing is one improved finding; the vital-sign trend shows a new problem.","2":"Walking a dizzy, hypotensive client risks falls; bed rest is maintained.","3":"Site checks are part of care, but the hemodynamic trend comes first."},"traps":{"0":"reassure","2":"unsafe","3":"lower"},"cues":"BP falling, HR rising, dizziness after large-volume removal.","aid":"After a big tap: watch the scissors (BP down, HR up).","principle":"A trend can show a new complication even while the original problem improves.","takeaway":"Better breathing after paracentesis doesn't cancel signs of hypovolemia.","safer":{"s":"Interpret the trend and act.","a":"BP 124/78 to 94/58, HR 88 to 116, dizzy.","f":"Evaluate outcomes; trend reading.","e":"Routine checks are falsely reassuring, walking is unsafe, the dressing is lower priority.","r":"Hypovolemia: bed rest and report now."},"compare":{"a":1,"b":0,"aWhy":"It recognizes the developing hypovolemia.","bWhy":"Easier breathing shows the paracentesis relieved the ascites.","decider":"The vital signs are moving toward shock even though breathing improved.","flip":"With stable BP and HR and no dizziness, easier breathing would show a successful outcome."},"src":"GI_Linear_Chapter_Sourcebook.pdf","pg":"pp121, 135–136"},{"id":"XE25","ver":2,"tier":"expert","cog":"judgment","subj":"Renal/Urinary","topic":"Rising potassium in oliguric AKI","cn":"Physiological Adaptation","cn2":"Reduction of Risk Potential","fmt":"mc","diff":"A","ask":"Prioritize","comp":"priority","sameFormExclude":["R03","XE12"],"stem":"A client in the oliguric phase of acute kidney injury has a potassium level that has risen above the reference range since yesterday. The client reports muscle weakness and feels \"fluttering\" in the chest. What should the nurse do first?","opts":["Encourage potassium-rich foods to replace losses","Place the client on cardiac (EKG) monitoring and report the findings","Encourage the client to walk to reduce muscle weakness","Plan teaching about a low-potassium diet before discharge"],"key":1,"rat":"In the oliguric phase, output falls, BUN and creatinine rise, and hyperkalemia may develop. The instructor's lecture is explicit that with potassium problems the heart is the priority: think EKG and place the client on a monitor. Diet teaching on low-potassium intake is part of later care.","why":{"0":"Potassium-rich foods raise potassium further.","2":"Activity does not treat hyperkalemia and the client has cardiac symptoms.","3":"Teaching comes after the cardiac risk is addressed."},"traps":{"0":"unsafe","2":"irrelevant","3":"later"},"cues":"Oliguria, rising potassium, palpitations, weakness.","aid":"Potassium problem? Think EKG.","principle":"Electrolyte emergencies are prioritized by the organ they threaten.","takeaway":"High potassium threatens the heart; monitor rhythm and report first.","safer":{"s":"Choose the first action.","a":"Oliguric AKI, rising potassium, weakness, palpitations.","f":"Priority: cardiac risk from hyperkalemia.","e":"Potassium foods are unsafe, walking is irrelevant, teaching is later.","r":"Cardiac monitoring and reporting come first."},"src":"Urinary_System_Chapters_1-6.pdf","pg":"pp84–85; Musculoskeletal_Complete_Project_Combined (2).pdf pp. 500–545 (F&E transcript)"}],"cases":[{"case":"CASE1","title":"Heart failure on digoxin and a diuretic","subj":"Cardiovascular","src":"Cardiovascular_Complete_Combined_Updated.pdf","pg":"Ch. 48: Box 48.4, Box 48.5, Table 48.1, digoxin toxicity and HF teaching (no page numbers in file)","record":"<table><tr><th>Field</th><th>Entry</th></tr><tr><td>Client</td><td>E.H., 72 years, admitted from the clinic</td></tr><tr><td>History</td><td>Heart failure; takes digoxin daily and furosemide daily</td></tr><tr><td>Nurse's note 0900</td><td>Increasing shortness of breath for 3 days; now sleeps sitting in a recliner. Crackles at both lung bases. 2+ edema of both ankles. Weight up 2 kg (4.4 lb) since last week. Reports nausea and poor appetite; says her vision \"looks hazy.\" Alert and oriented ×3. Lives with her daughter.</td></tr><tr><td>Vital signs</td><td>T 98.4°F, apical pulse 56 irregular, RR 24, BP 134/82, SpO₂ 92% on room air</td></tr><tr><td>Labs</td><td>Potassium 3.1 mEq/L (low)</td></tr></table>","items":[{"id":"C1-1","cj":"Recognize cues","fmt":"sata","cn":"Physiological Adaptation","cn2":"Pharmacological Therapies","diff":"I","stem":"Select all findings that require follow-up.","opts":["Sleeps sitting in a recliner","Crackles at both lung bases","Weight up 2 kg since last week","Nausea and hazy vision","Apical pulse 56","Potassium 3.1 mEq/L","Alert and oriented ×3","Lives with her daughter","Temperature 98.4°F"],"key":[0,1,2,3,4,5],"rat":"Orthopnea and crackles are left-sided HF signs; weight gain signals fluid retention. Nausea, poor appetite, and visual disturbance are digoxin-toxicity signs, and the apical pulse is below 60. Hypokalemia potentiates digoxin and can lead to toxicity.","why":{"6":"Normal finding.","7":"Social context only.","8":"Normal temperature."},"aid":"Two stories in one chart: the heart is drowning (fluid) and the digoxin is building up (toxicity).","ask":"Interpret","comp":"cue","traps":{"6":"irrelevant","7":"irrelevant","8":"irrelevant"},"safer":{"s":"Select findings that need follow-up.","a":"Orthopnea, crackles, weight gain, nausea and hazy vision, pulse 56, potassium 3.1.","f":"Recognize cues: heart failure plus possible digoxin toxicity.","e":"Orientation, living situation, and normal temperature are background.","r":"Every fluid and toxicity cue needs follow-up."},"principle":"Separate signal from background before interpreting.","takeaway":"Two stories in one chart: fluid overload and digoxin toxicity.","xv":2},{"id":"C1-2","cj":"Analyze cues","fmt":"matrix","cn":"Physiological Adaptation","cn2":"","diff":"I","stem":"For each finding, select the problem it is most consistent with.","rows":["Crackles and orthopnea","2+ ankle edema","Nausea, hazy vision, pulse 56","Frothy, blood-tinged sputum (if it develops)","Distended jugular veins (if present)"],"cols":["Left ventricular failure","Right ventricular failure","Digoxin toxicity"],"key":[0,1,2,0,1],"rat":"Box 48.4: left ventricular failure causes dyspnea, orthopnea, cough, frothy blood-tinged sputum, and crackles; right ventricular failure causes distended jugular veins and edema of the feet and ankles. GI upset, visual disturbances, and bradycardia point to digoxin toxicity.","why":{},"aid":"Left = Lungs. Right = the Rest of the body (legs, liver, neck veins).","ask":"Interpret","comp":"analysis","safer":{"s":"Match each finding to its problem.","a":"Crackles, orthopnea, edema, GI and visual changes, bradycardia, sputum, JVD.","f":"Analyze cues: left versus right failure versus toxicity.","e":"One answer per row.","r":"Lungs = left; periphery and JVD = right; GI, vision, slow pulse = digoxin."},"principle":"Left failure backs up into the lungs; right failure backs up into the body.","takeaway":"Left = lungs, right = rest of the body.","xv":2},{"id":"C1-3","cj":"Prioritize hypotheses","fmt":"mc","cn":"Pharmacological Therapies","cn2":"Reduction of Risk Potential","diff":"A","stem":"The next digoxin dose is due at 1000. Which finding is most important for the nurse to report before that dose?","opts":["Apical pulse 56 and potassium 3.1 mEq/L","2+ edema of both ankles this morning","Weight up 2 kg since last week","Has been sleeping sitting in a recliner"],"key":0,"rat":"Digoxin is withheld and the provider called when the apical pulse is below 60, and hypokalemia potentiates digoxin, increasing the risk of toxicity and dysrhythmias. Replacing potassium before giving digoxin is accepted practice.","why":{"1":"Important for HF, but not the digoxin-safety question.","2":"Reflects fluid retention, not digoxin safety.","3":"Reflects orthopnea, not digoxin safety."},"aid":"Before digoxin: count the pulse, check the potassium. Under 60 or low K? Hold and call.","ask":"Prioritize","comp":"med","traps":{"1":"lower","2":"lower","3":"lower"},"safer":{"s":"Choose the finding to report before the digoxin dose.","a":"Pulse 56, potassium 3.1, edema, weight gain, orthopnea.","f":"Medication safety: CHECK before GIVE.","e":"Edema, weight, and orthopnea matter for HF but not for this dose decision.","r":"Pulse below 60 with low potassium decides whether digoxin is safe."},"principle":"Before a drug, report the data that determines whether it is safe to give.","takeaway":"Digoxin: pulse under 60 or low potassium means hold and call.","med":{"why":"Heart failure.","check":"Apical pulse (hold below 60) and potassium.","give":"Only after checks are acceptable.","watch":"Nausea, visual changes, bradycardia, dysrhythmias.","teach":"Report vision changes and nausea; eat potassium-rich foods with a non-potassium-sparing diuretic."},"xv":2},{"id":"C1-4","cj":"Generate solutions","fmt":"sata","cn":"Pharmacological Therapies","cn2":"Basic Care and Comfort","diff":"I","stem":"Which actions should the nurse include in the plan of care? Select all that apply.","opts":["Withhold digoxin and notify the provider of the pulse and potassium.","Place the client in semi-Fowler's or high Fowler's position.","Give oxygen by nasal cannula per protocol.","Weigh the client daily and record intake and output.","Encourage salty soups to improve appetite.","Restrict high-potassium foods such as bananas and oranges.","Give digoxin with juice to reduce nausea."],"key":[0,1,2,3],"rat":"Box 48.5 lists oxygen by nasal cannula per protocol, semi- or high Fowler's position, daily weights and I&O, sodium restriction, and assessing for hypokalemia with diuretics. Teaching encourages high-potassium foods unless the client takes a potassium-sparing diuretic.","why":{"4":"Sodium is restricted in HF.","5":"Potassium-rich foods are encouraged with non-potassium-sparing diuretics.","6":"Digoxin is held with toxicity signs and a pulse below 60."},"aid":"HF care: Sit up, Oxygen on, Weigh daily, Watch salt, Watch potassium.","ask":"Intervene","comp":"intervention","traps":{"4":"unsafe","5":"otherdx","6":"unsafe"},"safer":{"s":"Select plan-of-care actions.","a":"HF with fluid overload, pulse 56, potassium 3.1.","f":"Generate solutions for both problems.","e":"Salty soup, restricting potassium, and giving digoxin are harmful.","r":"Hold digoxin and notify, upright position, oxygen, daily weights and I&O."},"principle":"Choose actions that address each identified problem.","takeaway":"Hold the drug, support breathing, track fluid.","xv":2},{"id":"C1-5","cj":"Take action","fmt":"mc","cn":"Physiological Adaptation","cn2":"Coordinated Care","diff":"A","stem":"At 1400 the client becomes more short of breath and coughs up frothy, pink-tinged sputum. RR is 30 and SpO₂ is 86%. What should the nurse do first?","opts":["Raise the head of the bed, apply oxygen per protocol, and notify the provider","Encourage oral fluids to help thin and loosen the secretions","Weigh the client to compare with the admission weight","Give the evening furosemide dose early on the nurse's own judgment"],"key":0,"rat":"Frothy, blood-tinged sputum with worsening dyspnea and hypoxemia signals severe left ventricular failure with pulmonary congestion. Positioning upright and oxygen per protocol support breathing while the provider is notified.","why":{"1":"Extra fluid worsens congestion.","2":"Weighing delays urgent care.","3":"Changing medication timing requires an order."},"aid":"Pink froth = lungs flooding: sit up, oxygen on, call now.","ehrAdd":{"notes":[{"t":"1400","text":"More short of breath. Coughing up frothy, pink-tinged sputum."}],"vitals":{"time":"1400","vals":{"Respirations":"30/min","SpO₂":"86% room air"}}},"ask":"Intervene","comp":"intervention","traps":{"1":"unsafe","2":"delay","3":"scope"},"safer":{"s":"Choose the first action.","a":"Frothy pink sputum, RR 30, SpO₂ 86%.","f":"Take action: acute pulmonary congestion.","e":"Fluids worsen it, weighing delays, early furosemide needs an order.","r":"Upright, oxygen, and immediate notification."},"principle":"Stabilize breathing first, then escalate.","takeaway":"Frothy pink sputum: sit up, oxygen on, call now.","xv":2},{"id":"C1-6","cj":"Evaluate outcomes","fmt":"matrix","cn":"Physiological Adaptation","cn2":"Health Promotion and Maintenance","diff":"I","stem":"The next morning, for each finding, indicate whether it shows the plan is effective or ineffective.","rows":["Weight down 1.5 kg from admission","Crackles diminished at the bases","Apical pulse 68 after potassium replacement","Still unable to sleep lying down","Says, \"I'll have canned soup for lunch; it's easy on my stomach.\""],"cols":["Effective","Ineffective"],"key":[0,0,0,1,1],"rat":"Weight loss, fewer crackles, and a pulse above 60 show improvement. Persistent orthopnea means fluid congestion is not resolved, and choosing high-sodium canned soup shows the dietary teaching has not been understood.","why":{},"aid":"Better = lighter, quieter lungs, pulse over 60. Not yet = still can't lie flat, still eating salt.","ask":"Evaluate","comp":"evaluate","safer":{"s":"Classify each finding.","a":"Weight, crackles, pulse, orthopnea, canned soup.","f":"Evaluate outcomes against the original problems.","e":"Weight loss, fewer crackles, pulse above 60 show improvement.","r":"Ongoing orthopnea and choosing canned soup show the plan isn't complete."},"principle":"Evaluate each original problem, including teaching.","takeaway":"Choosing salty soup means the teaching didn't land.","xv":2}],"ehr":{"hx":[["Client","E.H., 72 years, admitted from the clinic"],["Chief complaint","Increasing shortness of breath for 3 days"],["History","Heart failure"],["Home medications","Digoxin daily; furosemide daily"],["Social","Lives with her daughter"]],"notes":[{"t":"0900","text":"Increasing shortness of breath for 3 days; now sleeps sitting in a recliner. Crackles at both lung bases. 2+ edema of both ankles. Weight up 2 kg (4.4 lb) since last week. Reports nausea and poor appetite; says her vision \"looks hazy.\" Alert and oriented ×3."}],"vitals":{"times":["0900"],"rows":[["Temperature",["98.4°F"]],["Apical pulse",["56, irregular"]],["Respirations",["24/min"]],["Blood pressure",["134/82"]],["SpO₂",["92% room air"]]]},"labs":[{"t":"0800","name":"Potassium","val":"3.1 mEq/L","ref":"Flagged low by the lab"}],"mar":[{"med":"Digoxin","dose":"As prescribed","route":"PO","sched":"Daily at 1000","status":"Due 1000"},{"med":"Furosemide","dose":"As prescribed","route":"PO","sched":"Daily","status":"Due"}]}},{"case":"CASE2","title":"Preeclampsia on magnesium sulfate","subj":"Maternal-Newborn","src":"Maternal_Health_Linear_Chapter_Sources_Updated.pdf","pg":"Box 26.9 (pp. 51–74); preeclampsia assessment and magnesium sulfate (Ch. 39, pp. 331–381); seizure care: Neuro combined pp. 29–199","record":"<table><tr><th>Field</th><th>Entry</th></tr><tr><td>Client</td><td>A.R., 30 years, G2 P1, 34 weeks' gestation</td></tr><tr><td>Prenatal BP</td><td>118/74 at 30 weeks</td></tr><tr><td>Nurse's note 1100</td><td>Severe headache since this morning, not relieved by acetaminophen. Sees \"spots\" in front of her eyes. Epigastric pain. Face and fingers swollen; rings no longer fit. Weight up 3 lb in 4 days. Fetal movement felt this morning. Ate breakfast at 0700.</td></tr><tr><td>Vital signs</td><td>BP 158/104, HR 92, RR 18, T 98.6°F; FHR 140 with accelerations</td></tr><tr><td>Urine</td><td>Protein positive</td></tr></table>","items":[{"id":"C2-1","cj":"Recognize cues","fmt":"sata","cn":"Reduction of Risk Potential","cn2":"Health Promotion and Maintenance","diff":"I","stem":"Select all findings that are danger signs requiring prompt attention.","opts":["Severe headache not relieved by acetaminophen","Seeing spots","Epigastric pain","Swelling of face and fingers","Weight up 3 lb in 4 days","BP 158/104","Protein in urine","FHR 140 with accelerations","Ate breakfast at 0700"],"key":[0,1,2,3,4,5,6],"rat":"Box 26.9 danger signs include visual disturbances, severe or continuous headache, edema of the face or fingers, rapid weight gain, and severe epigastric pain. Preeclampsia involves elevated BP with proteinuria. FHR 140 with accelerations is reassuring.","why":{"7":"Reassuring fetal finding.","8":"Neutral information."},"aid":"Preeclampsia warning lights: Head (headache), Eyes (spots), Belly (epigastric pain), Hands/face (swelling), Scale (sudden gain).","ask":"Interpret","comp":"cue","traps":{"7":"irrelevant","8":"irrelevant"},"safer":{"s":"Select danger signs.","a":"Headache, spots, epigastric pain, facial swelling, weight gain, BP 158/104, proteinuria.","f":"Recognize cues: preeclampsia danger signs.","e":"Reassuring FHR and breakfast time are not danger signs.","r":"All seven maternal cues are danger signs."},"principle":"Separate maternal danger signs from reassuring fetal data.","takeaway":"Headache, vision, epigastric pain, swelling, weight, BP, protein.","xv":2},{"id":"C2-2","cj":"Analyze cues","fmt":"cloze","cn":"Physiological Adaptation","cn2":"","diff":"I","stem":"Complete the statement.","text":"The client's findings are most consistent with {0}, and the complication she is at greatest risk for is {1}.","blanks":[{"opts":["preeclampsia","normal third-trimester discomforts","a urinary tract infection","gestational diabetes"],"key":0},{"opts":["seizures (eclampsia)","preterm rupture of membranes","postpartum depression","hyperemesis gravidarum"],"key":0}],"rat":"Preeclampsia is elevated BP with proteinuria from a vasospastic process; eclampsia is convulsions accompanying preeclampsia. Assessment includes signs of seizure activity.","why":{},"aid":"Pre-eclampsia is the warning; eclampsia is the seizure.","ask":"Interpret","comp":"analysis","safer":{"s":"Complete the statement.","a":"Rising BP, proteinuria, neuro and epigastric symptoms.","f":"Analyze cues: name the condition and its threat.","e":"Discomforts, UTI, and diabetes don't explain the cluster.","r":"Preeclampsia, with eclamptic seizures as the threat."},"principle":"Read the completed sentence back for coherence.","takeaway":"Preeclampsia's big threat is a seizure.","xv":2},{"id":"C2-3","cj":"Prioritize hypotheses","fmt":"mc","cn":"Safety and Infection Prevention and Control","cn2":"Physiological Adaptation","diff":"A","stem":"Magnesium sulfate is ordered. Which concern is the nurse's priority at this time?","opts":["Risk for injury from seizure activity","Knowledge deficit about infant feeding","Anxiety about the hospital admission","Disturbed sleep from fetal movement"],"key":0,"rat":"Magnesium sulfate is given for seizure prevention in preeclampsia and eclampsia. Seizure precautions and assessment for seizure activity are listed nursing actions; physiologic safety ranks above psychosocial and teaching needs.","why":{"1":"Teaching follows stabilization.","2":"Real, but lower priority than a physiologic threat.","3":"Not a priority concern."},"aid":"Safety before teaching; seizure risk tops the list.","ehrAdd":{"orders":["Magnesium sulfate IV as prescribed"]},"ask":"Prioritize","comp":"priority","traps":{"1":"lower","2":"lower","3":"irrelevant"},"safer":{"s":"Choose the priority concern.","a":"Preeclampsia, magnesium ordered.","f":"Prioritize hypotheses: physiologic safety.","e":"Teaching, anxiety, and sleep are lower.","r":"Seizure injury risk is the reason for magnesium."},"principle":"Physiologic threats outrank psychosocial and teaching needs.","takeaway":"Magnesium is there to prevent a seizure.","xv":2},{"id":"C2-4","cj":"Generate solutions","fmt":"sata","cn":"Pharmacological Therapies","cn2":"Safety and Infection Prevention and Control","diff":"I","stem":"Which actions belong in the plan while the client receives magnesium sulfate? Select all that apply.","opts":["Check the patellar reflex hourly.","Monitor intake and output; report urine output below 30 mL/hr.","Monitor respirations and level of consciousness.","Keep calcium gluconate available.","Maintain seizure precautions and monitor FHR.","Position the client flat on her back.","Encourage frequent ambulation in the hall."],"key":[0,1,2,3,4],"rat":"During magnesium therapy, the patellar reflex is checked hourly with a continuous infusion; I&O is monitored with output kept at 30 mL/hr or more; respirations, orientation, and vital signs are monitored; calcium gluconate, the antidote, is kept available; and seizure precautions and FHR monitoring continue.","why":{"5":"Supine positioning causes supine hypotensive syndrome and decreased fetal circulation; side-lying is recommended.","6":"The client is at seizure risk and on a continuous infusion."},"aid":"Magnesium watch: Reflexes, Respirations, Renal output, and the antidote within Reach.","ask":"Intervene","comp":"med","traps":{"5":"unsafe","6":"unsafe"},"safer":{"s":"Select actions during magnesium therapy.","a":"Continuous magnesium infusion.","f":"Medication safety: toxicity monitoring.","e":"Supine positioning and ambulation are unsafe.","r":"Reflexes, I&O, respirations and LOC, calcium gluconate available, seizure precautions."},"principle":"Monitoring is part of giving a high-alert drug.","takeaway":"Reflexes, respirations, renal output.","med":{"why":"Seizure prevention.","check":"Patellar reflex hourly, respirations, output 30 mL/hr or more.","give":"Continuous IV by pump.","watch":"Absent reflexes, low respirations, low output.","teach":"Report flushing or breathing changes."},"xv":2},{"id":"C2-5","cj":"Take action","fmt":"mc","cn":"Safety and Infection Prevention and Control","cn2":"Physiological Adaptation","diff":"A","stem":"Two hours later, the client's eyes roll back and she begins a generalized tonic-clonic seizure. What should the nurse do?","opts":["Insert a padded tongue blade to protect her teeth and tongue","Turn her to her side, protect her head, and time the seizure","Restrain her arms and legs to prevent injury during the seizure","Leave the room quickly to bring the emergency cart to the bedside"],"key":1,"rat":"During a seizure the nurse turns the client to the side, protects the head, does not restrain, places nothing in the mouth, times the seizure, and maintains the airway. Side-lying also improves placental circulation.","why":{"0":"Nothing is placed in the mouth.","2":"Restraints are not used.","3":"The nurse stays with the client and calls for help."},"aid":"Side, Safe, Stopwatch — and never leave.","ehrAdd":{"notes":[{"t":"1300","text":"Eyes roll back; generalized tonic-clonic seizure begins."}]},"ask":"Intervene","comp":"intervention","traps":{"0":"unsafe","2":"invasive","3":"unsafe"},"safer":{"s":"Choose seizure care.","a":"Generalized tonic-clonic seizure.","f":"Take action: seizure safety.","e":"Tongue blade, restraints, and leaving are unsafe.","r":"Side, protect head, time it, call for help."},"principle":"Protect, don't restrain; stay with the client.","takeaway":"Side-lying also helps placental circulation.","xv":2},{"id":"C2-6","cj":"Evaluate outcomes","fmt":"matrix","cn":"Physiological Adaptation","cn2":"Pharmacological Therapies","diff":"I","stem":"Six hours later, for each finding, indicate whether it shows the plan is effective or ineffective.","rows":["No further seizure activity","Patellar reflex 2+","Urine output 40 mL/hr","Respirations 16/min","Still seeing spots and severe headache"],"cols":["Effective","Ineffective"],"key":[0,0,0,0,1],"rat":"No further seizures, a present reflex, output above 30 mL/hr, and normal respirations show safe, effective therapy. Persistent visual disturbance and severe headache are ongoing danger signs that must be reported.","why":{},"aid":"Good = no seizures, reflexes present, kidneys working, breathing steady.","ask":"Evaluate","comp":"evaluate","safer":{"s":"Classify each finding.","a":"No seizures, reflex 2+, output 40 mL/hr, RR 16, still seeing spots with headache.","f":"Evaluate outcomes.","e":"Four findings show safe, effective therapy.","r":"Persistent spots and headache are ongoing danger signs."},"principle":"An unresolved danger sign means the plan isn't finished.","takeaway":"Still seeing spots = still in danger.","xv":2}],"ehr":{"hx":[["Client","A.R., 30 years, G2 P1, 34 weeks' gestation"],["Prenatal blood pressure","118/74 at 30 weeks"],["Chief complaint","Severe headache since this morning"]],"notes":[{"t":"1100","text":"Severe headache since this morning, not relieved by acetaminophen. Sees \"spots\" in front of her eyes. Epigastric pain. Face and fingers swollen; rings no longer fit. Weight up 3 lb in 4 days. Fetal movement felt this morning. Ate breakfast at 0700."}],"vitals":{"times":["1100"],"rows":[["Blood pressure",["158/104"]],["Heart rate",["92"]],["Respirations",["18/min"]],["Temperature",["98.6°F"]],["FHR",["140 with accelerations"]]]},"labs":[{"t":"1100","name":"Urine protein","val":"Positive","ref":""}]}},{"case":"CASE3","title":"Infant with dehydration","subj":"Pediatrics","src":"PEDS_Complete_Linear_Word_Sources_2026.pdf","pg":"pp. 211–258 (Table 32.1; diarrhea and gastroenteritis); Pediatric packet dehydration signs","record":"<table><tr><th>Field</th><th>Entry</th></tr><tr><td>Client</td><td>9-month-old girl, admitted from the emergency department</td></tr><tr><td>History</td><td>Diarrhea for 2 days and vomiting since last night. Breastfed.</td></tr><tr><td>Nurse's note 0800</td><td>Only 2 wet diapers in the past 24 hours. Anterior fontanel sunken. Mucous membranes dry; no tears when crying. Skin cool and gray with poor turgor. Lethargic, rouses to voice. Parent gave an over-the-counter antidiarrheal last night.</td></tr><tr><td>Vital signs</td><td>HR 168, weak; RR 48; BP low for age; T 99.5°F</td></tr></table>","items":[{"id":"C3-1","cj":"Recognize cues","fmt":"sata","cn":"Physiological Adaptation","cn2":"","diff":"I","stem":"Select all findings that indicate dehydration.","opts":["Sunken anterior fontanel","Dry mucous membranes, no tears","Cool, gray skin with poor turgor","Lethargy","Rapid, weak pulse","Only 2 wet diapers in 24 hours","Breastfed","Temperature 99.5°F"],"key":[0,1,2,3,4,5],"rat":"Table 32.1: cold, dry, gray skin with loss of turgor; dry mucous membranes; sunken eyes and fontanels; lethargy; rapid, weak pulse; low BP; rapid respirations. Fewer than 6 wet diapers a day is a dehydration sign.","why":{"6":"Feeding method, not a sign.","7":"Not listed as a dehydration sign."},"aid":"Dehydrated infant: Dry (mouth), Down (fontanel), Drowsy (lethargic), Diapers few.","ask":"Interpret","comp":"cue","traps":{"6":"irrelevant","7":"irrelevant"},"safer":{"s":"Select dehydration findings.","a":"Fontanel, membranes, skin, lethargy, pulse, diapers.","f":"Recognize cues: pediatric dehydration.","e":"Feeding method and temperature aren't listed signs.","r":"All six listed findings."},"principle":"Cluster urine output, membranes, fontanel, skin, and activity.","takeaway":"Fewer than 6 wet diapers a day is a dehydration sign.","xv":2},{"id":"C3-2","cj":"Analyze cues","fmt":"cloze","cn":"Physiological Adaptation","cn2":"","diff":"I","stem":"Complete the statement.","text":"The infant's findings are most consistent with {0} caused by {1}.","blanks":[{"opts":["dehydration","fluid overload","expected teething symptoms","hypoglycemia only"],"key":0},{"opts":["fluid losses from diarrhea and vomiting","too much breast milk","the low-grade temperature alone","reduced activity"],"key":0}],"rat":"Dehydration results from abnormal fluid losses, most commonly through the GI system. Infants have greater fluid turnover relative to size and adjust less promptly, so shock is common with severe depletion.","why":{},"aid":"Infants are small cups: a little loss empties them fast.","ask":"Interpret","comp":"analysis","safer":{"s":"Complete the statement.","a":"Dehydration cues after diarrhea and vomiting.","f":"Analyze cues.","e":"Overload, teething, and hypoglycemia don't fit.","r":"Dehydration from GI losses."},"principle":"Name the condition and its cause together.","takeaway":"Infants lose fluid fast and shock quickly.","xv":2},{"id":"C3-3","cj":"Prioritize hypotheses","fmt":"mc","cn":"Physiological Adaptation","cn2":"","diff":"I","stem":"Which patient problem is the nurse's priority?","opts":["Inadequate fluid volume related to GI losses","Compromised skin integrity related to frequent stools","Anxiousness related to an unfamiliar environment","Parental knowledge deficit about antidiarrheals"],"key":0,"rat":"With lethargy, a rapid weak pulse, and low BP, inadequate fluid volume threatens perfusion and can progress to shock. Skin care, anxiety, and teaching are also on the care plan but follow restoring circulation.","why":{"1":"Needed, but not life-threatening.","2":"Psychosocial needs follow physiologic stability.","3":"Teaching follows stabilization."},"aid":"Fill the tank before polishing the car.","ask":"Prioritize","comp":"priority","traps":{"1":"lower","2":"lower","3":"lower"},"safer":{"s":"Choose the priority problem.","a":"Lethargy, rapid weak pulse, low BP.","f":"Priority ladder: perfusion first.","e":"Skin, anxiety, and teaching follow.","r":"Inadequate fluid volume threatens perfusion."},"principle":"Circulation outranks skin, anxiety, and teaching.","takeaway":"Restore volume first.","xv":2},{"id":"C3-4","cj":"Generate solutions","fmt":"sata","cn":"Basic Care and Comfort","cn2":"Reduction of Risk Potential","diff":"I","stem":"Which actions should the nurse include in the plan? Select all that apply.","opts":["Keep strict intake and output, including weighing diapers.","Weigh the infant daily.","Assess vital signs, skin turgor, mucous membranes, fontanel, and mental status at least every 4 hours.","Change diapers often and apply protective ointment to irritated skin.","Teach the parent to keep giving the antidiarrheal.","Withhold all food and fluids for 24 hours.","Start the BRAT diet (bananas, rice, applesauce, toast)."],"key":[0,1,2,3],"rat":"Interventions include strict I&O with diaper weights, daily weights, assessment every 4 hours, frequent diaper changes with protective ointment, and handwashing. Parents are taught to avoid antidiarrheals. Withholding food and fluids for 24 hours and the BRAT diet are no longer recommended.","why":{"4":"Parents are instructed to avoid antidiarrheals.","5":"No longer recommended.","6":"No longer recommended."},"aid":"Measure everything that goes in and comes out; protect the skin; skip the old BRAT advice.","ask":"Intervene","comp":"intervention","traps":{"4":"unsafe","5":"otherdx","6":"otherdx"},"safer":{"s":"Select plan actions.","a":"Dehydrated infant.","f":"Generate solutions.","e":"Antidiarrheals, NPO 24 hours, and BRAT are no longer recommended.","r":"Strict I&O, daily weight, frequent assessment, skin care."},"principle":"Recognize outdated interventions.","takeaway":"Diaper weights tell you output in mL.","xv":2},{"id":"C3-5","cj":"Take action","fmt":"mc","cn":"Pharmacological Therapies","cn2":"Safety and Infection Prevention and Control","diff":"A","stem":"The provider orders IV dextrose 5% in saline with potassium chloride added. The infant has not voided in the past 8 hours. What should the nurse do?","opts":["Hang the solution as ordered to replace potassium lost in the stools","Hold the solution and clarify the potassium order with the provider","Add extra potassium to the bag to make up for ongoing stool losses","Give the potassium by mouth instead to avoid IV complications"],"key":1,"rat":"IV dextrose 5% in saline rehydrates the infant, but potassium is added only once kidney function has been verified. With no urine output for 8 hours, the potassium order must be clarified.","why":{"0":"Potassium without verified kidney function risks dangerous accumulation.","2":"Changing the dose is outside nursing scope and unsafe.","3":"Changing the route requires an order."},"aid":"No pee, no K.","ehrAdd":{"orders":["IV dextrose 5% in saline with potassium chloride added"],"notes":[{"t":"1000","text":"Has not voided in the past 8 hours."}]},"ask":"Clarify","comp":"med","traps":{"0":"unsafe","2":"scope","3":"scope"},"safer":{"s":"Choose the action for the potassium order.","a":"No void in 8 hours; potassium in the IV.","f":"Medication safety: CHECK kidney function first.","e":"Hanging it, adding more, and switching route are unsafe or out of scope.","r":"Clarify until kidney function is verified."},"principle":"Potassium needs working kidneys.","takeaway":"No pee, no potassium.","med":{"why":"Replace losses after rehydration.","check":"Urine output and kidney function first.","give":"Only after kidney function is verified.","watch":"Rising potassium if output stays low.","teach":"Report diaper counts to the nurse."},"xv":2},{"id":"C3-6","cj":"Evaluate outcomes","fmt":"num","cn":"Basic Care and Comfort","cn2":"Physiological Adaptation","diff":"I","stem":"After 12 hours of IV fluids, the infant's wet diaper weighs 62 g. A dry diaper of the same type weighs 24 g. How many mL of urine are in the diaper? Enter a whole number.","key":38,"unit":"mL","tol":0,"rat":"Subtract the dry diaper weight from the wet weight: 62 − 24 = 38 g. The volume in mL equals the weight in grams, so the output is 38 mL — evidence that perfusion to the kidneys is improving.","why":{},"aid":"Wet minus dry; grams equal mL.","ask":"Calculate","comp":"evaluate","safer":{"s":"Calculate urine output.","a":"Wet 62 g, dry 24 g.","f":"Diaper weight method.","e":"Units: 1 g = 1 mL.","r":"62 − 24 = 38 mL."},"principle":"Subtract the dry weight; grams equal mL.","takeaway":"Diaper math: wet minus dry.","xv":2,"traps":{}}],"ehr":{"hx":[["Client","9-month-old girl, admitted from the emergency department"],["History of present illness","Diarrhea for 2 days and vomiting since last night"],["Feeding","Breastfed"],["Home medications","Parent gave an over-the-counter antidiarrheal last night"]],"notes":[{"t":"0800","text":"Only 2 wet diapers in the past 24 hours. Anterior fontanel sunken. Mucous membranes dry; no tears when crying. Skin cool and gray with poor turgor. Lethargic, rouses to voice."}],"vitals":{"times":["0800"],"rows":[["Heart rate",["168, weak"]],["Respirations",["48/min"]],["Blood pressure",["Low for age"]],["Temperature",["99.5°F"]]]}}},{"case":"CASE4","title":"Bipolar disorder on lithium","subj":"Psychiatric","src":"Psychiatric_Nursing_Complete_Linear_Study_Packet.pdf","pg":"pp. 257–280 (lithium); Fundamentals review pp47, 67, 73","record":"<table><tr><th>Field</th><th>Entry</th></tr><tr><td>Client</td><td>R.M., 46 years, admitted from the clinic</td></tr><tr><td>History</td><td>Bipolar disorder; takes lithium twice daily for 2 years</td></tr><tr><td>Nurse's note 1000</td><td>Reports nausea and 3–4 loose stools daily for 2 days. Started OTC ibuprofen 400 mg three times daily 10 days ago for knee pain. Works outdoors; heavy sweating during this week's heat wave. Says he \"cut way back on salt\" after his last blood pressure check. Hands \"shakier than usual\" since yesterday. Alert and oriented ×3. Lives with spouse. Last lithium dose 2000 last night.</td></tr><tr><td>Vital signs</td><td>T 98.6°F, HR 84, RR 16, BP 128/78, SpO₂ 97%</td></tr></table>","items":[{"id":"C4-1","cj":"Recognize cues","fmt":"sata","cn":"Pharmacological Therapies","cn2":"Psychosocial Integrity","diff":"I","stem":"Select all findings that require follow-up.","opts":["Nausea and 3–4 loose stools daily for 2 days","Ibuprofen three times daily for 10 days","Heavy sweating during a heat wave","\"Cut way back on salt\"","Hands shakier than usual since yesterday","Alert and oriented ×3","Lives with spouse","BP 128/78, HR 84, RR 16","Last lithium dose at 2000"],"key":[0,1,2,3,4],"rat":"NSAIDs such as ibuprofen raise lithium levels. Decreased sodium significantly enhances toxicity, and excessive sweating needs close observation. GI upset and tremor can be early toxicity signs. The other findings are normal or neutral.","why":{"5":"Normal.","6":"Neutral.","7":"Within normal ranges.","8":"Timing information for the blood draw."},"aid":"Lithium and salt sit on a seesaw: salt down, lithium up.","ask":"Interpret","comp":"cue","traps":{"5":"irrelevant","6":"irrelevant","7":"irrelevant","8":"irrelevant"},"safer":{"s":"Select findings needing follow-up.","a":"GI upset, NSAID use, sweating, salt restriction, new tremor.","f":"Recognize cues: lithium toxicity risk.","e":"Orientation, social, vitals, and dose timing are background.","r":"Each cue raises lithium or signals toxicity."},"principle":"Look for what raises a narrow-range drug's level.","takeaway":"NSAIDs, low sodium, sweating, and diarrhea push lithium up.","xv":2},{"id":"C4-2","cj":"Analyze cues","fmt":"cloze","cn":"Pharmacological Therapies","cn2":"","diff":"I","stem":"Complete the statement.","text":"The client's findings are most consistent with {0}. His use of {1} can raise the serum lithium concentration, and {2} increases the risk further.","blanks":[{"opts":["lithium toxicity","hypothyroidism","serotonin syndrome","expected, harmless adverse effects"],"key":0},{"opts":["ibuprofen","10 glasses of water a day","a twice-daily schedule"],"key":0},{"opts":["sodium loss from salt restriction, sweating, and diarrhea","a high-sodium diet","recent weight gain"],"key":0}],"rat":"NSAIDs increase serum lithium. Decreased sodium enhances toxicity, and high sodium lowers lithium levels. The source recommends 10–12 eight-ounce glasses of water daily.","why":{},"aid":"Ibuprofen and diuretics push lithium up; low salt pushes it up too.","ask":"Interpret","comp":"analysis","safer":{"s":"Complete the statement.","a":"Tremor, GI upset, ibuprofen, sodium losses.","f":"Analyze cues: mechanism.","e":"Hypothyroidism, serotonin syndrome, and harmless effects don't fit.","r":"Lithium toxicity from ibuprofen plus sodium loss."},"principle":"Link the cues to a mechanism.","takeaway":"Lithium and sodium move together.","xv":2},{"id":"C4-3","cj":"Prioritize hypotheses","fmt":"mc","cn":"Physiological Adaptation","cn2":"Pharmacological Therapies","diff":"A","stem":"Results from a blood draw at 0800, 12 hours after the last dose: lithium 1.9 mEq/L (reference 0.4–1.2), sodium 131 mEq/L, BUN and creatinine higher than last month. Which concern is the nurse's priority?","opts":["Serious lithium toxicity with neurologic and renal injury","Knee pain that is now limiting his ability to work","Possible hypothyroidism from long-term lithium therapy","Knowledge deficit about bipolar disorder and its treatment"],"key":0,"rat":"The level is above the 0.4–1.2 mEq/L reference, drawn correctly 12 hours after the dose. Sodium 131 is below 135. Rising BUN and creatinine indicate nephrotoxicity to report. Hypothyroidism would show progressive fatigue and weight gain.","why":{"1":"Real, but not life-threatening.","2":"Not supported by current findings.","3":"Teaching follows stabilization."},"aid":"Level above the ceiling + low sodium + kidneys straining = toxicity first.","ehrAdd":{"labs":[{"t":"0800","name":"Lithium (12 h after last dose)","val":"1.9 mEq/L","ref":"0.4–1.2 mEq/L"},{"t":"0800","name":"Sodium","val":"131 mEq/L","ref":""},{"t":"0800","name":"BUN and creatinine","val":"Higher than last month","ref":""}]},"ask":"Prioritize","comp":"priority","traps":{"1":"lower","2":"otherdx","3":"lower"},"safer":{"s":"Choose the priority concern.","a":"Lithium 1.9, sodium 131, rising BUN and creatinine.","f":"Prioritize hypotheses.","e":"Knee pain, hypothyroidism, and teaching are lower or unsupported.","r":"Serious toxicity with neuro and renal injury."},"principle":"Most likely and most dangerous comes first.","takeaway":"A high level plus rising kidney labs is a toxicity emergency.","xv":2},{"id":"C4-4","cj":"Generate solutions","fmt":"sata","cn":"Pharmacological Therapies","cn2":"Coordinated Care","diff":"A","stem":"Which actions should the nurse include in the plan? Select all that apply.","opts":["Withhold the next lithium dose until the provider reconfirms it.","Report the lithium level and symptoms to the provider now.","Monitor intake and output.","Monitor for hyperreflexia, lethargy, and weakness.","Encourage him to keep restricting salt.","Give the next lithium dose with milk to ease the nausea.","Restrict oral fluids to slow the diarrhea."],"key":[0,1,2,3],"rat":"For signs of serious toxicity, report immediately and do not give the next dose until administration is reconfirmed. Monitor urine output for nephrotoxicity and watch for hyperreflexia, lethargy, and weakness.","why":{"4":"Low sodium increases toxicity.","5":"Food or milk is for routine GI upset, not suspected toxicity.","6":"Adequate fluids are needed."},"aid":"Hold, Call, Measure, Watch.","ask":"Intervene","comp":"med","traps":{"4":"unsafe","5":"otherdx","6":"unsafe"},"safer":{"s":"Select plan actions.","a":"Lithium 1.9 with symptoms.","f":"Generate solutions: medication safety.","e":"Continued salt restriction, giving with milk, and restricting fluids are harmful.","r":"Hold, report, I&O, neuro monitoring."},"principle":"Stop the drug, report, and monitor its targets.","takeaway":"Toxicity: hold the dose and call.","xv":2},{"id":"C4-5","cj":"Take action","fmt":"mc","cn":"Coordinated Care","cn2":"Pharmacological Therapies","diff":"A","stem":"At 1400 the client is lethargic and weak, his reflexes are hyperactive, and he has vomited twice. What should the nurse do first?","opts":["Encourage oral fluids and recheck neurologic status in 1 hour.","Report the findings immediately to the provider using SBAR.","Give the 2000 lithium dose early with milk to settle his stomach.","Apply ice to his knee and give ibuprofen for comfort."],"key":1,"rat":"Hyperreflexia, lethargy, weakness, and persistent vomiting signal impending serious toxicity, to be reported immediately. SBAR structures communication for situations needing immediate attention.","why":{"0":"Delays the report.","2":"Gives more of the drug causing the problem.","3":"Adds an NSAID that raises lithium."},"aid":"Serious toxicity signs = SBAR call, not a wait-and-see.","ehrAdd":{"notes":[{"t":"1400","text":"Lethargic and weak. Reflexes hyperactive. Vomited twice."}]},"ask":"Intervene","comp":"intervention","traps":{"0":"delay","2":"unsafe","3":"unsafe"},"safer":{"s":"Choose the first action.","a":"Lethargy, weakness, hyperreflexia, vomiting.","f":"Take action: escalation.","e":"Waiting, more lithium, and ibuprofen worsen it.","r":"Immediate SBAR report."},"principle":"Worsening toxicity needs immediate escalation.","takeaway":"Use SBAR to report fast and clearly.","xv":2},{"id":"C4-6","cj":"Evaluate outcomes","fmt":"matrix","cn":"Pharmacological Therapies","cn2":"Health Promotion and Maintenance","diff":"I","stem":"The next morning, for each finding, decide whether it shows the plan is effective or ineffective.","rows":["Lithium level 1.0 mEq/L","No vomiting or diarrhea for 12 hours","Patellar reflexes still hyperactive","Oriented to name, place, and time","Says \"I'll keep cutting out salt so my blood pressure stays down\""],"cols":["Effective","Ineffective"],"key":[0,0,1,0,1],"rat":"A level of 1.0 is back within range, GI losses have stopped, and orientation is intact. Persistent hyperreflexia is still a toxicity sign, and cutting out salt shows the sodium teaching has not landed.","why":{},"aid":"Better = level in range, gut quiet, mind clear. Not yet = jumpy reflexes, still skipping salt.","ask":"Evaluate","comp":"evaluate","safer":{"s":"Classify each finding.","a":"Level 1.0, no GI losses, reflexes still hyperactive, oriented, plans to keep cutting salt.","f":"Evaluate outcomes.","e":"Level, GI, and orientation improved.","r":"Hyperreflexia and the salt plan mean not finished."},"principle":"Evaluate both physiology and understanding.","takeaway":"Cutting salt on lithium is unsafe.","xv":2}],"ehr":{"hx":[["Client","R.M., 46 years, admitted from the clinic"],["History","Bipolar disorder; takes lithium twice daily for 2 years"],["Recent changes","OTC ibuprofen 400 mg three times daily for 10 days for knee pain; cut back on salt after last BP check"],["Social","Works outdoors; lives with spouse"]],"notes":[{"t":"1000","text":"Reports nausea and 3–4 loose stools daily for 2 days. Heavy sweating during this week's heat wave. Hands \"shakier than usual\" since yesterday. Alert and oriented ×3."}],"vitals":{"times":["1000"],"rows":[["Temperature",["98.6°F"]],["Heart rate",["84"]],["Respirations",["16/min"]],["Blood pressure",["128/78"]],["SpO₂",["97%"]]]},"mar":[{"med":"Lithium","dose":"As prescribed","route":"PO","sched":"Twice daily","status":"Last given 2000 yesterday"}]}},{"case":"CASE5","title":"Postoperative opioid respiratory depression","subj":"Pain & Opioids","src":"Pain_Management_and_Urinary_System_Combined.pdf","pg":"pp3–5 (opiate agonists), pp15–17 (naloxone)","record":"<table><tr><th>Field</th><th>Entry</th></tr><tr><td>Client</td><td>T.L., 64 years, day 1 after open abdominal surgery</td></tr><tr><td>Medication</td><td>Morphine 10 mg IM every 4 hours PRN pain; hold for RR below 12. Given 0805.</td></tr><tr><td>1000</td><td>Drowsy, drifts back to sleep; RR 12 shallow; SpO₂ 93%</td></tr></table>","items":[{"id":"C5-1","cj":"Recognize cues","fmt":"highlight","cn":"Pharmacological Therapies","cn2":"Physiological Adaptation","diff":"I","ver":2,"ask":"Interpret","comp":"cue","stem":"Review the 1100 nurse's note. Click to highlight each finding that indicates opioid-related respiratory depression.","segs":[{"t":"1100: ","s":false},{"t":"Difficult to arouse; responds briefly to a firm shake.","s":true},{"t":" ","s":false},{"t":"Respirations 8/min and shallow.","s":true},{"t":" ","s":false},{"t":"SpO₂ 86%.","s":true},{"t":" ","s":false},{"t":"Abdominal dressing dry and intact.","s":true},{"t":" ","s":false},{"t":"Bowel sounds hypoactive.","s":true},{"t":" ","s":false},{"t":"Has not voided since 0600.","s":true}],"key":[1,3,5],"rat":"Opiate agonists make the respiratory centers less sensitive to carbon dioxide, causing respiratory depression, and sedation is a common neurologic effect. Falling respirations, decreasing arousability, and falling oxygen saturation form the pattern. Constipation and urinary retention are separate opioid adverse effects, and a dry dressing argues against bleeding.","why":{"7":"A dry, intact dressing is reassuring and argues against bleeding.","9":"Hypoactive bowel sounds relate to opioid constipation, a different adverse effect.","11":"Not voiding suggests possible urinary retention, a separate opioid effect to assess after breathing is addressed."},"traps":{"7":"irrelevant","9":"otherdx","11":"otherdx"},"aid":"Opioid overdose triad on the floor: sleepy, slow, low (LOC, RR, SpO₂).","principle":"Cluster the cues that belong to one problem; set aside real findings that belong to another.","takeaway":"An opioid patient can have three problems at once. Breathing cues outrank bowel and bladder cues.","safer":{"s":"Highlight cues of respiratory depression only.","a":"Difficult to arouse, RR 8 shallow, SpO₂ 86%; dressing dry; bowel sounds hypoactive; no void since 0600.","f":"Cue recognition: opioid adverse effects.","e":"Dressing, bowel sounds, and voiding belong to other questions.","r":"Arousability, respirations, and SpO₂ directly support respiratory depression."},"ehrAdd":{"notes":[{"t":"1100","text":"Difficult to arouse; responds briefly to a firm shake. Respirations 8/min and shallow. SpO₂ 86%. Abdominal dressing dry and intact. Bowel sounds hypoactive. Has not voided since 0600."}],"vitals":{"time":"1100","vals":{"Respirations":"8/min, shallow","SpO₂":"86%","Heart rate":"64","Blood pressure":"102/60","Level of consciousness":"Difficult to arouse","Pain":"Unable to rate"}}}},{"id":"C5-2","cj":"Analyze cues","fmt":"cloze","cn":"Pharmacological Therapies","cn2":"","diff":"I","ver":2,"ask":"Interpret","comp":"analysis","stem":"Complete the statement.","text":"The client's findings are most consistent with {0}, most likely caused by {1}.","blanks":[{"opts":["opiate-induced respiratory depression","expected postoperative fatigue","worsening incisional pain","urinary retention"],"key":0},{"opts":["the morphine given at 0805","a night without sleep","the surgical incision","limited fluid intake"],"key":0}],"rat":"Respirations fell from 18 to 12 to 8 with declining arousability and SpO₂ within hours of an IM morphine dose. Opiate agonists depress the respiratory center's response to carbon dioxide, and sedation is a common effect.","why":{},"traps":{},"aid":"Time it: dose at 0805, slowing by 1000, danger by 1100.","principle":"Link the trend to its most likely cause by timing.","takeaway":"When findings worsen on a timeline that starts with a drug dose, suspect the drug first.","safer":{"s":"Name the problem and its cause.","a":"RR 18 to 12 to 8, SpO₂ 97% to 86%, increasing sedation after IM morphine.","f":"Analyze cues: drug adverse effect.","e":"Fatigue, pain, and retention do not explain falling respirations and oxygenation.","r":"Opiate-induced respiratory depression from the 0805 dose fits every cue."}},{"id":"C5-3","cj":"Prioritize hypotheses","fmt":"mc","cn":"Physiological Adaptation","cn2":"Pharmacological Therapies","diff":"I","ver":2,"ask":"Prioritize","comp":"priority","stem":"Which problem is the nurse's priority at 1100?","opts":["Possible urinary retention related to opioid use","Risk for constipation related to opioids and immobility","Ineffective breathing from opioid respiratory depression","Acute pain related to the abdominal incision"],"key":2,"rat":"Respiratory depression threatens oxygenation now. Urinary retention and constipation are real opioid effects that need follow-up, and pain will need a new plan, but none outranks breathing.","why":{"0":"Not voiding needs assessment for bladder distention, but after breathing is addressed.","1":"Constipation is a later concern.","3":"Pain will need a new plan, but breathing comes first."},"traps":{"0":"lower","1":"lower","3":"lower"},"aid":"Breathing before bladder, bowel, or pain.","principle":"An actual threat to breathing outranks actual problems that are not immediately dangerous.","takeaway":"All four problems are real here; rank by immediate danger.","safer":{"s":"Choose the priority problem.","a":"RR 8, SpO₂ 86%, difficult to arouse; no void; hypoactive bowel sounds; pain.","f":"Priority ladder: immediate threat first.","e":"Retention, constipation, and pain are lower priority now.","r":"Breathing beats retention because it threatens life now."},"compare":{"a":2,"b":0,"aWhy":"Respiratory depression is threatening oxygenation now.","bWhy":"No void in 5 hours after surgery and opioids is a real concern.","decider":"RR 8 and SpO₂ 86% are an immediate threat; retention is not.","flip":"Once breathing is stable, assessing for bladder distention moves to the top."}},{"id":"C5-4","cj":"Generate solutions","fmt":"sata","cn":"Pharmacological Therapies","cn2":"Reduction of Risk Potential","diff":"A","ver":2,"ask":"Intervene","comp":"intervention","stem":"Which actions should the nurse anticipate? Select all that apply.","opts":["Have equipment to support respirations at the bedside","Notify the RN and provider of the respiratory changes","Prepare naloxone for the RN to give as ordered","Take vital signs at frequent intervals until the sedation resolves","Give the next morphine dose early at half strength","Let the client sleep with the lights dimmed","Check whether the client has a history of opioid dependence"],"key":[0,1,2,3,6],"rat":"Naloxone is the drug of choice for respiratory depression from excessive opiate doses. Before naloxone, the nurse assesses baseline neuro status, monitors vital signs at frequent intervals until CNS depression resolves, checks prior opioid use or dependence (naloxone can precipitate withdrawal), and keeps supportive equipment ready to maintain respirations. The prescription requires holding morphine for respirations below 12/min.","why":{"4":"More opioid worsens respiratory depression, and changing the dose without an order is outside nursing scope.","5":"Letting the client sleep leaves respiratory depression untreated."},"traps":{"4":"unsafe","5":"delay"},"aid":"Naloxone ready: equipment, frequent vitals, dependence check, call it in.","principle":"Generate solutions that treat the specific problem and prepare for its complications.","takeaway":"The dependence check matters because naloxone can trigger withdrawal.","safer":{"s":"Select every anticipated action.","a":"Opioid respiratory depression, naloxone ordered for the RN to give.","f":"Generate solutions: reversal and support.","e":"More morphine and letting the client sleep worsen the problem.","r":"Equipment, notification, naloxone preparation, frequent vitals, and the dependence check all address it."}},{"id":"C5-5","cj":"Take action","fmt":"mc","cn":"Pharmacological Therapies","cn2":"Reduction of Risk Potential","diff":"A","ver":2,"ask":"Intervene","comp":"med","stem":"At 1115 the RN has given naloxone. The client is awake with respirations 16/min, reports nausea and sweating, and rates incision pain 7/10. Which action by the nurse is most important now?","opts":["Give the PRN morphine now because pain is 7/10 and breathing has recovered","Continue vital signs at frequent intervals because naloxone's effect is short","Return to vital signs every 4 hours now that respirations are normal","Tell the client that nausea and sweating mean an allergy to naloxone"],"key":1,"rat":"Vital signs are taken at frequent intervals until CNS depression resolves and then at scheduled intervals because naloxone's duration of action is short. Early reversal of opiate effects can cause nausea, vomiting, sweating, and tachycardia, and naloxone after surgery can reverse analgesia. Poor pain control is reported so the provider can modify orders.","why":{"0":"Repeating the opioid that caused respiratory depression, without new orders, risks recurrence.","2":"Respirations look normal now, but naloxone's short action means depression can return.","3":"Nausea and sweating are expected with early reversal, not an allergy."},"traps":{"0":"unsafe","2":"reassure","3":"otherdx"},"aid":"Naloxone is a short bridge; the opioid can outlast it.","principle":"After a reversal agent, evaluate against the duration of the reversal, not the first good reading.","takeaway":"Recovery right after naloxone is not the finish line. Keep watching until the opioid has worn off.","safer":{"s":"Choose the most important action after reversal.","a":"Awake, RR 16 after naloxone, nausea, sweating, pain 7/10.","f":"Take action: monitoring after reversal.","e":"More morphine is unsafe, every-4-hour checks are too infrequent, the allergy claim is wrong.","r":"Frequent vital signs beat giving morphine because respiratory depression can return."},"compare":{"a":1,"b":0,"aWhy":"Naloxone's short duration means respiratory depression can return.","bWhy":"Pain of 7/10 after surgery needs treatment.","decider":"The opioid caused the emergency; pain is reported for new orders, not treated with the same dose.","flip":"Once the provider writes a new pain plan, carrying it out with close monitoring becomes the priority."},"ehrAdd":{"notes":[{"t":"1115","text":"RN gave naloxone IV per order. Client now awake. Respirations 16/min. Reports nausea, sweating, and incision pain 7/10."}],"vitals":{"time":"1115","vals":{"Respirations":"16/min","SpO₂":"94%","Heart rate":"96","Blood pressure":"132/80","Level of consciousness":"Awake, oriented","Pain":"7/10"}}}},{"id":"C5-6","cj":"Evaluate outcomes","fmt":"matrix","cn":"Reduction of Risk Potential","cn2":"Pharmacological Therapies","diff":"I","ver":2,"ask":"Evaluate","comp":"evaluate","stem":"At 1300, for each finding, indicate whether it shows the respiratory plan was effective or needs follow-up.","rows":["Respirations 16/min and regular","Oriented to name, place, and time","SpO₂ 95% on room air","Has not voided in 7 hours; lower abdomen feels full","Incision pain 7/10 since naloxone was given"],"cols":["Effective","Needs follow-up"],"key":[0,0,0,1,1],"rat":"Reversal of respiratory depression is the expected outcome of naloxone. Opioids can cause urinary retention through bladder and ureter spasm; the nurse assesses for bladder distention and reports it. Naloxone can reverse analgesia, and poor pain control is reported for a modified plan.","why":{"3":"Possible urinary retention needs assessment for distention and reporting.","4":"Unrelieved pain is reported so orders can be modified."},"traps":{},"aid":"Evaluate the original problem, then sweep for the side effects.","principle":"Evaluate whether the specific problem resolved, and catch new problems created by treatment.","takeaway":"A fixed breathing problem can leave pain and urinary retention behind.","safer":{"s":"Classify each finding.","a":"RR, orientation, SpO₂, voiding, pain.","f":"Evaluate outcomes.","e":"Breathing-related findings show success.","r":"Retention and pain need follow-up."}}],"ehr":{"hx":[["Client","T.L., 64 years"],["Surgery","Open abdominal surgery yesterday"],["Allergies","No known drug allergies"],["History","No history of opioid use before surgery"]],"notes":[{"t":"0800","text":"Rates incision pain 8/10. Alert and oriented ×3. Abdominal dressing dry and intact."},{"t":"1000","text":"Drowsy; opens eyes to voice, then drifts back to sleep. Respirations shallow."}],"vitals":{"times":["0800","1000"],"rows":[["Respirations",["18/min","12/min, shallow"]],["SpO₂",["97%","93%"]],["Heart rate",["82","72"]],["Blood pressure",["128/76","116/70"]],["Level of consciousness",["Alert","Drowsy"]],["Pain",["8/10","Not rated"]]]},"mar":[{"med":"Morphine","dose":"10 mg","route":"IM","sched":"Every 4 hours PRN pain; hold for respirations below 12/min","status":"Given 0805"}],"orders":["Vital signs every 4 hours and PRN","Naloxone 0.1–0.2 mg IV every 2–3 minutes PRN opiate-induced respiratory depression (RN to administer)","Notify provider for respirations below 12/min"]}}],"bankVersion":2};